WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:08.208
Around the world,

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:12.045
a jigsaw puzzle of life and death lies in pieces.

00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:15.382
It is a story written in the blood of young people,

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:16.483
defending a country,

00:00:16.816 --> 00:00:17.484
a cause,

00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:18.385
an ideal.

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:22.555
Some lived to return safely home,

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:23.723
and some did not.

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:26.659
Others disappeared.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.098
On a brisk autumn morning in upstate New York,

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:37.270
the earth yields the victims of a savage world war waged more than 200 years ago.

00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:40.974
Half a world away,

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:45.578
dense jungle guards secrets of soldiers gone but not forgotten.

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:48.248
Today,

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:55.288
a new breed of forensic detectives pieces together their terrible final hours in ways never before possible.

00:00:56.689 --> 00:01:00.527
How these people lived and died is written in their bones.

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:04.030
After long years of lying silent,

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:06.666
they begin to tell these soldiers'

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:07.333
stories.

00:01:34.294 --> 00:01:35.562
As the ancients have said,

00:01:35.995 --> 00:01:41.067
the war is not over as long as the last slain soldier remains unburied.

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:48.741
Although the unknown soldiers that lie here are in fact buried,

00:01:49.209 --> 00:01:51.010
no families were ever notified,

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:53.880
no loved ones ever appeared by the grave.

00:01:55.415 --> 00:01:56.382
Who these people were,

00:01:56.816 --> 00:01:58.518
the pain and fear they suffered,

00:01:59.052 --> 00:01:59.953
how they died,

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:05.692
all these mysteries are sealed forever beneath impenetrable marble slabs.

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:13.199
Today,

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:15.001
thanks to modern science,

00:02:15.502 --> 00:02:19.606
no one in the United States military need ever again become an unknown.

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:24.644
During Operation Desert Storm in 1990,

00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:26.446
For the first time in history,

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:35.555
the identity of two servicemen was established by extracting DNA from their shattered remains and matching it against the DNA of living relatives,

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:37.957
a costly and difficult process.

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:41.794
Now,

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:44.797
to more easily trace the names of war casualties,

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:49.402
blood is taken at recruitment to predetermine each person's DNA pattern,

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:52.038
as unique as their fingerprints.

00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:56.843
DNA,

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:59.312
deoxyribonucleic acid,

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:00.914
is the blueprint of life.

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:04.450
The chemical essence of genes,

00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:09.155
it exists in all cells of the body and carries the information for eye color,

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:10.323
race,

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.125
height and other traits.

00:03:15.395 --> 00:03:15.762
Today,

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:24.504
the technology to decipher DNA is so precise that recruits go off to war with a kind of genetic dog tag on file with the government.

00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:34.380
Hundreds of samples a year arrive at the Armed Forces Institute of Pathology outside Washington,

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:34.681
D.C.

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:35.582
How are you?

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:39.586
Fine.

00:03:39.586 --> 00:03:40.253
The laboratory,

00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:42.555
which is the largest of its type in the world,

00:03:42.922 --> 00:03:45.325
processes and stores the genetic material.

00:03:49.929 --> 00:03:54.567
Pewter checks name and social security number and prints labels for each packet.

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:58.371
Maintained under tight security,

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:01.441
the data may not be released except by court order.

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:09.282
More than a million samples are already placed in deep freeze,

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:11.818
each representing a unique human being.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.856
Deputy Program Manager is James Canick.

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:23.429
For those who have ever visited Arlington or any military cemetery,

00:04:24.464 --> 00:04:29.068
probably one of the most moving things is to see a grave.

00:04:29.168 --> 00:04:30.770
that doesn't have a name on it.

00:04:31.104 --> 00:04:31.237
And,

00:04:31.237 --> 00:04:31.537
of course,

00:04:31.537 --> 00:04:33.072
the inscription reads,

00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:37.644
Here lies in honored glory an American service member known but to God.

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:44.350
And we hope that in our little operation that we have here that we will preclude that from ever happening again.

00:04:46.352 --> 00:04:52.825
The first major war in which Americans fought and died was more than a decade before the War of Independence.

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:56.963
The tranquil region now known as Lake George,

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:00.333
New York was the scene of some of history's bloodiest fighting.

00:05:04.771 --> 00:05:06.272
In 1754,

00:05:06.673 --> 00:05:10.677
France and England clashed in a mighty struggle for control of North America.

00:05:12.712 --> 00:05:20.653
The brutal French and Indian War raged for nine years and spread to every country where Britain and France had territorial interests.

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:26.492
It has been called the first true world war.

00:05:26.492 --> 00:05:31.931
By 1763,

00:05:32.332 --> 00:05:33.933
with the French thoroughly defeated,

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.402
the English ruled most of North America,

00:05:36.736 --> 00:05:38.438
including the 13 colonies.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:44.711
A strategic British outpost during the war was Fort William Henry.

00:05:47.046 --> 00:05:47.246
In the

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:50.516
1950s, the site was developed as a tourist attraction.

00:05:51.484 --> 00:05:55.254
Excavations of the old fort guided architects during reconstruction.

00:05:56.956 --> 00:05:57.557
While digging,

00:05:57.957 --> 00:06:01.160
scientists stumbled on an undisturbed burial ground.

00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:02.929
Today,

00:06:03.262 --> 00:06:09.936
a team of anthropologists and volunteers begin to piece together the story of how these soldiers lived and died.

00:06:10.503 --> 00:06:15.041
What they unearthed is one of the most gruesome stories in the annals of war.

00:06:15.975 --> 00:06:22.315
Forensic anthropologist Mariah Liston first began working with the Fort William Henry burials in 1993.

00:06:23.983 --> 00:06:24.650
Her colleague,

00:06:24.984 --> 00:06:25.685
Brenda Baker,

00:06:26.052 --> 00:06:28.354
has worked with her since the beginning of the project.

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:36.129
Liston and Baker know from patterns in the earth that they are guaranteed to find burials here.

00:06:37.597 --> 00:06:40.767
This is the outline of one of the rectangular grave shafts.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:44.504
It extends from that corner to the head would be right here.

00:06:45.304 --> 00:06:51.277
We can tell it's a grave shaft because the disturbed soil from digging this hole has been thrown back in,

00:06:51.377 --> 00:06:57.183
and we get this mottled coloration that contrasts very strongly with the undisturbed sand in this area.

00:06:59.152 --> 00:07:00.119
Over the course of 10 years,

00:07:00.119 --> 00:07:00.653
After ten days,

00:07:00.953 --> 00:07:06.759
mounting excitement is tempered by the patience and precision for which every anthropologist is trained.

00:07:08.361 --> 00:07:11.264
As they carefully remove the earth within the grave shafts,

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:14.033
the top of a skull finally emerges.

00:07:17.003 --> 00:07:32.485
I think this is really one of the most exciting parts of this because this is the first time this person has been seen since he was laid to rest and you get an idea as you uncover his face of something about what he looked like and who he was.

00:07:36.589 --> 00:07:39.459
Slowly the extent of the find becomes evident.

00:07:42.395 --> 00:07:44.997
Remarkably the skeleton seems intact.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:49.402
When we first discover something new,

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:52.605
some specific bit of information we haven't had before,

00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:01.914
it's exciting to have another piece of the picture and to know that we're filling out the puzzle a little bit more with each of these discoveries.

00:08:02.515 --> 00:08:05.351
I often wonder what happened to their families.

00:08:05.451 --> 00:08:07.987
Did the families ever learn that these men had died,

00:08:07.987 --> 00:08:09.155
or what they'd died from,

00:08:10.723 --> 00:08:11.924
and who they left behind?

00:08:12.425 --> 00:08:15.795
Many of them probably had wives and children in other places.

00:08:17.096 --> 00:08:18.164
They're all pretty young.

00:08:18.364 --> 00:08:20.666
They're as young as about 14 or 15,

00:08:20.833 --> 00:08:21.667
up to early

00:08:22.068 --> 00:08:23.269
20s, most of them.

00:08:23.269 --> 00:08:25.471
There are a few that are a little bit older,

00:08:25.538 --> 00:08:27.540
but they're late teens and early

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:28.040
20s.

00:08:32.712 --> 00:08:36.082
Before the skeleton is removed for preservation and study,

00:08:36.616 --> 00:08:39.285
Liston carefully documents its exact position.

00:08:41.354 --> 00:08:41.954
Even now,

00:08:42.288 --> 00:08:47.360
she begins to recognize the telltale signs of a vicious war etched in these bones.

00:08:49.762 --> 00:08:54.267
It looks like this is one of the soldiers that has been doing a lot of really heavy labor.

00:08:54.333 --> 00:08:56.569
There's a lot of wear and tear on all of his joints.

00:08:58.671 --> 00:09:04.977
The muscles in his back had torn from the soldier's bones under the constant stress of carrying heavy loads,

00:09:05.478 --> 00:09:07.813
and his ribs became fused to the vertebrae.

00:09:09.415 --> 00:09:11.217
He must have been in constant pain.

00:09:17.123 --> 00:09:18.824
If left exposed to the elements,

00:09:19.025 --> 00:09:21.027
the bones would deteriorate over time.

00:09:21.894 --> 00:09:23.129
Working well into the night,

00:09:23.429 --> 00:09:27.400
the team removes and labels each bone fragment to take to the lab.

00:09:29.869 --> 00:09:32.371
I'm suspicious about this break in the back of the skull.

00:09:32.471 --> 00:09:35.741
It looks like something that may have happened while the bone was still fresh.

00:09:35.908 --> 00:09:37.143
It could easily be what killed it.

00:09:37.143 --> 00:09:45.017
His face is going to come off.

00:09:47.053 --> 00:09:49.355
As the fragile skull is pulled from the earth,

00:09:49.589 --> 00:09:50.656
the bones crumble.

00:09:51.357 --> 00:09:54.093
The skull apparently was crushed by the weight of the soil.

00:09:54.594 --> 00:09:55.361
What a shame.

00:09:57.029 --> 00:10:01.133
There's this bad fracture here on the bottom too that comes across and gets,

00:10:01.734 --> 00:10:02.101
you know,

00:10:02.134 --> 00:10:02.902
that as well.

00:10:03.102 --> 00:10:07.773
We may be able to see something a little bit more clearly when we get that all cleaned up.

00:10:10.943 --> 00:10:13.179
Two small buttons are found with the skeleton.

00:10:13.879 --> 00:10:15.081
They appear to be military,

00:10:15.448 --> 00:10:17.984
but the team can't be sure until they have been removed.

00:10:19.151 --> 00:10:21.554
One was found in the pelvic cavity of the skeleton,

00:10:21.887 --> 00:10:24.857
and the other seems to be attached to an unidentified material.

00:10:26.492 --> 00:10:30.696
And it looks like it's attached to either leather or it may very well be part of his skin.

00:10:30.863 --> 00:10:33.966
Copper often preserves organic tissue very well.

00:10:35.534 --> 00:10:39.005
American militia fought alongside the British at Fort William Henry.

00:10:40.239 --> 00:10:44.510
Items like buttons and fabric may provide clues to rank and nationality.

00:10:47.913 --> 00:10:48.948
Painstaking work,

00:10:49.448 --> 00:10:49.815
cleaning,

00:10:50.182 --> 00:10:55.221
sorting and documenting the remains will take more than a dozen people weeks to complete.

00:10:58.858 --> 00:11:00.226
This is probably where this other,

00:11:00.726 --> 00:11:01.794
this other button,

00:11:01.794 --> 00:11:02.528
these two were down,

00:11:02.662 --> 00:11:06.666
they dropped down into the pelvic cavity and this one was right around the hip socket.

00:11:08.067 --> 00:11:10.736
Some artifacts found with the bodies can be identified.

00:11:11.671 --> 00:11:12.972
Others remain a mystery.

00:11:16.542 --> 00:11:20.713
many of the pieces in this detective story finally begin to take form.

00:11:21.847 --> 00:11:23.883
Far more than a pile of bones,

00:11:24.183 --> 00:11:29.522
a young soldier has emerged from the depths with a story he waited centuries to tell.

00:11:33.426 --> 00:11:34.927
Two weeks into the excavation,

00:11:35.227 --> 00:11:36.295
as winter closes in,

00:11:36.862 --> 00:11:38.864
one complete burial has been unearthed,

00:11:39.231 --> 00:11:41.500
and several others are still being excavated.

00:11:42.802 --> 00:11:46.105
The team's task now is to try to read the life history

00:11:46.372 --> 00:11:48.074
that is written in the bones.

00:11:50.776 --> 00:11:56.716
The first thing that we can do is take a look at the skull and some of the features on the skull that indicate he's a male.

00:11:57.216 --> 00:12:00.453
If you look at the area over his eye orbits,

00:12:00.486 --> 00:12:04.924
you see that it's fairly well developed and there are brow ridges essentially there.

00:12:04.924 --> 00:12:05.791
And that's a feature that

00:12:05.825 --> 00:12:06.692
that you find in men.

00:12:06.959 --> 00:12:08.994
Women's foreheads tend to be a little bit higher.

00:12:09.128 --> 00:12:10.596
This one slopes back a bit,

00:12:10.663 --> 00:12:12.665
so that's an indication that this is a man.

00:12:13.265 --> 00:12:14.166
This is his jaw,

00:12:14.467 --> 00:12:15.234
his lower jaw.

00:12:15.534 --> 00:12:17.670
And you can see it's very large and robust.

00:12:17.803 --> 00:12:19.939
And his chin is extremely square.

00:12:20.072 --> 00:12:21.841
And this is another feature of a male.

00:12:23.309 --> 00:12:23.676
Next,

00:12:23.909 --> 00:12:26.245
they must determine the person's age at death.

00:12:27.446 --> 00:12:31.183
And if you look at the ends of his bones in his arms and legs,

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:34.420
you can see that they're completely fused together.

00:12:34.854 --> 00:12:36.021
In younger individuals,

00:12:36.021 --> 00:12:38.591
you would see separate caps at the ends of the bones,

00:12:38.591 --> 00:12:40.426
and because those caps have fused on,

00:12:40.459 --> 00:12:43.295
we know that he's at least more than about 18 years old.

00:12:43.996 --> 00:12:47.233
We can see that his molars are all completely erupted here,

00:12:47.867 --> 00:12:49.235
including his wisdom tooth,

00:12:49.502 --> 00:12:52.605
and that usually erupts when you're about 18 to 21 years old.

00:12:52.772 --> 00:12:53.205
So again,

00:12:53.272 --> 00:12:56.142
another indicator that he's an adult male.

00:12:58.077 --> 00:12:59.545
Many other questions remain.

00:13:00.346 --> 00:13:03.149
What was life at Fort William Henry like for this man?

00:13:03.883 --> 00:13:05.317
What was the condition of his health?

00:13:06.418 --> 00:13:09.355
Most of the younger individuals had very severe arthritis.

00:13:09.688 --> 00:13:13.492
The only place where he has any significant arthritis is here in his right elbow.

00:13:14.326 --> 00:13:15.795
And this is the forearm.

00:13:16.362 --> 00:13:21.200
This is the bone where it hooks over your upper arm and allows you to move it back.

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:22.401
back and forth like that.

00:13:22.935 --> 00:13:26.305
And if you compare the right elbow to the left elbow,

00:13:26.772 --> 00:13:29.809
you can see how much rougher this surface is here on the right.

00:13:30.509 --> 00:13:34.146
So this means he had a lot more action in his right arm.

00:13:34.747 --> 00:13:36.182
And because of the arthritis showing

00:13:36.282 --> 00:13:37.516
up just in his right elbow,

00:13:37.583 --> 00:13:39.518
it suggests that he's a right-handed man.

00:13:40.419 --> 00:13:47.159
We have another clue that he was right-handed because he's got wear on some of his teeth on his lower jaw,

00:13:48.194 --> 00:13:52.698
and you can see it's beveled right on these cusp surfaces here.

00:13:53.132 --> 00:13:57.102
So he's probably clenching a pipe over on the left side of his jaw,

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:01.974
and that's what would have caused that kind of strange wear pattern on his lower teeth.

00:14:02.541 --> 00:14:05.077
And because he was clenching that on the left side of his jaw,

00:14:05.177 --> 00:14:05.978
he was probably right.

00:14:06.512 --> 00:14:10.850
You would put the pipe in the left side of your mouth and use your left hand to take the pipe in and out.

00:14:15.921 --> 00:14:18.724
The French and Indian War was fierce and long.

00:14:21.894 --> 00:14:22.595
Yet the soldiers'

00:14:22.661 --> 00:14:24.697
most constant enemy was not a bullet,

00:14:24.997 --> 00:14:25.898
but disease.

00:14:30.569 --> 00:14:31.136
Smallpox,

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:32.137
influenza,

00:14:32.271 --> 00:14:34.173
and tuberculosis ran rampant.

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:40.546
This group of skeletons is more sickly,

00:14:41.146 --> 00:14:43.382
in worse shape than anything I've ever worked with.

00:14:44.116 --> 00:14:45.451
I've looked at people as old as about...

00:14:45.551 --> 00:14:47.753
3,000 years old and modern,

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:50.022
more modern populations as well,

00:14:50.122 --> 00:14:52.191
and yet this is the most diseased group.

00:14:52.191 --> 00:14:53.525
They've got the most injuries.

00:14:54.627 --> 00:14:55.261
All the way around,

00:14:55.261 --> 00:14:57.162
there's more pathology here than anything.

00:14:59.598 --> 00:15:03.469
Although more soldiers died from disease than war-related injuries,

00:15:03.769 --> 00:15:07.006
the battle picture that emerges becomes increasingly grim.

00:15:12.478 --> 00:15:14.747
This soldier is an example of one that was shot.

00:15:15.281 --> 00:15:22.655
There's this depression that caused the bone to be crushed here and another one at the top of the bone here.

00:15:23.122 --> 00:15:25.624
You can actually see the rounded depression of the musket ball.

00:15:27.626 --> 00:15:30.796
A lot of the soldiers that we found died from blows to the head.

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:31.497
Here,

00:15:31.797 --> 00:15:34.266
something struck the soldier and punctured the bone,

00:15:34.366 --> 00:15:41.640
forcing fragments inside the skull and also fracturing it here down toward his eye and also running back toward the back of the skull.

00:15:44.243 --> 00:15:47.379
Diaries reveal that as the French attacked Fort William Henry,

00:15:47.746 --> 00:15:49.181
the British simply surrendered,

00:15:49.682 --> 00:15:51.717
too sick with smallpox to fight back.

00:15:53.385 --> 00:15:53.585
Then,

00:15:53.886 --> 00:15:54.620
without warning,

00:15:54.853 --> 00:15:59.358
a group of Indians allied to the French attacked and slaughtered the ill and injured men.

00:16:04.830 --> 00:16:08.100
The event was central to the novel The Last of the Mohicans,

00:16:08.467 --> 00:16:10.602
long thought to be an exaggerated account.

00:16:11.303 --> 00:16:11.904
If anything,

00:16:12.237 --> 00:16:12.972
it turns out,

00:16:13.405 --> 00:16:14.606
it was an understatement.

00:16:19.111 --> 00:16:19.745
these skeletons.

00:16:19.778 --> 00:16:22.581
We have found extensive evidence of this massacre,

00:16:23.115 --> 00:16:26.118
documenting the fact that atrocities were committed.

00:16:26.452 --> 00:16:30.856
We know that there were mutilations and massacres on both sides.

00:16:31.323 --> 00:16:32.157
As part of this war,

00:16:32.324 --> 00:16:33.926
everyone was participating in this.

00:16:35.961 --> 00:16:46.638
This soldier was one that was beheaded and we have the account of a French priest who saw the beginning of the massacre describing one of the native allies of the French coming out of the casemates,

00:16:46.972 --> 00:16:48.474
which were the hospitals at the fort,

00:16:49.074 --> 00:16:51.777
and carrying the head of his victim.

00:16:52.678 --> 00:16:54.680
Buried underneath the floor of the casemates,

00:16:54.847 --> 00:16:59.952
there was a mass grave of massacre victims and this individual was indeed beheaded.

00:17:00.386 --> 00:17:01.954
In the neck bone that we have here,

00:17:02.388 --> 00:17:03.789
there are a number of cuts.

00:17:04.623 --> 00:17:05.991
slicing across the bone,

00:17:06.058 --> 00:17:09.128
and a final one all the way through the bone here across the top.

00:17:09.628 --> 00:17:14.166
And so we're fairly certain that this is the individual that was beheaded that the priest saw.

00:17:15.968 --> 00:17:20.839
The bones found inside the fort tell the story of the quick and ferocious attack.

00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:22.875
As part of the massacre,

00:17:22.941 --> 00:17:26.478
part of the picture we've been able to reconstruct is that there were mutilations on the body,

00:17:26.578 --> 00:17:28.580
and this included genital mutilations.

00:17:28.614 --> 00:17:30.315
There are cuts on the front of the pelvis.

00:17:31.116 --> 00:17:32.584
in the area of the genitals.

00:17:33.585 --> 00:17:34.119
In addition,

00:17:34.153 --> 00:17:45.030
we think some of the individuals were disemboweled and there are cuts going all the way through the region of the stomach and abdomen into the backbone behind the abdomen.

00:17:45.564 --> 00:17:50.536
And we have here a number of cut marks and damage to the bone from that incident.

00:17:53.806 --> 00:17:54.139
Today,

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:59.211
forensic science reveals much about the hardships with which these young men lived,

00:17:59.845 --> 00:18:01.146
the horrors they suffered,

00:18:01.547 --> 00:18:04.116
and the savage ways in which many died.

00:18:05.751 --> 00:18:09.822
It can create a picture of a soldier who was over 18 and right-handed,

00:18:10.689 --> 00:18:11.857
a man who smoked a pipe,

00:18:12.191 --> 00:18:14.126
which he probably held in his left hand.

00:18:15.494 --> 00:18:19.098
A strong man who spent his days doing a lot of heavy lifting,

00:18:19.598 --> 00:18:21.667
his arms and legs free of arthritis,

00:18:21.967 --> 00:18:23.802
but his back in constant pain.

00:18:25.137 --> 00:18:29.808
His bones and those of his fallen comrades tell us much about what these soldiers'

00:18:29.875 --> 00:18:30.776
lives were like.

00:18:32.377 --> 00:18:36.248
But we can never know they're ever anonymous.

00:18:36.849 --> 00:18:38.350
They belong to the ages.

00:18:41.887 --> 00:18:43.288
In 1861,

00:18:43.622 --> 00:18:49.661
passions deeply felt over slavery brought the young American nation into a convulsive civil war.

00:18:51.530 --> 00:18:52.631
North against South,

00:18:53.098 --> 00:18:54.199
brother against brother,

00:18:54.633 --> 00:18:56.835
they fought for four bitter years.

00:18:57.703 --> 00:19:01.006
When the terrible trial by fire and sword came to an end,

00:19:01.507 --> 00:19:05.010
more than 600,000 boys and young men had died.

00:19:09.648 --> 00:19:12.351
The National Museum of Health and Medicine in Washington,

00:19:12.417 --> 00:19:12.651
D.C.

00:19:13.318 --> 00:19:18.357
was established in 1862 for record keeping and follow up care of the wounded.

00:19:19.358 --> 00:19:19.591
Here,

00:19:19.958 --> 00:19:24.329
Paul Sledzik has studied more than 2,000 bones of Civil War casualties.

00:19:29.868 --> 00:19:32.070
Although bone may appear rock solid,

00:19:32.604 --> 00:19:35.440
in actuality it changes over one's lifetime,

00:19:35.974 --> 00:19:37.709
even over the course of a few days,

00:19:38.076 --> 00:19:39.478
in response to exercise,

00:19:39.845 --> 00:19:40.579
wear and tear,

00:19:40.913 --> 00:19:41.680
and disease.

00:19:44.616 --> 00:19:46.618
Bone is like any other organ system in the body.

00:19:46.685 --> 00:19:50.789
It's constantly replenishing its cells and changing the cells within it.

00:19:51.323 --> 00:19:53.725
One of the changes that we can pick up in the bone that's interesting,

00:19:54.393 --> 00:19:58.530
because it gets at what activities the soldiers were doing,

00:19:58.931 --> 00:19:59.498
are these changes.

00:19:59.498 --> 00:20:00.832
is in the upper part of the humerus.

00:20:01.833 --> 00:20:04.670
This is the humerus or the upper arm bone from a Civil War soldier,

00:20:04.703 --> 00:20:05.938
and this defect here,

00:20:05.938 --> 00:20:07.472
this etched area in the bone,

00:20:08.106 --> 00:20:10.943
is indicative of heavy muscle activity of the upper arm,

00:20:11.009 --> 00:20:14.046
probably lifting 15 to 20 pound rifles and firing them,

00:20:14.613 --> 00:20:16.715
digging with picks and axes.

00:20:19.117 --> 00:20:21.853
The bones also tell of the savagery of battle.

00:20:23.989 --> 00:20:25.624
Although the wire was added later,

00:20:26.024 --> 00:20:31.129
this piece of shot was lodged in this man's leg for perhaps two months before he died.

00:20:33.966 --> 00:20:38.870
These three cranial sections show very well how bone responds following trauma.

00:20:39.538 --> 00:20:40.639
This is a skull section,

00:20:40.672 --> 00:20:42.908
an entrance wound going to the inner part of the skull.

00:20:43.375 --> 00:20:44.876
If we look around the fracture margin,

00:20:45.644 --> 00:20:46.845
there's no bony growth,

00:20:46.878 --> 00:20:48.847
and we know this man died as a result of this injury.

00:20:48.847 --> 00:20:49.414
injury.

00:20:49.414 --> 00:20:51.617
This man lived for a month with this cranial injury.

00:20:51.950 --> 00:20:56.855
You can see there's some discoloration around the corners of the fracture margin here.

00:20:57.256 --> 00:20:58.957
If we look on the interior part of the skull,

00:20:59.258 --> 00:21:03.128
you also have some deposition of new bones called periosteal reaction.

00:21:03.328 --> 00:21:05.631
You can see some of the gray appearance here.

00:21:05.631 --> 00:21:10.269
This is a new bone that's being laid down during the healing and or infection process.

00:21:11.403 --> 00:21:15.941
And then if we advance the time even further and go to a similar injury in the cranium,

00:21:16.341 --> 00:21:18.176
this man lived for 10 years with this injury,

00:21:18.343 --> 00:21:19.378
a shot fracture injury.

00:21:19.378 --> 00:21:23.949
You can see there's been very good healing occurring around the site.

00:21:25.617 --> 00:21:29.655
Changes in the bones show the effects of one of the soldier's deadliest enemies,

00:21:29.988 --> 00:21:30.555
infection.

00:21:33.558 --> 00:21:36.428
This is the upper leg bone of Private Julius Fabre,

00:21:36.895 --> 00:21:38.330
who was wounded at Deep Bottom,

00:21:38.430 --> 00:21:38.864
Virginia,

00:21:39.197 --> 00:21:39.398
on the

00:21:39.798 --> 00:21:40.632
16th of August in

00:21:42.401 --> 00:21:45.304
1864. He was shot just above the knee,

00:21:45.470 --> 00:21:46.505
the knee would be here,

00:21:46.872 --> 00:21:49.808
and his limb was amputated the following day on the

00:21:50.142 --> 00:21:50.742
17th of August.

00:21:51.143 --> 00:21:56.748
He lived for six years with this femur becoming infected.

00:21:57.649 --> 00:21:58.917
You can see the change that's occurred.

00:21:58.917 --> 00:22:01.420
Let me show you a normal femur for comparison.

00:22:02.254 --> 00:22:05.624
You can see the amount of infection that has set in on this bone.

00:22:07.659 --> 00:22:07.893
Later,

00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:12.297
Fabre required more extensive surgery as the infection spread toward his hip.

00:22:16.301 --> 00:22:17.469
For archival purposes,

00:22:17.736 --> 00:22:22.174
the museum routinely photographed amputees alongside their severed limbs.

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:32.117
The first extensive medical encyclopedia on military casualties was made possible by these Civil War survivors.

00:22:33.118 --> 00:22:39.391
But it would take another major war before scientists could begin to put names to soldiers who had died.

00:22:42.694 --> 00:22:43.128
December 7th,

00:22:43.128 --> 00:22:45.931
1941.

00:22:45.931 --> 00:22:46.965
In the pre-dawn light,

00:22:47.299 --> 00:22:49.568
Japanese bombers appear out of nowhere.

00:23:05.951 --> 00:23:06.752
Sirens shriek,

00:23:07.319 --> 00:23:08.387
bombs explode.

00:23:09.721 --> 00:23:11.390
Pearl Harbor is destroyed.

00:23:12.057 --> 00:23:13.458
America is at war.

00:23:14.826 --> 00:23:15.360
Ironically,

00:23:15.694 --> 00:23:22.401
a war that took millions of lives would become the greatest boon to the fledgling science of forensic anthropology.

00:23:23.402 --> 00:23:28.540
World War II was the first war for which our service members had documented health records.

00:23:29.374 --> 00:23:33.145
By matching skeletal remains against known medical information,

00:23:33.779 --> 00:23:41.353
scientists were able to gather enough anatomical statistics to understand the life history each of us has written in our bones.

00:23:43.588 --> 00:23:45.190
During World War II and Korea,

00:23:45.757 --> 00:23:58.203
we had the first opportunity as a science to really look at some good documented modern samples and develop some new techniques for determining stature and age on these men that were killed during the war.

00:23:58.837 --> 00:24:00.338
Since we knew who they were,

00:24:00.372 --> 00:24:02.174
they were positively identified,

00:24:02.474 --> 00:24:09.181
we could measure the length of a femur or a long bone and plug that into the stature regression formula or make new stature regression formula from that.

00:24:10.949 --> 00:24:13.385
Now with a simple wooden board and a

00:24:13.385 --> 00:24:15.020
a set of mathematical equations,

00:24:15.454 --> 00:24:19.224
anthropologists could begin to piece together profiles of the dead.

00:24:20.358 --> 00:24:22.928
This bone is 323 millimeters long,

00:24:23.495 --> 00:24:25.564
and if we plug that into our stature formula,

00:24:25.664 --> 00:24:27.299
our regression formula for stature,

00:24:28.099 --> 00:24:29.801
we know this is a white male.

00:24:30.735 --> 00:24:33.905
We come up with an estimate of about 67 and a half inches,

00:24:34.439 --> 00:24:35.941
plus or minus an inch on either side.

00:24:35.941 --> 00:24:38.643
That would be the stature estimate for this individual.

00:24:40.479 --> 00:24:44.115
But there are nearly 90,000 Americans from World War II,

00:24:44.416 --> 00:24:44.783
Korea,

00:24:45.016 --> 00:24:48.220
and Vietnam whose whereabouts are still unknown.

00:24:50.422 --> 00:24:53.258
Some of their remains have been recovered but not yet named.

00:24:54.226 --> 00:24:59.231
Other soldiers exist only as charred and shattered fragments hidden in remote jungle.

00:25:00.932 --> 00:25:03.568
But these men and women are far from forgotten.

00:25:04.436 --> 00:25:09.307
The search for their identity is one of the great detective stories in forensic science.

00:25:11.142 --> 00:25:14.779
More than two and a half million Americans served in the Vietnam War,

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:17.315
the longest war in American history.

00:25:18.650 --> 00:25:22.487
58,000 died at an average age of 19.

00:25:24.489 --> 00:25:26.191
Boys barely out of high school.

00:25:29.127 --> 00:25:32.597
the ferocious guerrilla combat mangled and mutilated bodies,

00:25:33.098 --> 00:25:34.533
often beyond recognition.

00:25:38.003 --> 00:25:43.074
The remains of over 2,100 of these young men never made it back to the United States.

00:25:43.975 --> 00:25:47.879
Many of the men were buried upon impact at crash sites in the jungles,

00:25:48.446 --> 00:25:51.016
where their bones lay undiscovered in the foliage,

00:25:51.683 --> 00:25:53.485
decomposing from the intense,

00:25:53.785 --> 00:25:54.119
moist,

00:25:54.319 --> 00:25:55.120
tropical heat.

00:25:57.989 --> 00:25:59.891
After nearly 20 years in the military,

00:26:00.792 --> 00:26:02.661
38-year-old Air Force Major Edward M.

00:26:02.694 --> 00:26:08.133
Hudgens was preparing to retire when he was sent to Southeast Asia in 1969.

00:26:10.101 --> 00:26:11.269
At home in Big Spring,

00:26:11.336 --> 00:26:11.736
Texas,

00:26:12.037 --> 00:26:14.839
he left behind his wife Mary and their four children,

00:26:15.273 --> 00:26:15.640
Stacy,

00:26:16.141 --> 00:26:16.441
Wendy,

00:26:16.875 --> 00:26:17.108
Jeff,

00:26:17.409 --> 00:26:17.909
and Doug.

00:26:20.845 --> 00:26:21.680
On March 21,

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:23.415
1970,

00:26:23.415 --> 00:26:24.716
seven months into his tour,

00:26:25.350 --> 00:26:32.324
Major Hudgens was leading a search and rescue mission flying over Laos when his A-1 aircraft was hit by intense enemy fire.

00:26:35.360 --> 00:26:39.130
Other pilots saw his plane go into an inverted spin and crash,

00:26:39.598 --> 00:26:43.568
but they saw no parachute and heard no emergency beeper signals.

00:26:44.536 --> 00:26:49.240
There was never any uncertainty as to my husband's death.

00:26:50.208 --> 00:26:54.245
The uncertainty was not knowing when they were going to bring him back.

00:26:54.713 --> 00:26:59.351
All we could do was wait and wait for the government to get permission to go in.

00:26:59.951 --> 00:27:03.755
They were supposed to go into the site one time about two years ago,

00:27:04.155 --> 00:27:06.124
and we were all excited about it.

00:27:06.324 --> 00:27:10.729
And then there was a change in plans,

00:27:10.729 --> 00:27:12.030
and they could not go in.

00:27:12.230 --> 00:27:12.430
And

00:27:13.031 --> 00:27:14.399
I was absolutely devastated.

00:27:14.432 --> 00:27:15.533
It just tore me apart,

00:27:15.700 --> 00:27:18.203
and I didn't realize how much I had counted on that.

00:27:20.171 --> 00:27:22.040
Mary Hudgens has never remarried.

00:27:22.774 --> 00:27:23.975
Using her VA benefits,

00:27:24.175 --> 00:27:25.210
she went back to school,

00:27:25.543 --> 00:27:26.111
got a job,

00:27:26.411 --> 00:27:28.179
and raised her four children alone.

00:27:29.848 --> 00:27:33.385
When other kids my age have lost a parent,

00:27:34.119 --> 00:27:37.122
they have a cemetery and a grave site they can go to,

00:27:37.255 --> 00:27:39.257
and they can commune with their parent,

00:27:39.724 --> 00:27:40.392
their lost one.

00:27:40.925 --> 00:27:41.726
We didn't have that.

00:27:43.461 --> 00:27:46.865
Hope for the Hudgens family lies thousands of miles away,

00:27:47.298 --> 00:27:52.037
where scientists they had never met worked to find Ed Hudgens and bring him home.

00:28:05.583 --> 00:28:05.784
The U.S.

00:28:05.784 --> 00:28:08.086
National Cemetery is known as the Punchbowl.

00:28:11.723 --> 00:28:19.898
The stairs are flanked by stone walls etched with the names of thousands of soldiers who disappeared in combat beginning with the Second World War.

00:28:40.585 --> 00:28:42.520
Nearby at Hickam Air Force Base,

00:28:42.721 --> 00:28:43.154
the U.S.

00:28:43.288 --> 00:28:43.455
Army...

00:28:43.655 --> 00:28:45.356
Central Identification Laboratory,

00:28:45.423 --> 00:28:45.857
Hawaii,

00:28:46.391 --> 00:28:47.492
SEALHI for short,

00:28:47.959 --> 00:28:51.029
is one of the world's leading institutions in forensic science.

00:28:54.299 --> 00:29:00.038
The soldiers and civilians who work here have pledged that the unaccounted for are not to be forgotten.

00:29:04.008 --> 00:29:04.409
Currently,

00:29:04.809 --> 00:29:09.814
over 78,000 men and women are missing or unaccounted for from World War II.

00:29:10.682 --> 00:29:16.788
8,100 from Korea and 2,154 from the Vietnam War.

00:29:19.858 --> 00:29:25.063
Deputy Commander of SEAL-HI is retired Colonel Johnny Webb.

00:29:25.096 --> 00:29:25.363
Okay,

00:29:25.363 --> 00:29:31.703
as we prepare to go out to sites to recover service members lost in previous wars,

00:29:32.170 --> 00:29:38.276
a lot of work has to be accomplished before the teams can actually go out and begin the recovery operations.

00:29:40.812 --> 00:29:43.214
Metal shelves hold thousands of files.

00:29:44.482 --> 00:29:49.120
In each folder are the medical and dental records of soldiers missing from the Vietnam War.

00:29:51.222 --> 00:29:51.556
Today,

00:29:52.023 --> 00:29:56.094
much of the information has been transferred into a massive computer database.

00:30:01.366 --> 00:30:08.339
The real research is done in this room to prepare our teams to go out and do the excavations,

00:30:08.973 --> 00:30:14.746
as well as to provide the information to the scientific staff to make the individual identifications.

00:30:17.115 --> 00:30:20.218
Before SEAL-HI sends an excavation team into the field,

00:30:20.585 --> 00:30:24.622
they study the reports from the Casualty Data Section for each missing soldier.

00:30:27.992 --> 00:30:31.329
They then research any information gathered from outside sources.

00:30:34.933 --> 00:30:38.703
Eyewitness reports from local villagers who may have found a plane crash,

00:30:39.237 --> 00:30:41.739
or people conducting oil or timber exploration,

00:30:42.006 --> 00:30:46.911
can prove invaluable in pinpointing the exact location where a soldier was last seen.

00:30:55.353 --> 00:31:00.091
Seal High recovery teams train in Hawaii before excavations begin abroad.

00:31:02.060 --> 00:31:07.999
A mock demonstration site simulates an A-6 series aircraft crash with meticulous detail.

00:31:12.136 --> 00:31:14.239
Here we have a lot of pin flags,

00:31:14.272 --> 00:31:15.073
different colors,

00:31:15.840 --> 00:31:17.442
yellow designating wreckage,

00:31:18.376 --> 00:31:21.179
red designating unexploded ordnance or bombs,

00:31:21.679 --> 00:31:24.749
and the blue is designating any life support material,

00:31:25.149 --> 00:31:28.486
things that the pilot was wearing at the time of the incident.

00:31:29.921 --> 00:31:37.729
A 12 by 16 meter area is laid out in 4 by 4 meter units so the team can document where each artifact is found.

00:31:44.802 --> 00:31:48.006
Dirt is carefully removed and brought to a sifting station,

00:31:48.406 --> 00:31:51.509
where it is sorted by hand through a fine mesh screen.

00:32:02.353 --> 00:32:06.291
The smallest fragment might make the difference in finding a soldier's name.

00:32:07.492 --> 00:32:10.228
The team is looking for anything that can be identified,

00:32:10.595 --> 00:32:12.430
either from the aircraft or personnel,

00:32:13.164 --> 00:32:13.631
bones,

00:32:14.065 --> 00:32:14.799
ID tags,

00:32:15.066 --> 00:32:16.768
or pieces of a life support system.

00:32:22.707 --> 00:32:26.678
Metal detectors scour the site in hopes of finding scraps from the plane.

00:32:29.047 --> 00:32:29.647
In the field,

00:32:29.948 --> 00:32:31.816
the detectors are vitally important,

00:32:32.183 --> 00:32:35.687
as they also help locate any live ammunition still on the site.

00:32:39.624 --> 00:32:40.425
Mostly coral,

00:32:40.525 --> 00:32:41.993
just this band of red.

00:32:44.228 --> 00:32:47.532
Ground-penetrating radar sends radio waves into the earth,

00:32:47.799 --> 00:32:49.667
which reflect off buried objects.

00:32:51.402 --> 00:32:53.738
Disturbance close to the surface.

00:32:54.072 --> 00:32:56.307
The surface profile has been disturbed recently.

00:32:57.575 --> 00:32:57.875
Okay,

00:32:57.909 --> 00:32:58.876
we have an anomaly.

00:32:59.877 --> 00:33:01.079
We have metal objects.

00:33:02.714 --> 00:33:05.650
Different types of materials create colored bands,

00:33:05.984 --> 00:33:08.419
which helps the team assess the likelihood of a burial.

00:33:11.823 --> 00:33:20.365
The first dramatic use is that when we go to Southeast Asia and we have these witnesses to incidents that occurred in the 60s,

00:33:20.365 --> 00:33:21.632
these people are apt to be old.

00:33:22.300 --> 00:33:22.900
And what they do,

00:33:23.034 --> 00:33:23.334
they say,

00:33:23.401 --> 00:33:23.701
well,

00:33:23.968 --> 00:33:24.302
yes,

00:33:25.269 --> 00:33:26.237
I did bury him.

00:33:26.738 --> 00:33:27.538
I buried the American,

00:33:28.406 --> 00:33:30.408
and I buried him somewhere around here,

00:33:30.441 --> 00:33:31.409
but it's changed.

00:33:31.743 --> 00:33:32.877
And then they might point to an area,

00:33:33.611 --> 00:33:36.147
it's a football field in size,

00:33:36.147 --> 00:33:36.447
you know?

00:33:36.848 --> 00:33:37.749
Maybe the body's there,

00:33:37.849 --> 00:33:38.483
maybe it isn't.

00:33:38.916 --> 00:33:42.687
But I can eliminate vast areas of it within minutes.

00:33:44.722 --> 00:33:46.691
After weeks and months of training,

00:33:47.358 --> 00:33:51.396
men and technology are put to the test in a once forbidden land.

00:33:53.231 --> 00:33:58.136
the jungles of Vietnam that claimed its victims more than two decades ago.

00:34:06.577 --> 00:34:14.419
The Army's Central Identification Laboratory sends teams all over the world to find the remains of service members who are missing or unaccounted for.

00:34:15.586 --> 00:34:17.989
Working in some of the world's most remote locations,

00:34:18.322 --> 00:34:22.827
the teams work tirelessly to ensure that they recover every piece of evidence.

00:34:25.863 --> 00:34:28.232
But two decades after the Vietnam War,

00:34:28.566 --> 00:34:30.568
crash sites are far from intact.

00:34:31.536 --> 00:34:34.405
Villagers have scavenged whatever they could find a use for.

00:34:36.908 --> 00:34:41.913
This piece of wreckage from a downed American airplane is being used as a blade for a plow.

00:34:44.649 --> 00:34:49.587
And now that plow may be hundreds of miles from where the plane actually went down.

00:34:51.322 --> 00:34:57.628
Seal High teams work closely with local villagers who might remember the crash or may have discovered some wreckage.

00:34:58.262 --> 00:34:58.763
The goal,

00:34:59.263 --> 00:35:02.467
to try to pinpoint the exact location of the crash.

00:35:03.434 --> 00:35:05.736
She said she saw 13 people.

00:35:06.170 --> 00:35:09.740
She personally counted 13 people that morning.

00:35:14.178 --> 00:35:22.954
The team compares the information they receive from the villagers with detailed maps prepared at seal-high to determine the coordinates of the probable crash site.

00:35:25.123 --> 00:35:31.863
Global positioning systems fix their exact position as they trek sometimes for miles toward the site.

00:35:43.841 --> 00:35:44.775
Right head on it.

00:35:44.775 --> 00:35:49.680
Right in the middle of it.

00:35:49.714 --> 00:35:51.215
Once the excavation is set up,

00:35:51.616 --> 00:35:54.952
work begins to find and clear the area of any ammunition.

00:35:58.389 --> 00:36:00.625
The work requires patience and precision,

00:36:01.092 --> 00:36:02.760
and can be extremely dangerous.

00:36:05.763 --> 00:36:06.831
Okay,

00:36:06.864 --> 00:36:10.902
move on back.

00:36:12.470 --> 00:36:16.574
Live 40 millimeter shells litter this C-130 crash site.

00:36:18.042 --> 00:36:18.976
Once they are located,

00:36:19.210 --> 00:36:24.382
they have to be carefully removed and carried to a safe distance where they will be disposed of later.

00:36:30.288 --> 00:36:33.491
The clues at the crash site come in different shapes and sizes.

00:36:34.926 --> 00:36:35.960
When a plane crashes,

00:36:36.360 --> 00:36:41.632
artifacts and remains bear the unmistakable scars of the 600 mile per hour impact.

00:36:43.334 --> 00:36:45.970
Large guns are buried by the force of the crash.

00:36:46.571 --> 00:36:48.506
The plane is literally torn apart.

00:36:50.174 --> 00:36:53.177
Pieces of the fuselage are scattered in all directions.

00:36:56.180 --> 00:36:59.584
It's a helmet.

00:37:00.184 --> 00:37:01.719
Microphone assembly.

00:37:01.852 --> 00:37:02.386
Fire's helmet.

00:37:07.592 --> 00:37:08.459
Deep in the jungle,

00:37:08.759 --> 00:37:12.463
another Seal High scouting team uncovers a button from a flight suit.

00:37:15.066 --> 00:37:15.266
Then,

00:37:15.499 --> 00:37:19.303
further digging reveals a remnant of fabric from the suit beneath the den.

00:37:19.604 --> 00:37:20.571
jungle foliage.

00:37:22.974 --> 00:37:24.108
In a high-tech war,

00:37:24.508 --> 00:37:26.644
soldiers are mangled by their machines.

00:37:28.946 --> 00:37:32.583
Finding an intact skeleton in Vietnam would be inconceivable.

00:37:33.384 --> 00:37:35.820
So every fragment is a vital clue.

00:37:36.787 --> 00:37:40.191
The most significant finds are human bones and teeth.

00:37:44.795 --> 00:37:50.935
All the remains found in Vietnam are brought back to the Seal High Laboratory in Hawaii for analysis.

00:37:58.809 --> 00:37:59.443
Sometimes,

00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:03.147
all the forensic scientists have to work with are mere fragments.

00:38:06.751 --> 00:38:07.418
From these,

00:38:07.618 --> 00:38:09.654
they must piece together whether they are human,

00:38:10.154 --> 00:38:10.621
and if so,

00:38:11.122 --> 00:38:12.256
the individual's age,

00:38:12.590 --> 00:38:12.890
race,

00:38:13.324 --> 00:38:13.557
height.

00:38:14.058 --> 00:38:14.225
weight,

00:38:14.792 --> 00:38:15.660
and ultimately,

00:38:16.093 --> 00:38:16.694
their name.

00:38:23.034 --> 00:38:23.501
To date,

00:38:23.868 --> 00:38:29.940
SEAL-HI has positively identified 570 unaccounted war service members from World War II,

00:38:31.042 --> 00:38:31.442
Korea,

00:38:32.276 --> 00:38:33.010
and Vietnam,

00:38:35.112 --> 00:38:36.914
used to their families.

00:38:39.417 --> 00:38:43.421
Often bones arrive with no clear idea of where they came from.

00:38:44.422 --> 00:38:48.025
Identifying those remains is Sihlhai's most difficult task.

00:38:49.026 --> 00:38:52.797
Many times several portions of a skeleton arrive at Sihlhai,

00:38:53.297 --> 00:38:56.634
each reported to be the remains of the same missing serviceman.

00:38:57.902 --> 00:39:04.075
Sihlhai anthropologists must try to identify each fragment of bone and determine who it belonged to.

00:39:05.309 --> 00:39:08.179
When bones cannot be identified by the standard methods,

00:39:08.579 --> 00:39:12.083
Seal High turns to DNA testing to analyze the remains.

00:39:12.983 --> 00:39:15.653
But the test won't work on small bone samples.

00:39:16.220 --> 00:39:23.227
I will tell you that this is the only bone fragment on the table that is large enough and in a good enough state of preservation,

00:39:23.527 --> 00:39:23.994
we think,

00:39:24.662 --> 00:39:25.463
to yield DNA.

00:39:26.997 --> 00:39:28.566
Before the bone can be tested,

00:39:28.833 --> 00:39:30.801
a sample must be cut and measured.

00:39:33.437 --> 00:39:39.510
Mitochondrial DNA will be extracted from the bone sample and compared to that of a living maternal relative.

00:39:39.577 --> 00:39:42.179
What do you think,

00:39:42.179 --> 00:39:43.114
5 grams?

00:39:43.214 --> 00:39:43.848
5 grams.

00:39:45.383 --> 00:39:54.825
We're going to send this sample to AFTL and in about 4 weeks they should issue a report to us on whether or not they were able to sequence mitochondrial DNA out of this sample.

00:40:00.264 --> 00:40:05.736
Before any work begins at the Armed Forces DNA Identification Laboratory outside Washington,

00:40:05.770 --> 00:40:06.036
D.C.,

00:40:06.704 --> 00:40:09.440
the bone sample must be catalogued and photographed.

00:40:14.111 --> 00:40:14.879
In the laboratory,

00:40:15.279 --> 00:40:18.215
before the process of extracting its DNA can begin,

00:40:18.749 --> 00:40:23.087
the exterior surface of the bone sample is cleaned to remove any contaminants.

00:40:25.122 --> 00:40:25.322
Then...

00:40:25.823 --> 00:40:27.491
Two gram fragment is washed,

00:40:27.825 --> 00:40:28.259
dried,

00:40:28.492 --> 00:40:29.994
and pulverized in a blender.

00:40:33.030 --> 00:40:39.937
The powder then undergoes a chemical reaction that disrupts the cells and releases the DNA into a liquid solution.

00:40:43.073 --> 00:40:48.112
This incubates overnight with an enzyme which breaks up the proteins within the cells,

00:40:48.446 --> 00:40:49.914
leaving purified DNA.

00:40:53.150 --> 00:40:56.754
Mitch Holland is the branch chief of service in genetic systems.

00:40:57.655 --> 00:40:59.890
DNA fingerprinting is just like a fingerprint.

00:41:00.791 --> 00:41:02.660
What we do is we look along the sequence of the DNA,

00:41:03.327 --> 00:41:05.162
we analyze those regions,

00:41:05.629 --> 00:41:06.630
we compare those regions to,

00:41:06.664 --> 00:41:06.797
say,

00:41:06.797 --> 00:41:07.498
a suspect,

00:41:07.865 --> 00:41:08.699
a family member.

00:41:09.033 --> 00:41:09.934
When we do that comparison,

00:41:09.934 --> 00:41:11.702
we're looking for inclusions and exclusions.

00:41:11.702 --> 00:41:13.471
We're looking for matches and non-matches.

00:41:15.873 --> 00:41:19.143
Scientists at the Armed Forces Lab have a two-fold job.

00:41:19.610 --> 00:41:22.780
To identify DNA fingerprints from blood samples,

00:41:23.147 --> 00:41:28.118
and the more complicated task of extracting DNA from remains which may be decades old.

00:41:30.221 --> 00:41:33.224
In order to match the DNA fingerprint of a missing soldier,

00:41:33.824 --> 00:41:39.597
the scientists must test it against a blood sample which has been mailed to the Institute by a maternal relative.

00:41:43.000 --> 00:41:51.141
The process of extracting DNA from old skeletal remains is such a significant advance in biological science that in 1993,

00:41:51.575 --> 00:41:52.443
one of its creators,

00:41:52.743 --> 00:41:52.977
Dr.

00:41:53.043 --> 00:41:53.711
Cary Mullis,

00:41:54.078 --> 00:41:55.379
earned a Nobel Prize.

00:42:00.284 --> 00:42:03.454
Scientists had known of DNA since the 19th century,

00:42:03.988 --> 00:42:07.358
but the mysterious nucleic acid structure was not known until the

00:42:07.725 --> 00:42:08.225
20th century.

00:42:10.561 --> 00:42:11.595
In 1962,

00:42:12.062 --> 00:42:18.569
James Watson and Francis Crick won the Nobel Prize for uncovering DNA's three-dimensional molecular structure.

00:42:21.238 --> 00:42:22.673
There are two types of DNA.

00:42:23.440 --> 00:42:24.308
Nuclear DNA,

00:42:24.742 --> 00:42:26.744
which is found only in the cell's nucleus,

00:42:27.344 --> 00:42:28.779
and mitochondrial DNA,

00:42:29.280 --> 00:42:31.048
found in the surrounding mitochondria.

00:42:32.616 --> 00:42:35.019
The first type is not as plentiful as the second,

00:42:35.419 --> 00:42:38.756
which can endure in bones for thousands of years after death.

00:42:42.092 --> 00:42:42.860
Both varieties,

00:42:43.294 --> 00:42:48.165
DNA resembles a twisting ladder with cross rungs composed of adenine,

00:42:48.566 --> 00:42:49.066
thymine,

00:42:49.567 --> 00:42:50.100
guanine,

00:42:50.334 --> 00:42:51.135
and cytosine,

00:42:51.602 --> 00:42:51.969
or A,

00:42:52.336 --> 00:42:52.469
T,

00:42:53.003 --> 00:42:53.103
G,

00:42:53.437 --> 00:42:54.505
and C for short.

00:42:56.707 --> 00:42:59.810
The prints in DNA fingerprinting are pieces of A,

00:43:00.044 --> 00:43:00.177
T,

00:43:00.511 --> 00:43:00.644
G,

00:43:00.945 --> 00:43:03.814
and C of varying lengths and arrangements.

00:43:06.150 --> 00:43:09.253
Mitochondrial DNA is inherited only from the mother.

00:43:09.954 --> 00:43:10.454
Therefore,

00:43:10.754 --> 00:43:15.059
people related along maternal lines share identical patterns in their A's,

00:43:15.359 --> 00:43:15.759
C's,

00:43:16.026 --> 00:43:16.427
G's,

00:43:16.527 --> 00:43:19.630
and T's that non-related people probably would not.

00:43:20.931 --> 00:43:24.168
A match indicates a high probability of identification,

00:43:24.702 --> 00:43:27.738
but in and of itself is not 100%

00:43:27.871 --> 00:43:28.539
conclusive.

00:43:30.441 --> 00:43:32.443
To have a large enough sample to work with,

00:43:32.843 --> 00:43:38.515
scientists are able to reproduce or copy the DNA in a machine called a thermocycler,

00:43:39.016 --> 00:43:43.687
which synthesizes millions of exact copies of the DNA within a few hours.

00:43:45.990 --> 00:43:49.226
The process is known as a polymerase chain reaction.

00:43:53.163 --> 00:43:58.135
The sample is then carefully loaded onto a gel through which an electric current is passed.

00:44:00.170 --> 00:44:04.208
Ultraviolet light helps visualize the individual bars of DNA.

00:44:06.844 --> 00:44:07.077
Then,

00:44:07.311 --> 00:44:10.681
several samples are loaded onto what is known as a sequencing gel,

00:44:11.281 --> 00:44:12.583
one sample per lane.

00:44:14.818 --> 00:44:16.887
The sample is then read by a laser,

00:44:17.254 --> 00:44:20.457
which runs back and forth across it several hundred times a minute.

00:44:24.294 --> 00:44:26.030
The information is then converted into a peak,

00:44:26.430 --> 00:44:27.965
which can be translated into an A,

00:44:28.499 --> 00:44:28.599
G,

00:44:29.133 --> 00:44:29.233
T,

00:44:29.633 --> 00:44:29.900
or C.

00:44:32.703 --> 00:44:34.805
When compared to the family reference sample,

00:44:35.205 --> 00:44:36.640
if the sequences are identical,

00:44:36.940 --> 00:44:38.709
they are considered a probable match.

00:44:42.613 --> 00:44:43.447
Over the years,

00:44:43.714 --> 00:44:48.218
remains alleged to be those of Ed Hudgens came to Seal High four times.

00:44:49.086 --> 00:44:50.454
Once from an unknown source,

00:44:51.155 --> 00:44:52.523
once from a Laotian native,

00:44:52.890 --> 00:44:54.892
and twice from his known crash site.

00:44:55.859 --> 00:44:59.029
The bone tested for DNA was from the Laotian source,

00:44:59.363 --> 00:45:01.832
and was clearly ruled out by the lab tests.

00:45:02.733 --> 00:45:07.705
But some of the bones acquired from the unknown source aligned with fragments from the crash site.

00:45:08.605 --> 00:45:11.575
These can be certified as belonging to Hudgens.

00:45:13.310 --> 00:45:17.147
Artifacts at the site found near Ed's remains included his watch,

00:45:18.382 --> 00:45:19.783
a buckle from his seat harness,

00:45:21.085 --> 00:45:22.186
a dog tag chain,

00:45:22.519 --> 00:45:23.253
and a dime,

00:45:24.788 --> 00:45:27.825
an American coin deep in a faraway jungle.

00:45:30.194 --> 00:45:31.662
But it would be a single tooth,

00:45:32.129 --> 00:45:34.865
not much bigger than the dime he carried in his pocket,

00:45:35.265 --> 00:45:38.569
which provided the pivotal clue the scientists sought.

00:45:45.609 --> 00:45:45.809
Good.

00:45:45.909 --> 00:45:48.078
Cases come into the Seal High Laboratory.

00:45:48.445 --> 00:45:49.847
If there are dental remains,

00:45:49.880 --> 00:45:52.282
they are studied by forensic odontologists.

00:45:53.083 --> 00:45:57.788
They look for any unique characteristics which would help match the tooth to a missing soldier.

00:46:00.724 --> 00:46:01.291
This tooth,

00:46:01.725 --> 00:46:02.693
we see a root canal.

00:46:03.327 --> 00:46:03.827
In other words,

00:46:03.827 --> 00:46:07.498
the tooth had the insides removed and was filled with paste.

00:46:08.532 --> 00:46:12.503
And that matches very closely with what we see in the post-mortem x-rays.

00:46:13.537 --> 00:46:18.609
Other fillings that are easy to match are these single fillings on the lower teeth,

00:46:19.109 --> 00:46:20.911
here and here.

00:46:22.880 --> 00:46:25.382
as well as this series of fillings on the upper teeth.

00:46:26.350 --> 00:46:30.187
And so this is a good example of when we have enough evidence to work with,

00:46:30.721 --> 00:46:33.190
both anti-mortem and post-mortem,

00:46:33.557 --> 00:46:36.059
about how closely we can match the dental records.

00:46:39.096 --> 00:46:40.130
Teeth are x-rayed,

00:46:40.297 --> 00:46:44.635
and the information is directly input into a huge database of dental records.

00:46:48.672 --> 00:46:52.676
This represents the post-mortem image that we just took on the tooth.

00:46:54.144 --> 00:46:59.082
All the dental records of people missing from Southeast Asia and Korea are kept on file.

00:46:59.983 --> 00:47:10.360
The forensic odontologist enters the specific details of the tooth that has been found into the computer and begins to make a match by checking the tooth against the information in the records.

00:47:12.462 --> 00:47:14.998
This patient had fillings on...

00:47:16.800 --> 00:47:20.337
A couple of teeth in the top right side.

00:47:21.405 --> 00:47:27.110
The computer checks the characteristics of this single tooth against the thousands of records in its memory.

00:47:28.278 --> 00:47:29.780
Each time it eliminates a record,

00:47:29.947 --> 00:47:32.015
it moves closer to finding a match.

00:47:32.883 --> 00:47:35.586
The forensic odontologist enters more information,

00:47:35.919 --> 00:47:38.822
and the computer eliminates all but four records.

00:47:39.389 --> 00:47:42.459
This tooth could belong to one of four missing men.

00:47:43.527 --> 00:47:45.729
One more piece of information remains.

00:47:46.630 --> 00:47:50.033
This molar had a small filling in the bottom right quadrant.

00:47:50.801 --> 00:47:54.204
This information eliminates all the records but one.

00:47:54.972 --> 00:47:56.673
The team has made a match.

00:47:59.843 --> 00:48:05.749
Can you take this down to casualty data and tell them that we have a pretty good hit on CAPME and I'd like to take a look at the record?

00:48:06.049 --> 00:48:06.183
Yes,

00:48:06.250 --> 00:48:06.450
sir.

00:48:06.683 --> 00:48:06.984
Thanks.

00:48:09.686 --> 00:48:11.588
Only a few hundred bone fragments,

00:48:11.922 --> 00:48:14.091
most less than half an inch in size,

00:48:14.358 --> 00:48:18.462
and one tooth can be said with certainty to belong to Ed Hudgens.

00:48:19.796 --> 00:48:20.464
For the family,

00:48:20.864 --> 00:48:21.431
it is enough.

00:48:24.034 --> 00:48:25.302
Whether transporting tiny,

00:48:25.535 --> 00:48:29.873
unidentified bone fragments from an excavation site to a lab in Hawaii,

00:48:30.674 --> 00:48:34.778
or finally sending a soldier with a name home to his loved ones,

00:48:35.412 --> 00:48:38.448
Seal High treats all remains with utmost respect.

00:48:45.555 --> 00:48:45.856
All right,

00:48:45.856 --> 00:48:46.256
hurt

00:48:56.633 --> 00:48:58.268
At Arlington National Cemetery,

00:48:58.669 --> 00:49:00.637
the ritual is repeated every hour,

00:49:01.104 --> 00:49:03.273
365 days of the year.

00:49:04.207 --> 00:49:05.175
Fallen heroes,

00:49:05.375 --> 00:49:06.243
forever honored,

00:49:06.777 --> 00:49:09.212
yet forever unnamed and unknown.

00:49:13.083 --> 00:49:13.550
One hero,

00:49:14.151 --> 00:49:15.252
once among the missing,

00:49:15.619 --> 00:49:17.220
has finally been found.

00:49:20.757 --> 00:49:23.193
He comes to eternal rest with a name.

00:49:23.961 --> 00:49:26.596
Full military honors and a purple heart.

00:49:36.340 --> 00:49:38.041
For Mary Hudgens and her children,

00:49:38.608 --> 00:49:39.209
Ed Hudgens'

00:49:39.276 --> 00:49:43.113
journey cannot be measured in years or calculated in miles.

00:49:44.481 --> 00:49:45.382
Blade.

00:49:45.615 --> 00:49:58.662
in the jungle for 26 years and we now have put him to rest and with honors and his his fellow countrymen have honored him and it brings it to a conclusion for us

00:50:05.502 --> 00:50:08.105
26 years to the day after Ed Hudgens'

00:50:08.171 --> 00:50:10.807
plane crashed in the jungles of Southeast Asia,

00:50:11.408 --> 00:50:14.344
his children lay a wreath at the Tomb of the Unknowns.

00:50:32.295 --> 00:50:35.932
Dozens of scientists around the world have worked toward this day.

00:50:36.733 --> 00:50:37.601
Piece by piece,

00:50:38.101 --> 00:50:38.902
clue by clue,

00:50:39.302 --> 00:50:43.707
they have unraveled a mystery that tormented a family for more than a quarter century.

00:50:45.208 --> 00:50:48.578
The final chapter in this soldier's story has been written.

00:50:49.413 --> 00:50:51.014
A family is reunited.

00:50:51.882 --> 00:50:53.550
An incomplete circle is,

00:50:53.784 --> 00:50:54.651
at long last,

00:50:54.951 --> 00:50:55.485
closed.

00:50:58.221 --> 00:50:59.823
Ed Hudgens has come home.

00:51:07.898 --> 00:51:08.865
In Fairfax County,

00:51:08.965 --> 00:51:09.399
Virginia,

00:51:09.666 --> 00:51:11.401
police find a decomposed body,

00:51:12.569 --> 00:51:14.137
but have no report of a crime.

00:51:18.975 --> 00:51:19.776
In Fort Myers,

00:51:19.810 --> 00:51:20.210
Florida,

00:51:20.477 --> 00:51:22.779
a killer confesses to a grisly crime,

00:51:23.080 --> 00:51:24.781
but the police cannot find a body.

00:51:26.650 --> 00:51:28.485
A serial killer may go free,

00:51:28.919 --> 00:51:31.955
unless police find the evidence to put him behind bars.

00:51:37.928 --> 00:51:38.662
In each case,

00:51:38.962 --> 00:51:43.400
forensic scientists must find proof of the crime in the bones of the murder victim.

00:51:45.602 --> 00:51:49.940
Using scientific studies of human decay to find out when the victim died,

00:51:50.307 --> 00:51:51.808
so the killer may be traced.

00:51:53.677 --> 00:51:56.646
Hoping to prove there is no perfect crime.

00:52:00.584 --> 00:52:02.185
Dead men do talk.

00:52:36.353 --> 00:52:37.954
December 1993.

00:52:38.788 --> 00:52:40.390
In a wooded area near Washington,

00:52:40.423 --> 00:52:40.690
DC,

00:52:41.158 --> 00:52:43.326
a surveyor has made a gruesome discovery.

00:52:47.130 --> 00:52:49.900
While mapping out a new suburb in the Virginia countryside,

00:52:50.133 --> 00:52:52.969
he has stumbled on a body buried in a shallow grave.

00:53:06.917 --> 00:53:12.289
Officers from the Fairfax County Police Department arrive at the secluded area where the body had been found.

00:53:17.561 --> 00:53:21.031
The first homicide detective on the scene is Detective Jerry Farrell.

00:53:22.532 --> 00:53:22.632
Hi,

00:53:22.632 --> 00:53:22.933
how you doing?

00:53:22.933 --> 00:53:23.400
Officer Gregory,

00:53:23.433 --> 00:53:23.800
what do you got?

00:53:23.867 --> 00:53:26.036
There's a human skull right over here.

00:53:26.036 --> 00:53:33.476
OK.

00:53:33.476 --> 00:53:34.077
Let's take a look.

00:53:38.014 --> 00:53:43.787
The police assume it's a homicide.

00:53:43.820 --> 00:53:46.356
No immediate clues to help identify the victim.

00:53:47.791 --> 00:53:48.792
And Lieutenant Wilson,

00:53:49.059 --> 00:53:49.459
he's there.

00:53:49.459 --> 00:53:50.093
You want to take a look?

00:54:00.270 --> 00:54:02.405
What you're going to need is...

00:54:03.173 --> 00:54:05.675
Start mapping out the area.

00:54:06.876 --> 00:54:08.111
Secure this area here,

00:54:08.245 --> 00:54:12.882
and then we're going to need at least a 50-foot area perimeter set up around this one.

00:54:14.184 --> 00:54:16.686
The team of investigators will search a wide area.

00:54:18.622 --> 00:54:21.057
Scavengers have dragged parts of the body away.

00:54:26.496 --> 00:54:30.400
The police meticulously document the position of the body with photographs,

00:54:30.600 --> 00:54:31.067
sketches,

00:54:31.268 --> 00:54:31.868
and notes.

00:54:41.411 --> 00:54:44.481
Detective Dennis Wilson heads the cold case squad,

00:54:44.881 --> 00:54:47.917
investigating crimes where the trail of evidence has gone cold.

00:54:50.287 --> 00:54:51.087
In such cases,

00:54:51.454 --> 00:54:52.088
every clue,

00:54:52.522 --> 00:54:53.657
every piece of evidence,

00:54:53.657 --> 00:54:54.691
no matter how small,

00:54:55.091 --> 00:54:57.093
may be the key to solving the crime.

00:55:02.932 --> 00:55:04.467
From bits of clothing and hair,

00:55:04.834 --> 00:55:08.138
detectives Wilson and Farrell are fairly sure the victim is a woman.

00:55:10.340 --> 00:55:12.942
Since decomposition often leaves fatty residues,

00:55:13.276 --> 00:55:15.545
the officers take soil samples near the body.

00:55:16.579 --> 00:55:18.948
Chemical analysis could help determine when the victim was

00:55:18.948 --> 00:55:19.649
and was buried.

00:55:21.418 --> 00:55:21.584
Well,

00:55:21.851 --> 00:55:23.787
you only have one chance at a crime scene,

00:55:23.787 --> 00:55:25.455
and you have to do it right the first time.

00:55:25.755 --> 00:55:27.691
So you slow things down at that point.

00:55:27.724 --> 00:55:28.491
There's no hurry.

00:55:28.692 --> 00:55:30.960
The body's obviously been there for some time.

00:55:32.295 --> 00:55:35.999
We're going to try to gather all the evidence we can and make sure we don't miss anything.

00:55:36.066 --> 00:55:42.305
There just may be one small little clue here that's going to lead to either her identity or the identity of the perpetrator.

00:55:44.908 --> 00:55:47.243
Because the body is so badly decomposed,

00:55:47.510 --> 00:55:49.879
the detectives make a call to nearby Washington,

00:55:49.913 --> 00:55:50.180
D.C.

00:55:52.148 --> 00:55:52.449
Dr.

00:55:52.515 --> 00:55:58.822
Douglas Owsley of the Smithsonian Institution is an anthropologist whose specialty is identifying human remains.

00:56:00.023 --> 00:56:04.427
From soldiers who died in the Civil War to the modern-day victims of homicide,

00:56:05.428 --> 00:56:08.965
he has testified in many criminal trials as a forensic scientist.

00:56:09.532 --> 00:56:13.103
Forensic meaning science used as proof in a court of law.

00:56:14.771 --> 00:56:17.073
Receiving the call from the Fairfax County Police,

00:56:17.340 --> 00:56:20.176
he agrees to visit the site where the skeleton has been found.

00:56:22.812 --> 00:56:23.813
In the usual case,

00:56:24.080 --> 00:56:26.416
murder victims are assigned to medical examiners,

00:56:26.649 --> 00:56:29.018
who dissect the body to find the cause of death.

00:56:33.957 --> 00:56:37.093
But medical examiners are accustomed to dealing with fresh bodies.

00:56:43.600 --> 00:56:43.933
Here,

00:56:44.401 --> 00:56:46.703
only bones are left to tell the tale,

00:56:47.203 --> 00:56:49.839
requiring the special skills of the anthropologist.

00:56:51.241 --> 00:56:55.211
It takes three days of careful excavation to take the body from the ground.

00:57:00.450 --> 00:57:01.618
Back in his laboratory,

00:57:02.018 --> 00:57:04.421
Owsley hunts for signs of the victim's age,

00:57:04.821 --> 00:57:05.021
sex,

00:57:05.555 --> 00:57:06.556
and the manner of death.

00:57:09.359 --> 00:57:11.861
It is immediately apparent that the victim is female.

00:57:12.495 --> 00:57:16.266
The pelvic bone is wider in females to accommodate childbirth.

00:57:18.968 --> 00:57:21.304
Though Owsley believes the victim was a young adult,

00:57:21.671 --> 00:57:23.573
it's difficult to be more precise.

00:57:24.674 --> 00:57:26.676
She does not show any signs of arthritis,

00:57:27.544 --> 00:57:29.078
not severe arthritis.

00:57:29.279 --> 00:57:30.780
If you look at her spinal column,

00:57:31.881 --> 00:57:34.284
there's only minor changes,

00:57:34.984 --> 00:57:36.152
no development of arthritis.

00:57:38.521 --> 00:57:45.028
Her pubic bones are showing a stage of maturity that would be consistent with somebody that is about 30 years of age.

00:57:46.729 --> 00:57:47.530
In a young person,

00:57:47.797 --> 00:57:50.099
the sutures of the skull are plainly visible.

00:57:53.603 --> 00:57:54.671
As a person grows older,

00:57:54.938 --> 00:57:56.506
the sutures tend to fuse together.

00:57:58.341 --> 00:57:59.342
In the victim's skull,

00:57:59.642 --> 00:58:01.311
the sutures are starting to disappear,

00:58:01.711 --> 00:58:03.913
confirming that the victim was a young adult.

00:58:07.750 --> 00:58:09.486
Only 5 feet 1 inch tall,

00:58:09.886 --> 00:58:11.321
she'd been stabbed repeatedly.

00:58:12.622 --> 00:58:14.924
the knife blade leaving marks on the collarbone,

00:58:15.258 --> 00:58:15.625
ribs,

00:58:15.825 --> 00:58:16.426
and vertebrae.

00:58:20.296 --> 00:58:23.132
Looking at the pattern of cuts in the bone.

00:58:24.901 --> 00:58:28.338
You can tell that the individual was behind her at least some of the time.

00:58:29.205 --> 00:58:33.042
She has knife wounds that penetrated in the back and the midline,

00:58:34.444 --> 00:58:38.448
the cut that is in the clavicle and the collarbone.

00:58:39.816 --> 00:58:41.951
There's many different positions that could account for that,

00:58:41.985 --> 00:58:44.654
but one would be the individual reaching over her.

00:58:48.091 --> 00:58:50.727
Attaching a name to the body will not be easy.

00:58:51.528 --> 00:58:53.930
To shed more light on this mysterious case,

00:58:54.264 --> 00:58:56.733
police must determine the identity of the victim.

00:59:03.273 --> 00:59:04.073
In Fort Myers,

00:59:04.140 --> 00:59:04.574
Florida,

00:59:04.841 --> 00:59:06.409
police will have the opposite problem.

00:59:08.645 --> 00:59:10.380
A crime without a body.

00:59:10.380 --> 00:59:15.418
911,

00:59:15.418 --> 00:59:16.252
what is your emergency?

00:59:18.087 --> 00:59:20.056
Yeah.

00:59:20.056 --> 00:59:20.290
Okay,

00:59:20.390 --> 00:59:20.823
one moment,

00:59:20.890 --> 00:59:21.057
sir.

00:59:22.091 --> 00:59:22.292
Betty,

00:59:22.358 --> 00:59:23.293
I have a man on the phone.

00:59:23.326 --> 00:59:24.727
Advise he just murdered someone.

00:59:28.865 --> 00:59:31.134
In a police communication center in Fort Myers,

00:59:31.167 --> 00:59:31.568
Florida,

00:59:31.868 --> 00:59:37.807
a 911 call will lead to the discovery of a grisly crime dating to 1989.

00:59:37.840 --> 00:59:38.374
911,

00:59:38.441 --> 00:59:39.242
what is your emergency?

00:59:39.242 --> 00:59:41.811
I'd like to report a homicide on Piney Road.

00:59:41.811 --> 00:59:42.111
A murder?

00:59:42.245 --> 00:59:43.046
Where did it happen?

00:59:43.079 --> 00:59:48.184
I'm calling in because I should confess to what I did.

00:59:48.184 --> 00:59:48.718
And you did it?

00:59:48.718 --> 00:59:49.052
Yeah.

00:59:49.953 --> 00:59:52.855
The caller reports a murder he has recently committed.

00:59:54.257 --> 00:59:55.024
Why did you do that?

00:59:55.024 --> 00:59:56.059
I don't know.

00:59:56.059 --> 00:59:56.292
I don't know.

00:59:56.292 --> 00:59:57.794
I'm going with everything.

00:59:58.561 --> 01:00:00.263
He says he's thought of turning himself in,

01:00:00.597 --> 01:00:01.731
but hasn't decided yet.

01:00:01.831 --> 01:00:03.333
Why turn yourself in?

01:00:03.333 --> 01:00:03.399
Oh,

01:00:03.399 --> 01:00:03.499
man,

01:00:03.499 --> 01:00:06.302
I'm thinking about it.

01:00:06.336 --> 01:00:07.670
Are you going to wait there for us?

01:00:08.838 --> 01:00:11.808
The 911 call is traced to a shopping center phone booth.

01:00:13.409 --> 01:00:18.748
The operator tries to keep the killer on the line while a message goes out to police units in the area.

01:00:23.653 --> 01:00:24.988
Lake County 2-11-1,

01:00:24.988 --> 01:00:25.722
10-65,

01:00:25.722 --> 01:00:28.324
a possible signal 5 at Coral Gate Shopping Center.

01:00:28.858 --> 01:00:30.059
Orange Grove and Pondella.

01:00:30.827 --> 01:00:31.995
Orange Grove and Pondella.

01:00:35.331 --> 01:00:40.903
Officer Paul Rose monitors the call and finds no one at the shopping center phone booth where the call originated.

01:00:42.905 --> 01:00:44.774
But less than a mile from the shopping center,

01:00:45.041 --> 01:00:46.876
he has spotted a possible suspect.

01:00:51.914 --> 01:00:54.717
The suspect had been walking hurriedly at the side of the road,

01:00:55.118 --> 01:00:56.986
away from the direction of the shopping center.

01:01:00.356 --> 01:01:00.957
The suspect,

01:01:01.157 --> 01:01:02.492
whose name is Paul Klein,

01:01:02.859 --> 01:01:06.129
quickly admits he made the 911 call confessing murder.

01:01:10.133 --> 01:01:12.568
He is advised of his rights and taken into custody.

01:01:22.245 --> 01:01:23.112
The previous night,

01:01:23.246 --> 01:01:27.417
he'd broken into a house and strangled an elderly woman on the couch where she lay.

01:01:29.419 --> 01:01:31.020
There is no sign of anything missing.

01:01:31.954 --> 01:01:33.556
Robbery had not been the motive.

01:01:37.760 --> 01:01:38.961
Lieutenant Jeff Taylor,

01:01:39.262 --> 01:01:40.029
entering the house,

01:01:40.263 --> 01:01:42.765
found the victim just as Klein had described her,

01:01:43.633 --> 01:01:47.203
but he would soon find out that she had not been Klein's first victim.

01:01:50.540 --> 01:01:54.811
Klein related to me that he heard voices and the voices made him become angry.

01:01:55.578 --> 01:01:58.948
And every time he became angry he had to kill someone.

01:01:59.882 --> 01:02:00.983
And when he said that,

01:02:01.317 --> 01:02:05.088
it indicated to me that perhaps there were other victims also.

01:02:06.422 --> 01:02:10.293
And I asked him at that time if he had killed before,

01:02:10.793 --> 01:02:12.295
and he said yes twice.

01:02:15.064 --> 01:02:18.768
Klein had murdered one other woman in a trailer some months before.

01:02:19.569 --> 01:02:22.605
But his first victim had been a friend named Danny Webster,

01:02:23.039 --> 01:02:38.254
killed a year and a half earlier in August 1989.

01:02:38.254 --> 01:02:41.124
Luring Webster into a field to look for aluminum cans,

01:02:41.457 --> 01:02:43.726
Klein had beaten him to death with a lead pipe.

01:02:46.596 --> 01:02:50.600
A crime scene search unit scours the area where the killing allegedly occurred.

01:02:51.968 --> 01:02:52.268
There you go,

01:02:52.268 --> 01:02:54.137
I got something over here.

01:02:55.071 --> 01:02:55.304
Oh yeah?

01:02:55.304 --> 01:02:55.471
What is it?

01:02:55.471 --> 01:03:00.777
Is it a little bag that's been there a while?

01:03:05.648 --> 01:03:09.852
We had information that we believed at the time that the crime was committed,

01:03:10.520 --> 01:03:13.156
the original act of the homicide was committed in this field.

01:03:13.856 --> 01:03:14.390
So what we did,

01:03:14.390 --> 01:03:16.159
we did a grid search of this whole field,

01:03:16.726 --> 01:03:17.927
and upon doing that,

01:03:17.927 --> 01:03:20.696
we located a shoe.

01:03:21.397 --> 01:03:23.733
It's early to tell right now if it actually belongs to the victim,

01:03:23.766 --> 01:03:25.768
but we believe there's a good possibility that it does.

01:03:25.768 --> 01:03:26.736
Therefore,

01:03:26.836 --> 01:03:29.472
once we found the shoe,

01:03:29.472 --> 01:03:30.807
we have to treat it as evidence.

01:03:44.253 --> 01:03:45.621
According to Klein's confession,

01:03:45.888 --> 01:03:49.091
he had returned to the scene of the crime three days after the murder.

01:03:50.693 --> 01:03:52.662
Arriving at night to a detection,

01:03:52.962 --> 01:03:55.665
he swam with the body to a marshy swamp island,

01:03:58.000 --> 01:04:01.304
where he dismembered the corpse with an axe and a paring knife.

01:04:11.080 --> 01:04:15.885
After disposing of Webster's body so thoroughly and getting away with his crime for a year and a half,

01:04:16.319 --> 01:04:18.187
Klein has now decided to confess.

01:04:26.863 --> 01:04:30.032
The crime scene search officers find a small piece of bone,

01:04:31.534 --> 01:04:33.603
photographed exactly where it is found.

01:04:35.872 --> 01:04:37.273
But they will not find a body.

01:04:39.475 --> 01:04:40.109
Without a body,

01:04:40.443 --> 01:04:42.612
police do not have enough evidence to convict.

01:04:45.081 --> 01:04:48.484
Detective Jack Schell interrogated Klein under tight security.

01:04:49.185 --> 01:04:52.188
Klein had once bent a stop sign with his bare hands.

01:04:54.657 --> 01:04:56.926
We needed a positive identification of the body.

01:04:57.393 --> 01:04:59.929
Although we had somebody telling us who they killed and that they killed them,

01:05:00.863 --> 01:05:03.733
we still have to prove who was killed,

01:05:04.200 --> 01:05:04.967
how they were killed,

01:05:05.001 --> 01:05:07.069
and when they were killed to the best we can.

01:05:07.837 --> 01:05:09.272
It's the corpus delicti,

01:05:09.538 --> 01:05:10.406
the body of the crime.

01:05:11.240 --> 01:05:13.075
We have to be able to establish that yes,

01:05:13.476 --> 01:05:18.080
person died and that this person did it.

01:05:18.080 --> 01:05:19.882
I guess I must actually hit him about

01:05:20.316 --> 01:05:28.057
I don't know maybe more than 200 times I'm not sure I just kept whacking at everything I get in the legs arms everywhere I could get.

01:05:28.858 --> 01:05:36.432
This head region was more where I actually hit more anything but uh that was after he was already dead.

01:05:38.701 --> 01:05:43.539
Klein's confession by itself is not enough to prove the case beyond a reasonable doubt.

01:05:44.240 --> 01:05:45.975
The police must find the body.

01:05:50.212 --> 01:05:54.317
Klein pointed out the marshy island where he says he left the dismembered corpse.

01:05:57.920 --> 01:05:59.522
But even if police can find it,

01:05:59.755 --> 01:06:03.159
it will be hard to identify after a year and a half in the swamp.

01:06:08.431 --> 01:06:09.699
The case will go to Dr.

01:06:09.799 --> 01:06:12.068
William Maples of the University of Florida,

01:06:12.468 --> 01:06:16.038
a world-renowned authority on the identification of skeletal remains.

01:06:16.038 --> 01:06:21.377
In 1991,

01:06:21.711 --> 01:06:25.114
he led the team that exhumed the bones of President Zachary Taylor,

01:06:25.414 --> 01:06:27.650
who died mysteriously in 1850.

01:06:28.718 --> 01:06:30.886
Some historians say Taylor was poisoned,

01:06:31.320 --> 01:06:31.887
making him,

01:06:32.321 --> 01:06:33.389
not Abraham Lincoln,

01:06:33.689 --> 01:06:36.058
the first American president to be assassinated.

01:06:38.227 --> 01:06:41.530
After testing the dead president's hair and fingernails for poison,

01:06:42.031 --> 01:06:42.331
Dr.

01:06:42.331 --> 01:06:46.035
Maples laid the body and the assassin theories to rest.

01:06:48.704 --> 01:06:50.139
In the case of Paul Klein,

01:06:50.439 --> 01:06:56.345
it is immediately clear to Maples he is dealing with a possible psychopath totally unaffected by death.

01:06:57.880 --> 01:06:57.980
Well,

01:06:58.080 --> 01:07:01.784
bodies in Florida tend to bloat very rapidly.

01:07:01.884 --> 01:07:04.887
Florida is known for its sunshine and good weather,

01:07:05.187 --> 01:07:08.657
especially in the area of Fort Myers where this took place.

01:07:10.126 --> 01:07:11.894
So the first thing that our...

01:07:12.828 --> 01:07:16.632
killer attempted to use was a paring knife.

01:07:17.967 --> 01:07:19.368
It is very flexible,

01:07:19.735 --> 01:07:20.569
it is light,

01:07:20.669 --> 01:07:21.737
it may be sharp,

01:07:21.771 --> 01:07:23.773
it may be serrated,

01:07:24.106 --> 01:07:25.274
and may be very,

01:07:25.474 --> 01:07:28.444
very effective sometimes in cutting up bones.

01:07:28.944 --> 01:07:37.420
The so-called Ginsu steak knife is amazingly flexible and yet will go right through bone if it's properly used.

01:07:38.788 --> 01:07:39.522
So our killer...

01:07:40.289 --> 01:07:48.397
Sticks the knife into this bloated body and out pours all over him yellow and green discharge,

01:07:49.131 --> 01:07:51.167
foul-smelling fluid.

01:07:52.168 --> 01:07:55.104
This is enough to really disturb anyone,

01:07:55.171 --> 01:07:57.473
even if they weren't disturbed to start with.

01:07:59.175 --> 01:08:05.981
But the killer's use of a paring knife may be the key to identifying the victim's remains and corroborating Klein's confession.

01:08:06.816 --> 01:08:08.984
If we take a paring knife

01:08:09.752 --> 01:08:18.127
and rub along a stick or a bone or whatever the case might be,

01:08:18.727 --> 01:08:23.199
you notice that the blade jumps and chatters.

01:08:24.433 --> 01:08:31.240
This chatter produces an interrupted type of cutting on the bone surface.

01:08:31.640 --> 01:08:34.343
And we look for this evidence of chattering,

01:08:34.443 --> 01:08:38.681
and that tells us how flexible or inflexible the blade is.

01:08:39.582 --> 01:08:40.583
On the other hand,

01:08:41.250 --> 01:08:49.792
if we take a good heavy bladed sharp knife and cut on the bone,

01:08:50.359 --> 01:08:52.027
it doesn't chatter,

01:08:52.394 --> 01:08:54.263
it simply shaves the bone.

01:08:54.897 --> 01:08:57.666
And this is evidence of a heavier,

01:08:58.334 --> 01:09:00.569
less flexible blade.

01:09:08.878 --> 01:09:13.115
Crime scene search officers arrive at the island where Klein left the dismembered body.

01:09:19.788 --> 01:09:22.558
If they can find bones that were sliced with a paring knife,

01:09:22.825 --> 01:09:25.928
they'll have strong proof that Klein's confession is real.

01:09:30.900 --> 01:09:33.969
We're going to earn this one.

01:09:36.872 --> 01:09:39.275
Klein was to have accompanied the officers himself,

01:09:39.942 --> 01:09:41.744
but in the boat going over to the island,

01:09:41.810 --> 01:09:43.646
he'd been much too excited at the prospect of a new opportunity.

01:09:43.879 --> 01:09:45.714
of seeing his victim's remains.

01:09:47.349 --> 01:09:48.217
For their own safety,

01:09:48.551 --> 01:09:51.654
the officers decided to search without Klein's assistance.

01:09:54.190 --> 01:09:55.324
It's a serious mission,

01:09:56.025 --> 01:09:57.459
for unlike his other crimes,

01:09:57.726 --> 01:10:02.364
the murder of Danny Webster was vicious enough to send Klein to Florida's electric chair,

01:10:02.998 --> 01:10:08.370
or else prove beyond all doubt he's a dangerous psychopath to be put away indefinitely.

01:10:10.606 --> 01:10:11.740
He seemed like a normal person.

01:10:12.541 --> 01:10:16.412
You didn't notice any psychological problems or anything,

01:10:16.412 --> 01:10:19.481
but the more it went on and the more I realized he was telling me the truth,

01:10:19.949 --> 01:10:22.451
and that these gory facts he was telling me really did happen,

01:10:23.085 --> 01:10:24.086
then I'm beginning to think,

01:10:24.086 --> 01:10:24.353
you know,

01:10:24.386 --> 01:10:29.491
we've got a real weirdo here that can do this and talk about it so calmly and so intelligently.

01:10:32.127 --> 01:10:34.096
Klein had a nickname he didn't like.

01:10:36.332 --> 01:10:39.134
Because his arms extended out from his body as he walked,

01:10:39.401 --> 01:10:40.369
kids made fun of him,

01:10:40.736 --> 01:10:41.904
calling him Popeye.

01:10:45.975 --> 01:10:48.611
He'd killed Danny Webster for calling him names,

01:10:48.978 --> 01:10:52.681
repeating time after time it was because Webster had harassed him.

01:10:53.649 --> 01:10:54.350
How are you feeling?

01:10:55.417 --> 01:10:56.352
I'm feeling pretty good.

01:10:56.952 --> 01:10:58.220
Everything's all right.

01:10:58.687 --> 01:10:59.655
I have to go ahead and,

01:10:59.822 --> 01:11:00.189
I don't know,

01:11:00.189 --> 01:11:02.725
I think I feel almost a little dizzy for that matter.

01:11:02.725 --> 01:11:04.593
I need a little bit of something to take my medication.

01:11:04.793 --> 01:11:06.962
Is that person in the march,

01:11:06.962 --> 01:11:07.363
Danny Webster?

01:11:07.363 --> 01:11:07.463
Yes,

01:11:07.463 --> 01:11:07.563
ma'am.

01:11:07.796 --> 01:11:07.963
Yep,

01:11:08.063 --> 01:11:08.530
that's him.

01:11:09.231 --> 01:11:09.531
All right.

01:11:09.531 --> 01:11:09.965
That was him.

01:11:09.965 --> 01:11:12.401
Why did you kill him?

01:11:12.668 --> 01:11:14.336
He was always being cruel and,

01:11:14.336 --> 01:11:14.703
I don't know,

01:11:14.803 --> 01:11:15.938
calling me names and stuff.

01:11:15.938 --> 01:11:16.739
I decided to go for it.

01:11:16.739 --> 01:11:17.740
Watch your head,

01:11:17.740 --> 01:11:18.007
baby.

01:11:18.007 --> 01:11:18.440
Hey,

01:11:18.507 --> 01:11:18.807
thank you,

01:11:18.841 --> 01:11:19.108
ma'am.

01:11:19.208 --> 01:11:20.175
I appreciate everything.

01:11:29.718 --> 01:11:34.056
The spine left the body is partially submerged and subject to changing tides.

01:11:35.491 --> 01:11:38.460
The logistics of finding evidence are very difficult.

01:11:55.944 --> 01:11:56.345
Finally,

01:11:56.779 --> 01:11:58.113
more bones are found.

01:12:04.353 --> 01:12:05.688
The body is in pieces,

01:12:05.988 --> 01:12:07.356
just as Klein had said.

01:12:12.761 --> 01:12:13.162
I asked him,

01:12:13.195 --> 01:12:14.763
how did you take this body apart?

01:12:15.497 --> 01:12:17.299
I believe his response was just like I always do.

01:12:18.667 --> 01:12:19.668
I took off the left leg,

01:12:20.135 --> 01:12:20.736
the left arm,

01:12:21.937 --> 01:12:22.304
the head,

01:12:22.538 --> 01:12:23.072
the right arm,

01:12:23.205 --> 01:12:23.706
the right leg,

01:12:24.340 --> 01:12:25.607
in a circular motion like that.

01:12:27.209 --> 01:12:28.143
And he had used a hatchet,

01:12:29.378 --> 01:12:29.678
axe.

01:12:30.546 --> 01:12:31.847
He told me where the axe was,

01:12:32.548 --> 01:12:33.649
which was in another state.

01:12:35.017 --> 01:12:39.288
Him and his father had went to their home in another state.

01:12:41.290 --> 01:12:42.958
Left it there.

01:12:42.958 --> 01:12:47.296
The boy came back.

01:12:47.296 --> 01:12:51.867
Pieces of bone found on the Marshy Island are sent to the FBI for examination.

01:12:54.236 --> 01:12:57.873
The FBI confirms that the bones show evidence of hatchet trauma.

01:12:58.841 --> 01:13:01.944
An axe or hatchet had been used to cut the body apart.

01:13:02.911 --> 01:13:04.713
But according to his harrowing confession,

01:13:05.180 --> 01:13:07.983
Klein had reserved special treatment for the victim's head.

01:13:11.887 --> 01:13:12.121
Yup,

01:13:12.187 --> 01:13:16.091
got his head off with a parry knife and the head,

01:13:16.825 --> 01:13:25.734
it was more or less like in pieces and I tossed it right on an embankment about 25-30 feet from the mangroves and I stuck it there.

01:13:25.901 --> 01:13:26.502
for like,

01:13:26.502 --> 01:13:27.236
let's see,

01:13:27.236 --> 01:13:30.005
I had it there for like the longest time,

01:13:30.005 --> 01:13:32.574
I guess something like three and a half months.

01:13:34.443 --> 01:13:35.811
He told me that he would,

01:13:35.978 --> 01:13:39.281
he took the head and kept it to come back and visit it.

01:13:39.348 --> 01:13:40.816
He would visit it every night and talk to it.

01:13:42.050 --> 01:13:44.052
And after about two weeks,

01:13:44.052 --> 01:13:49.057
it got to the point where it was deteriorated to the extent that he didn't want it anymore.

01:13:49.258 --> 01:13:50.025
So he discarded it.

01:13:52.561 --> 01:13:55.497
Klein had carried the head around with him in a paper sack,

01:13:56.031 --> 01:13:57.900
often engaging it in conversation.

01:13:59.001 --> 01:14:00.836
Spotting a police car on one occasion,

01:14:01.136 --> 01:14:03.439
he panicked and threw the head into a waterway,

01:14:04.006 --> 01:14:05.607
but he could not remember where.

01:14:05.607 --> 01:14:06.775
you

01:14:07.676 --> 01:14:12.614
A police dive team searches the area where Klein may have discarded Danny Webster's skull.

01:14:22.925 --> 01:14:24.626
It is an area of murky waters,

01:14:25.427 --> 01:14:26.762
frequented by alligators.

01:14:30.699 --> 01:14:32.568
They find nothing but coconuts.

01:14:35.037 --> 01:14:36.371
Unless the head is found,

01:14:36.705 --> 01:14:40.442
it may be impossible to identify the remains as those of Danny Webster.

01:14:42.044 --> 01:14:42.344
Here,

01:14:42.711 --> 01:14:43.946
as in suburban Virginia,

01:14:44.379 --> 01:14:48.450
the police must team up with science to bring a killer to justice.

01:14:53.989 --> 01:14:56.325
At the Museum of Natural History in Washington,

01:14:56.358 --> 01:14:56.625
D.C.,

01:14:57.192 --> 01:15:00.028
tourists gaze at the wonders of the natural world.

01:15:01.230 --> 01:15:03.232
They are unaware that a few feet away,

01:15:04.099 --> 01:15:08.003
a storage area holds the unnatural work of a vicious killer.

01:15:11.773 --> 01:15:13.108
Police in Fairfax County,

01:15:13.208 --> 01:15:13.609
Virginia,

01:15:13.909 --> 01:15:19.314
have called on forensic scientist Doug Owsley to help identify the victim of a brutal homicide.

01:15:23.685 --> 01:15:25.220
The body has decomposed.

01:15:25.554 --> 01:15:27.089
Only a skeleton remains.

01:15:31.093 --> 01:15:35.864
Owsley has determined that the victim was a female from 27 to 34 years old.

01:15:36.532 --> 01:15:38.700
She died from repeated stab wounds.

01:15:39.735 --> 01:15:40.502
But who is she,

01:15:40.936 --> 01:15:42.170
and where did she come from?

01:15:44.506 --> 01:15:49.111
When you're working with police cases and working on problems of human identification,

01:15:49.244 --> 01:15:53.348
often when the remains come into the laboratory or when you're involved in the recovery,

01:15:53.515 --> 01:15:55.050
the identification follows very quickly.

01:15:55.117 --> 01:15:56.151
There's someone that's missing.

01:15:56.218 --> 01:15:58.387
There are records that can be obtained,

01:15:58.453 --> 01:15:59.955
dental records or medical records,

01:15:59.955 --> 01:16:04.393
that you can compare against and you can get that person identified within a very short period of time.

01:16:07.796 --> 01:16:12.034
But police in Fairfax County have found no immediate links to a missing person.

01:16:13.035 --> 01:16:17.606
Owsley will need every piece of evidence he can find to produce a life history of the victim,

01:16:18.073 --> 01:16:18.740
to be matched,

01:16:18.807 --> 01:16:19.241
hopefully,

01:16:19.608 --> 01:16:22.644
with someone who disappeared as many as six years before.

01:16:26.048 --> 01:16:28.517
Personal effects found with the victim are minimal.

01:16:31.887 --> 01:16:33.088
An inexpensive hair clip,

01:16:33.388 --> 01:16:34.056
a hair pick.

01:16:36.858 --> 01:16:38.427
Her blue jeans had rotted away.

01:16:38.994 --> 01:16:40.629
Her synthetic underwear remained.

01:16:44.399 --> 01:16:47.903
Lightweight sandals indicate that the crime was committed in warm weather.

01:16:49.871 --> 01:16:51.373
Her hair was a light brown color,

01:16:52.040 --> 01:16:56.511
as determined from analysis of hair found at the site.

01:16:56.945 --> 01:17:00.616
We know that her fingernails were painted from fingernails that were recovered at the site.

01:17:01.049 --> 01:17:04.453
It was a dark glossy pink in color.

01:17:04.553 --> 01:17:05.887
So there's a lot of details.

01:17:07.889 --> 01:17:11.193
On the earrings found near the body is a fragment of human tissue.

01:17:12.027 --> 01:17:15.197
The metal of the earring had protected it from bacterial decay.

01:17:16.665 --> 01:17:19.735
The earrings are included in sketches distributed nationwide.

01:17:21.236 --> 01:17:21.603
Slowly,

01:17:22.270 --> 01:17:22.771
painfully,

01:17:23.538 --> 01:17:26.575
the haunting details of the victim's life are coming into focus.

01:17:29.344 --> 01:17:31.446
In the spinal column are signs of trauma.

01:17:33.715 --> 01:17:38.186
Depressions in the vertebra are evidence that the discs between the vertebrae have herniated.

01:17:40.856 --> 01:17:43.592
The victim may have held a job that required heavy lifting.

01:17:46.561 --> 01:17:48.330
She'd once taken care of her teeth,

01:17:48.664 --> 01:17:51.366
but in the last years of her life had allowed them to decay.

01:17:52.334 --> 01:17:55.237
She had fillings on the front teeth to maintain her appearance,

01:17:55.570 --> 01:17:57.205
but five of her molars are missing.

01:17:58.140 --> 01:18:01.777
Black stains on the teeth are evidence the victim was probably a smoker.

01:18:04.846 --> 01:18:05.514
Overall,

01:18:06.314 --> 01:18:07.649
the impression that you would have,

01:18:07.683 --> 01:18:10.152
but it's an impression based not only on the dentition,

01:18:10.152 --> 01:18:12.387
but perhaps some of the things found with it,

01:18:13.088 --> 01:18:16.391
it would suggest that it was an individual that did not have a lot of money,

01:18:16.925 --> 01:18:20.162
that fairly limited financial resources.

01:18:29.905 --> 01:18:34.242
The information gleaned by Owsley is passed on to the Fairfax County Police Department,

01:18:34.543 --> 01:18:37.245
where Detective Bruce Guth heads the homicide branch.

01:18:39.281 --> 01:18:40.248
In our jurisdiction,

01:18:41.049 --> 01:18:45.854
many of the murders are committed by people who know the victim.

01:18:47.155 --> 01:18:48.023
In the normal case,

01:18:48.390 --> 01:18:49.524
once the victim is known,

01:18:49.825 --> 01:18:53.361
the police will talk to neighbors and relatives about the victim's lifestyle,

01:18:53.695 --> 01:18:54.563
who her friends were,

01:18:55.030 --> 01:18:55.964
and her enemies.

01:18:56.598 --> 01:18:59.301
A chain of evidence often will lead to the guilty party.

01:19:00.635 --> 01:19:01.103
In this case,

01:19:01.136 --> 01:19:02.070
we don't know the identity,

01:19:02.204 --> 01:19:08.477
so it makes it difficult to really go much further until we know who it is.

01:19:15.484 --> 01:19:16.752
Detail by detail,

01:19:17.185 --> 01:19:20.889
Fairfax County Police re-examine the evidence found at the scene.

01:19:24.493 --> 01:19:28.697
A sketch of what the victim may have looked like is distributed to police across the country,

01:19:29.131 --> 01:19:30.866
along with other details of the case.

01:19:33.802 --> 01:19:35.437
Even though this case is two years old,

01:19:35.637 --> 01:19:38.440
I'm still actively pursuing leads that have occurred.

01:19:39.074 --> 01:19:43.078
I receive inquiries approximately two to three a month.

01:19:43.712 --> 01:19:46.148
Just recently I received one from Philadelphia,

01:19:46.448 --> 01:19:47.115
New York City,

01:19:47.315 --> 01:19:47.983
and Ohio.

01:19:49.151 --> 01:19:52.020
Despite the continuing efforts of the Fairfax County Police,

01:19:52.320 --> 01:19:55.457
there is no matchup of the murder victim with a missing person.

01:19:58.860 --> 01:20:01.129
To facilitate the search for the victim's identity,

01:20:01.530 --> 01:20:07.068
Doug Owsley provides data for a second composite sketch using an FBI computer program.

01:20:09.738 --> 01:20:17.979
When you look at a skull and you're trying to assess what this individual looked like,

01:20:18.980 --> 01:20:21.449
the basic form is going to be defined by the skull itself.

01:20:23.151 --> 01:20:24.085
In relation to that,

01:20:25.120 --> 01:20:27.255
Then you take into consideration the clothing that's found,

01:20:27.255 --> 01:20:27.689
for instance,

01:20:27.689 --> 01:20:32.627
because the clothing helps you gain an idea as to the size,

01:20:32.661 --> 01:20:33.595
the weight of the individual,

01:20:33.628 --> 01:20:34.162
for instance.

01:20:36.198 --> 01:20:37.866
Starting with an image of the skull,

01:20:38.200 --> 01:20:40.669
the computer adds successive layers of detail.

01:20:42.671 --> 01:20:45.574
Markers indicate the probable thickness of facial tissue.

01:20:46.441 --> 01:20:50.278
The measurements are based on population studies of similar age and gender.

01:20:51.546 --> 01:20:52.781
Hair found at the scene,

01:20:53.081 --> 01:20:55.350
along with the plastic hair clip and hair pick,

01:20:55.717 --> 01:20:57.252
suggest a possible hairstyle.

01:21:00.689 --> 01:21:02.023
The victim had an overbite,

01:21:02.390 --> 01:21:03.758
a gap in her front teeth,

01:21:04.059 --> 01:21:05.093
and a cosmetic filling.

01:21:06.928 --> 01:21:08.964
Since these features would have been visible in life,

01:21:09.197 --> 01:21:11.800
the victim is shown smiling in the final illustration.

01:21:15.503 --> 01:21:18.607
But despite the pains taken to create a lifelike image,

01:21:18.907 --> 01:21:22.477
there is still no response when the image is published nationwide.

01:21:25.213 --> 01:21:26.014
Part of the problem,

01:21:26.481 --> 01:21:29.417
the police are still unsure when the murder occurred.

01:21:32.320 --> 01:21:34.055
When discovered in 1993,

01:21:34.456 --> 01:21:35.657
the bones had been dry.

01:21:36.224 --> 01:21:38.426
The flesh had long since rotted away.

01:21:40.028 --> 01:21:43.932
You'd have to say that it would be at least a year and a half before that.

01:21:45.500 --> 01:21:46.368
that this could have happened,

01:21:46.368 --> 01:21:47.102
but in reality,

01:21:47.469 --> 01:21:49.537
I think it could extend back further in time.

01:21:50.171 --> 01:21:53.842
And so if we take the maximum range,

01:21:54.476 --> 01:21:59.281
one of the things found in a pocket was a quarter that dates to 1980.

01:21:59.281 --> 01:22:01.016
So looking at the time frame,

01:22:01.049 --> 01:22:01.616
we're,

01:22:02.384 --> 01:22:03.652
in terms of the extremes,

01:22:03.985 --> 01:22:08.757
probably talking between 1991 and 1980.

01:22:09.291 --> 01:22:14.729
The problem of dating murder victims found long after the crime has occurred may soon have an answer.

01:22:15.697 --> 01:22:17.599
At a body farm in Tennessee,

01:22:17.966 --> 01:22:19.801
an unusual study is underway,

01:22:20.468 --> 01:22:23.805
using the volunteered flesh and bones of the dead.

01:22:27.142 --> 01:22:28.143
In Fairfax County,

01:22:28.209 --> 01:22:28.643
Virginia,

01:22:28.910 --> 01:22:33.848
police continue their search for clues to identify a woman found in the Washington suburbs.

01:22:36.217 --> 01:22:37.018
In Fort Myers,

01:22:37.085 --> 01:22:37.485
Florida,

01:22:37.786 --> 01:22:41.289
a confessed serial killer has led police to a headless body,

01:22:42.057 --> 01:22:43.892
barely identifiable as human.

01:22:45.193 --> 01:22:45.994
In both cases,

01:22:46.394 --> 01:22:49.297
work done at the Tennessee Anthropology Research Facility,

01:22:49.664 --> 01:22:50.231
or TARF,

01:22:50.598 --> 01:22:51.800
will prove invaluable.

01:22:52.701 --> 01:22:54.069
Its director is Dr.

01:22:54.135 --> 01:22:54.903
William Bass,

01:22:55.236 --> 01:22:58.673
a forensic anthropologist at the University of Tennessee in Knoxville.

01:23:01.743 --> 01:23:03.878
His expertise is the human skeleton.

01:23:04.579 --> 01:23:06.915
The individual had been dead about 10 days,

01:23:06.915 --> 01:23:07.582
found out on the edge of the railroad.

01:23:11.319 --> 01:23:12.854
When a person is recently dead,

01:23:13.254 --> 01:23:18.059
morphological features such as prints on the fingers and palms can help with identification.

01:23:19.761 --> 01:23:22.597
When these features are gone and only the bones remain,

01:23:22.964 --> 01:23:25.266
the case is one for forensic anthropology.

01:23:27.602 --> 01:23:31.906
The diagnosis facility receives on average one body a week for identification.

01:23:34.843 --> 01:23:38.313
Parts of Tennessee have become a dumping ground for murder victims.

01:23:40.148 --> 01:23:42.550
We don't think of the interstates as being avenues of crime,

01:23:42.550 --> 01:23:44.586
but they're absolute avenues of crime.

01:23:45.487 --> 01:23:45.954
So you can...

01:23:47.489 --> 01:23:49.691
Kidnap somebody in Chicago can come down

01:23:50.091 --> 01:23:52.794
I-75 which comes down through Cincinnati through Lexington,

01:23:52.794 --> 01:23:54.029
Kentucky into Tennessee

01:23:54.696 --> 01:23:59.034
The first real area that you get to on I-75 going toward Florida

01:23:59.834 --> 01:24:04.873
That is really rural is Tennessee and we get a lot of bodies thrown out there

01:24:07.375 --> 01:24:11.679
With so many bodies bass has invented a boxing system for storage

01:24:14.082 --> 01:24:15.784
Every skeleton gets his own box.

01:24:17.085 --> 01:24:19.421
You want to box them because if you don't,

01:24:19.521 --> 01:24:20.588
they will get lost,

01:24:20.655 --> 01:24:21.489
they will get dirty.

01:24:22.757 --> 01:24:29.531
On the shelves surrounding him are over 2,000 skeletons sent to Bass's facility by medical examiners around the state.

01:24:31.833 --> 01:24:33.368
With the help of graduate students,

01:24:33.735 --> 01:24:36.137
Bass assists the police in determining the sex,

01:24:36.604 --> 01:24:36.871
race,

01:24:37.072 --> 01:24:40.175
and other identifying characteristics of unclaimed bodies.

01:24:42.210 --> 01:24:43.178
But in doing his work,

01:24:43.545 --> 01:24:48.550
Bass became acutely aware there were no reliable statistics on rates of human decay.

01:24:54.022 --> 01:24:59.360
He established near Knoxville an outdoor preserve known informally as the Body Farm.

01:25:00.528 --> 01:25:00.795
Here,

01:25:01.029 --> 01:25:02.997
he has planted not living things,

01:25:03.231 --> 01:25:03.765
but dead.

01:25:11.272 --> 01:25:14.209
Human bodies left in the open to decompose.

01:25:18.446 --> 01:25:23.084
It's an experiment that will tell him how bodily decomposition is affected by weather,

01:25:23.485 --> 01:25:23.885
climate,

01:25:24.219 --> 01:25:26.287
and the degree of exposure to the elements.

01:25:29.157 --> 01:25:33.728
This is a body that we're studying the effects of.

01:25:34.963 --> 01:25:37.465
The covering of the body that the maggots leave.

01:25:38.566 --> 01:25:40.802
This body has been outside for a year.

01:25:41.536 --> 01:25:43.638
Maggots have eaten its inside away,

01:25:44.072 --> 01:25:46.174
but left some of the skin for protection.

01:25:48.109 --> 01:25:57.051
Maggots don't like sunlight and what they do is they leave this as an umbrella to protect themselves from the sun.

01:25:57.719 --> 01:26:08.429
And so you get bodies like this that will have the covering on them holding the bones together although there are no internal organs left there at all.

01:26:12.734 --> 01:26:16.738
The specimens used for Bass's experiments come from two basic sources.

01:26:17.405 --> 01:26:24.379
Those who have donated their bodies to science and unclaimed bodies sent to the facility by medical examiners.

01:26:27.682 --> 01:26:30.318
After the bodies have lain in the open to decompose,

01:26:30.518 --> 01:26:32.921
they are brought back to the laboratory for analysis.

01:26:35.823 --> 01:26:39.727
Dissection will tell how their exposure to the elements has affected their skin,

01:26:40.195 --> 01:26:41.262
bones and tissue.

01:26:43.164 --> 01:26:50.205
These statistics will then aid police departments in determining how long a newly discovered homicide victim has been dead.

01:26:53.641 --> 01:26:56.277
To keep out intruders with morbid curiosities,

01:26:56.678 --> 01:26:59.948
security guards keep the body farm under tight surveillance.

01:27:01.416 --> 01:27:06.955
Bass has attempted to duplicate all the common ways in which killers dispose of their victims.

01:27:09.157 --> 01:27:09.357
Well,

01:27:09.524 --> 01:27:14.295
we try to keep as honest a setting as we can.

01:27:15.563 --> 01:27:17.632
We try not to make anything artificial.

01:27:17.732 --> 01:27:19.734
It's exactly the way it is in nature.

01:27:20.401 --> 01:27:26.841
What we have here is an automobile that we have been looking at the decay rates of people in automobiles,

01:27:26.874 --> 01:27:28.977
both in the passenger compartment and in the trunk.

01:27:31.079 --> 01:27:34.882
A body placed in the trunk for observation has since been removed,

01:27:35.416 --> 01:27:36.951
but its byproducts remain.

01:27:39.687 --> 01:27:42.557
Tiny pupil cases from which flies have emerged.

01:27:43.491 --> 01:27:45.660
A matter of minutes after death has occurred,

01:27:45.994 --> 01:27:48.596
flies are attracted to the body to lay their eggs.

01:27:49.497 --> 01:27:51.099
The eggs will hatch into larvae,

01:27:51.466 --> 01:27:52.066
or maggots,

01:27:52.367 --> 01:27:55.837
that feed on the decaying flesh before turning into flies.

01:27:57.739 --> 01:28:06.614
We can tell you that this individual has been in this trunk at least 21 days from the fly pupae that are present,

01:28:06.648 --> 01:28:09.450
from the pupal cases that are present here.

01:28:11.219 --> 01:28:12.520
In the back seat of the car,

01:28:12.687 --> 01:28:14.022
another body has been removed,

01:28:14.422 --> 01:28:16.691
again leaving a clue as to time of death.

01:28:17.792 --> 01:28:18.326
Hair mass.

01:28:20.361 --> 01:28:24.365
What's called the hair mass falls off a dead body after a week's time.

01:28:25.733 --> 01:28:27.902
Bass has found that in enclosed vehicles,

01:28:28.169 --> 01:28:31.739
the buildup of heat often accelerates the process of decomposition.

01:28:37.779 --> 01:28:39.847
Some of the bodies have been dead only a week.

01:28:41.215 --> 01:28:41.416
Well,

01:28:41.416 --> 01:28:42.483
he hasn't gone very far.

01:28:45.253 --> 01:28:45.420
Well...

01:28:49.691 --> 01:28:49.891
Well,

01:28:49.891 --> 01:28:51.392
they're both at about the same stage,

01:28:51.426 --> 01:28:51.726
aren't they?

01:28:55.897 --> 01:28:56.464
On that body.

01:28:56.764 --> 01:28:58.066
Others are much older.

01:28:59.267 --> 01:29:02.103
Well there are some maggots right there,

01:29:02.170 --> 01:29:03.504
but that maggot is frozen.

01:29:04.605 --> 01:29:09.110
He's gotten away from the warmth of the body and didn't make it back to the body.

01:29:09.210 --> 01:29:11.279
So there are a couple right there.

01:29:12.213 --> 01:29:14.682
I'm going to push this back just a little bit more here.

01:29:15.616 --> 01:29:18.052
And we'll see if we can't get under there.

01:29:18.986 --> 01:29:19.120
And

01:29:20.421 --> 01:29:21.522
I'm not really sure.

01:29:24.092 --> 01:29:24.258
Well,

01:29:24.258 --> 01:29:25.493
you see there are lots of maggots.

01:29:25.493 --> 01:29:25.626
See,

01:29:25.626 --> 01:29:28.696
there are hundreds and hundreds of maggots in there.

01:29:29.297 --> 01:29:31.032
And they are either slowed down,

01:29:31.699 --> 01:29:33.000
they're not doing much right now,

01:29:33.735 --> 01:29:34.869
because it's so cold.

01:29:43.277 --> 01:29:43.745
Each month,

01:29:44.078 --> 01:29:47.715
Bass sends bone and hair samples to the FBI for analysis.

01:29:51.486 --> 01:29:56.290
The samples come from a number of different specimens in a variety of locales within the farm.

01:29:59.694 --> 01:30:01.596
The FBI will study how time,

01:30:01.996 --> 01:30:03.030
exposure to the elements,

01:30:03.297 --> 01:30:06.934
and the environment in which a body is found may alter its DNA.

01:30:09.570 --> 01:30:15.243
You can do DNA analysis on the maggots and they will produce the same DNA.

01:30:15.810 --> 01:30:19.414
If the individual has been on drugs,

01:30:19.547 --> 01:30:21.015
the maggots will pick up those drugs.

01:30:21.015 --> 01:30:22.784
You can tell from analysis of the maggot.

01:30:23.751 --> 01:30:28.423
Also the volatile fatty acids which are that the goo that you see there,

01:30:28.990 --> 01:30:32.126
those are what are called the volatile fatty acids that leach out of bodies.

01:30:32.960 --> 01:30:34.562
Now we've been able to take

01:30:34.595 --> 01:30:41.002
Take this material and analyze that and determine the length of time since death up to about two years.

01:30:43.204 --> 01:30:44.505
In other parts of the facility,

01:30:44.906 --> 01:30:47.141
bodies lie in coffins above ground.

01:30:48.810 --> 01:30:53.648
Drainage tubes allow the testing of fluids and air samples without opening the coffin lid.

01:30:57.084 --> 01:30:57.585
Nearby,

01:30:57.952 --> 01:31:00.755
bodies are buried in coffins six feet underground,

01:31:01.022 --> 01:31:02.690
with a culvert allowing access.

01:31:03.291 --> 01:31:03.558
Here,

01:31:03.891 --> 01:31:08.196
Bass and his associates study how subterranean burial affects the rate of decay.

01:31:10.731 --> 01:31:14.168
Since killers have been known to dismember their victims and scatter them around,

01:31:14.535 --> 01:31:17.038
another area is set aside for body parts.

01:31:20.842 --> 01:31:23.044
It is not always an exact science,

01:31:23.744 --> 01:31:24.245
but Dr.

01:31:24.312 --> 01:31:25.012
Bass's work,

01:31:25.313 --> 01:31:26.380
the first of its kind,

01:31:26.747 --> 01:31:30.184
will help the police with murder victims found long after the crime.

01:31:36.157 --> 01:31:38.192
In the case of Danny Webster in Florida,

01:31:38.559 --> 01:31:40.127
police have only part of the body.

01:31:42.096 --> 01:31:45.967
The killer has done a thorough job of dismembering and scattering the remains.

01:31:47.268 --> 01:31:52.807
Filling in the grisly details of murder and mutilation will be the job of forensic science.

01:31:56.611 --> 01:31:59.146
At the Lee County Sheriff's Office in Fort Myers,

01:31:59.213 --> 01:31:59.614
Florida,

01:31:59.914 --> 01:32:03.017
Paul Klein has confessed to the murder of Danny Webster.

01:32:03.818 --> 01:32:07.054
But the killer has carved up the body and disposed of its head.

01:32:07.955 --> 01:32:11.726
The few bones found on a marshy island are a year and a half old.

01:32:12.560 --> 01:32:17.665
It may be impossible to prove beyond a reasonable doubt they are the bones of Danny Webster.

01:32:20.701 --> 01:32:23.971
Klein had been relentless in attempting to conceal his crime.

01:32:24.639 --> 01:32:26.107
Before decapitating the body,

01:32:26.374 --> 01:32:27.642
he had pulled out the teeth,

01:32:28.109 --> 01:32:30.678
knowing they're often used to identify the victim.

01:32:33.047 --> 01:32:36.951
He explained that instead of digging a normal grave-type hole,

01:32:37.685 --> 01:32:41.789
that he dug a round hole as if to put trash or something in it.

01:32:42.356 --> 01:32:43.691
He set the victim down in it,

01:32:44.325 --> 01:32:45.092
buttocks first,

01:32:45.593 --> 01:32:47.562
and shoved him into the hole by his shoulders.

01:32:48.296 --> 01:32:49.497
When he didn't go in all the way,

01:32:49.931 --> 01:32:55.202
he jumped up and down on the body until he was able to get it deep enough into the hole that he thought he had it hidden.

01:32:55.736 --> 01:33:04.712
Then he pulled weeds and dirt over it and laid on his belly and his words were slithered back into the water like a big gator and swam back home.

01:33:06.414 --> 01:33:07.848
Is Klein a psychopath,

01:33:08.449 --> 01:33:12.053
or is he a man who has plotted and planned the perfect crime?

01:33:13.254 --> 01:33:13.454
Okay,

01:33:13.688 --> 01:33:14.689
a perfect crime.

01:33:15.323 --> 01:33:15.690
Let's think.

01:33:15.790 --> 01:33:16.624
How about Jimmy Hoffa?

01:33:17.425 --> 01:33:19.193
How far have we gotten on Jimmy Hoffa solving?

01:33:19.327 --> 01:33:20.528
We don't even know where he is.

01:33:21.262 --> 01:33:23.364
How can we solve the crime if we don't know where he is?

01:33:24.065 --> 01:33:24.365
You see,

01:33:24.398 --> 01:33:25.833
so somebody,

01:33:26.300 --> 01:33:26.534
I mean,

01:33:26.534 --> 01:33:27.635
somebody's already thought of this.

01:33:27.635 --> 01:33:27.902
They said,

01:33:27.902 --> 01:33:28.169
you know,

01:33:28.369 --> 01:33:29.170
if you leave the body,

01:33:29.604 --> 01:33:30.871
there's a good chance you're going to be caught.

01:33:31.973 --> 01:33:36.143
Because there's all these hair and fiber sections of the FBI and the forensic anthropologist.

01:33:36.143 --> 01:33:36.544
There's a lot of police,

01:33:50.358 --> 01:33:54.295
It was soon apparent Klein may have learned the techniques of killing from books.

01:33:55.830 --> 01:34:02.336
We found probably every true life murder story that was ever written,

01:34:03.571 --> 01:34:04.772
like Son of Sam,

01:34:05.439 --> 01:34:06.674
The Hillside Strangler.

01:34:06.674 --> 01:34:08.943
It was just book after book after book.

01:34:11.479 --> 01:34:12.747
From sparse remains,

01:34:13.047 --> 01:34:16.150
forensic science must identify the body of Danny Webster.

01:34:17.184 --> 01:34:17.652
If not,

01:34:18.052 --> 01:34:19.487
the case may be lost.

01:34:22.356 --> 01:34:26.160
In order to corroborate Klein's confession and make the case hold up in court,

01:34:26.560 --> 01:34:29.730
anthropologist William Maples will try to determine the age,

01:34:30.064 --> 01:34:30.297
sex,

01:34:30.598 --> 01:34:32.400
and race of the partial skeleton.

01:34:35.603 --> 01:34:37.505
This is the shaft of the bone.

01:34:37.938 --> 01:34:39.540
This is the epiphysis,

01:34:39.573 --> 01:34:40.541
the end of the bone.

01:34:41.008 --> 01:34:42.276
And they start...

01:34:43.210 --> 01:34:46.781
from separate origins in a child.

01:34:48.783 --> 01:34:51.452
And they slowly change shape.

01:34:52.953 --> 01:34:55.389
As the epiphyses reach the end of their growth,

01:34:55.690 --> 01:34:58.025
they become fused to the shafts of bone.

01:34:58.859 --> 01:35:00.094
On the bones in this case,

01:35:00.428 --> 01:35:01.896
fusion is not yet complete.

01:35:02.963 --> 01:35:07.001
Maples is able to put the age of the victim between 17 and 23,

01:35:07.468 --> 01:35:09.170
the same age as Danny Webster.

01:35:13.007 --> 01:35:16.811
Determining sex is almost always a matter of studying the pelvic bones,

01:35:17.178 --> 01:35:19.146
narrower in males than females.

01:35:20.114 --> 01:35:22.416
The victim in this case was clearly a male.

01:35:23.417 --> 01:35:25.453
But determining race will be more difficult,

01:35:25.853 --> 01:35:27.288
especially with the head missing.

01:35:30.324 --> 01:35:30.591
Dr.

01:35:30.624 --> 01:35:31.892
Maples uses the femur,

01:35:32.059 --> 01:35:32.793
or thigh bone,

01:35:33.027 --> 01:35:34.895
to distinguish Caucasian and black.

01:35:35.863 --> 01:35:36.630
In a Caucasian,

01:35:36.931 --> 01:35:40.201
the femur is bowed enough to allow his knuckle to pass under.

01:35:42.069 --> 01:35:46.340
In the case of a black individual,

01:35:47.108 --> 01:35:53.347
the shaft tends to be much flatter and straighter and there is no anterior bowing.

01:35:54.515 --> 01:36:02.490
The amount of curvature in the femur clearly indicated to me that we were dealing with a white male.

01:36:04.825 --> 01:36:05.226
Finally,

01:36:05.559 --> 01:36:11.732
Maples examines the bones for evidence of the chatter marks that a paring knife would leave if used to dismember the body.

01:36:14.135 --> 01:36:14.568
Killers,

01:36:15.069 --> 01:36:15.736
by and large,

01:36:15.770 --> 01:36:18.305
aren't stupid enough to use paring knives.

01:36:19.406 --> 01:36:21.075
So we don't have a lot of evidence of that.

01:36:22.376 --> 01:36:24.145
But the results are positive.

01:36:24.812 --> 01:36:26.313
By the end of his examination,

01:36:26.614 --> 01:36:31.585
Maples has conclusive proof that the bones match the description of Danny Webster.

01:36:32.219 --> 01:36:35.956
The necessary corroboration of Klein's confession is in place.

01:36:36.557 --> 01:36:37.825
For Lieutenant Jeff Taylor,

01:36:38.225 --> 01:36:39.527
the case is closed.

01:36:41.328 --> 01:36:45.833
Paul Klein is presently in an institution in Chattahoochee,

01:36:45.866 --> 01:36:50.204
Florida for mentally insane.

01:36:55.709 --> 01:36:56.844
But in Fairfax County,

01:36:56.944 --> 01:36:57.378
Virginia,

01:36:57.745 --> 01:36:59.113
police have a tougher problem.

01:37:01.248 --> 01:37:03.450
A murder victim remains unidentified.

01:37:03.951 --> 01:37:05.753
A killer may go undetected,

01:37:06.253 --> 01:37:07.588
free to kill again.

01:37:13.427 --> 01:37:19.166
More than two years have passed since the discovery of a female body in the Virginia countryside near Washington,

01:37:19.233 --> 01:37:19.466
D.C.

01:37:22.236 --> 01:37:23.838
Fairfax County investigators,

01:37:24.004 --> 01:37:26.841
including detectives Jerry Farrell and Dennis Wilson,

01:37:27.241 --> 01:37:29.476
have sent out extensive information on the case,

01:37:30.377 --> 01:37:31.912
including descriptions of the victim,

01:37:32.546 --> 01:37:33.180
dental charts,

01:37:33.747 --> 01:37:34.949
and a description of some,

01:37:35.182 --> 01:37:35.950
but not all,

01:37:35.983 --> 01:37:39.220
of the wounds discovered by forensic expert Doug Owsley.

01:37:41.388 --> 01:37:44.391
Persons have been known to confess crimes they did not commit,

01:37:44.692 --> 01:37:47.494
having learned the details of the crime from public sources.

01:37:48.963 --> 01:37:50.664
By withholding certain information,

01:37:50.965 --> 01:37:55.669
the police can be sure when a confession is fabricated or genuine.

01:37:59.573 --> 01:38:00.908
Far from having a confession,

01:38:01.342 --> 01:38:02.643
police in Fairfax County,

01:38:03.010 --> 01:38:05.546
after two years of searching missing person files,

01:38:05.946 --> 01:38:07.948
still do not know who the victim is.

01:38:10.618 --> 01:38:17.958
Even today we think a lot about her and are hoping that through the facial reproductions that have been done that someone might be able to recognize her,

01:38:18.058 --> 01:38:25.399
might be able to contact the police and offer new insights because this is somebody that we need to get identified and we certainly need to in order to...

01:38:25.566 --> 01:38:27.201
hopefully prevent this from happening again,

01:38:27.735 --> 01:38:28.602
find out who did this.

01:38:29.203 --> 01:38:31.205
We've sent hundreds of leads out,

01:38:31.305 --> 01:38:32.306
hundreds of posters.

01:38:33.207 --> 01:38:36.277
We're going to probably revise this again at least once or twice a year.

01:38:36.343 --> 01:38:38.145
We try to cover it on the TV stations,

01:38:38.145 --> 01:38:38.679
the media.

01:38:39.980 --> 01:38:43.484
We try to get the newspapers interested to run the picture.

01:38:43.984 --> 01:38:46.120
We run not only the artist's renditions,

01:38:46.186 --> 01:38:48.289
but we also run pictures of the clothing.

01:38:49.189 --> 01:38:52.493
This is no way a case that's just sitting on a shelf and nothing being done.

01:38:55.729 --> 01:38:58.999
With a growing population and more and more people on the move,

01:38:59.300 --> 01:39:01.302
many crimes may never be solved.

01:39:02.269 --> 01:39:04.605
Victims like the one found in Fairfax County,

01:39:05.039 --> 01:39:07.308
her friends and relatives unaware of her death,

01:39:07.675 --> 01:39:09.209
may remain unidentified,

01:39:09.476 --> 01:39:11.345
her killer free to kill again.

01:39:13.247 --> 01:39:13.847
I think that...

01:39:15.616 --> 01:39:18.252
Homicide investigations are getting a little more difficult.

01:39:19.186 --> 01:39:22.122
The national trend is more stranger-on-stranger murders.

01:39:22.823 --> 01:39:23.691
As in past years,

01:39:23.691 --> 01:39:25.059
it was family members,

01:39:25.125 --> 01:39:26.293
domestic kind of murders.

01:39:27.494 --> 01:39:27.928
Nationally,

01:39:27.962 --> 01:39:30.064
there's more stranger-stranger murders.

01:39:30.798 --> 01:39:35.569
And forensics becomes a very important part of the investigation.

01:39:37.204 --> 01:39:39.006
Was this the work of a serial killer?

01:39:39.940 --> 01:39:41.342
Doug Owsley believes not.

01:39:42.209 --> 01:39:43.811
The body was buried in haste,

01:39:44.178 --> 01:39:47.815
the killer lacking the tools to dismember it so it would never be found.

01:39:49.350 --> 01:39:53.520
Serial killers like Florida's Paul Klein are far more efficient.

01:39:53.620 --> 01:39:56.757
You don't.

01:39:58.192 --> 01:40:01.762
Gain necessarily that same sort of sophistication in this case.

01:40:02.529 --> 01:40:04.832
Certainly it led to a tragic end,

01:40:04.932 --> 01:40:08.535
but in terms of accomplishing what he set out to do,

01:40:09.136 --> 01:40:10.270
he was much less prepared.

01:40:10.971 --> 01:40:11.672
On the other hand,

01:40:14.108 --> 01:40:18.312
he was able to take someone's life and he's been able to get away with it all these years.

01:40:18.379 --> 01:40:24.952
And so there's just some piece that we haven't been able to put together to get on his trail yet.

01:40:26.720 --> 01:40:28.922
Despite the obstacles to solving the case,

01:40:29.289 --> 01:40:31.992
detectives in Fairfax County have not given up.

01:40:34.862 --> 01:40:36.830
Somebody sooner or later is going to talk about this.

01:40:37.364 --> 01:40:39.400
That's one of the premises of the Cold Case Squad,

01:40:39.433 --> 01:40:41.402
that relationships are changing.

01:40:42.503 --> 01:40:43.670
Technology is changing,

01:40:43.737 --> 01:40:45.105
things are becoming more advanced.

01:40:46.440 --> 01:40:48.609
I'm optimistic that this case could get solved.

01:40:49.510 --> 01:40:50.911
We keep working at it,

01:40:50.944 --> 01:40:51.812
chipping away at it,

01:40:51.812 --> 01:40:53.847
eventually it's going to unfold.

01:40:55.115 --> 01:40:55.449
Oh yeah,

01:40:56.016 --> 01:40:58.218
I'm optimistic we're going to do it.

01:40:59.186 --> 01:41:00.854
Sometimes things just take a little bit longer,

01:41:00.888 --> 01:41:01.288
that's all.

01:41:05.492 --> 01:41:06.360
William Maples,

01:41:06.393 --> 01:41:06.894
for one,

01:41:07.261 --> 01:41:09.897
believes that few killers escape detection.

01:41:12.166 --> 01:41:12.299
No,

01:41:13.133 --> 01:41:13.267
no,

01:41:13.300 --> 01:41:13.934
of course not.

01:41:15.269 --> 01:41:16.637
They don't play by our rules.

01:41:19.807 --> 01:41:21.141
They will take chances,

01:41:21.975 --> 01:41:24.445
do things that we wouldn't do.

01:41:26.513 --> 01:41:27.848
They just don't play by the rules.

01:41:29.249 --> 01:41:34.588
And that's helpful to us because that means that they make a lot of mistakes.

01:41:35.389 --> 01:41:36.757
And by taking chances,

01:41:37.091 --> 01:41:40.427
they invariably end up getting caught.

01:41:41.728 --> 01:41:43.263
Thrill is a big element in that.

01:41:43.597 --> 01:41:46.100
And thrill means that they're taking chances.

01:41:47.301 --> 01:41:47.701
And

01:41:49.203 --> 01:41:49.803
God love them.

01:41:50.871 --> 01:41:52.072
The more chances they take,

01:41:52.072 --> 01:41:53.841
the more chances we have of catching them.

01:41:57.144 --> 01:41:58.145
In Washington State,

01:41:58.479 --> 01:42:00.681
two people die agonizing deaths.

01:42:02.950 --> 01:42:05.953
Both are victims of poison-laced Excedrin capsules.

01:42:07.121 --> 01:42:12.025
The news causes public panic and costs manufacturers tens of millions of dollars.

01:42:14.795 --> 01:42:15.362
In Virginia,

01:42:15.696 --> 01:42:19.466
a cardiac care unit experiences an epidemic of unexpected deaths.

01:42:20.300 --> 01:42:22.803
Is this bad care or bad luck?

01:42:23.971 --> 01:42:25.639
Or is it something more sinister?

01:42:27.975 --> 01:42:31.044
And a man tries to murder his wife in an insurance fraud.

01:42:31.678 --> 01:42:32.079
Your emergency.

01:42:32.079 --> 01:42:33.180
I've got a medical emergency.

01:42:33.180 --> 01:42:36.416
My wife is going through some kind of convulsion.

01:42:36.950 --> 01:42:37.484
And she's now,

01:42:37.584 --> 01:42:38.519
she's,

01:42:38.519 --> 01:42:39.419
her mouth is foaming.

01:42:42.589 --> 01:42:45.058
Poisoners are among the most elusive killers.

01:42:45.759 --> 01:42:46.093
Today,

01:42:46.460 --> 01:42:50.864
forensic detectives are employing an arsenal of techniques to uncover and convict them.

01:42:53.000 --> 01:42:54.468
Who is winning the war?

01:43:19.693 --> 01:43:20.627
How's Miss Thomas doing?

01:43:20.627 --> 01:43:22.362
She's doing great.

01:43:22.362 --> 01:43:24.264
We take her chart and move her down to check on her.

01:43:25.532 --> 01:43:27.834
It is 9 o'clock on a Saturday night when Dr.

01:43:27.901 --> 01:43:29.937
Joseph Quiddle makes his final rounds.

01:43:32.506 --> 01:43:36.577
Whittle is co-director of the coronary care unit at General Hospital in Petersburg,

01:43:36.643 --> 01:43:37.044
Virginia.

01:43:39.680 --> 01:43:40.214
I'm Thomas.

01:43:40.214 --> 01:43:41.281
I'm Dr.

01:43:41.348 --> 01:43:41.582
Whittle.

01:43:41.748 --> 01:43:43.317
He has been on duty since noon.

01:43:43.750 --> 01:43:44.751
It is time to go home.

01:43:45.752 --> 01:43:47.788
Among his patients is Josephine Thomas,

01:43:48.088 --> 01:43:48.188
a

01:43:48.822 --> 01:43:50.057
73-year-old retired teacher.

01:43:50.857 --> 01:43:55.195
She was admitted with heart problems several hours earlier and now is resting comfortably.

01:43:57.531 --> 01:43:58.699
At around 11 p.m.,

01:43:59.132 --> 01:44:00.167
Whittle orders an I.V.

01:44:17.818 --> 01:44:18.385
Miss Thomas?

01:44:18.652 --> 01:44:19.253
Miss Thomas?

01:44:24.224 --> 01:44:26.226
The duty nurse signals a code blue,

01:44:26.493 --> 01:44:27.494
a patient emergency,

01:44:27.828 --> 01:44:28.795
and calls in Dr.

01:44:28.829 --> 01:44:29.096
Whittle.

01:44:30.897 --> 01:44:32.733
Josephine Thomas is in crisis.

01:44:36.303 --> 01:44:38.772
It takes Whittle just 15 minutes to return.

01:44:45.779 --> 01:44:46.046
Everyone clear?

01:44:46.046 --> 01:44:48.415
Stopping.

01:44:48.415 --> 01:44:48.915
Too late.

01:44:49.516 --> 01:44:51.718
Josephine Thomas cannot be revived.

01:44:57.457 --> 01:44:59.026
The death troubles the staff.

01:44:59.493 --> 01:45:03.964
An epidemic of unexpected deaths has struck the cardiac care unit in the past few weeks.

01:45:05.666 --> 01:45:10.103
Two nurses suspect that Josephine Thomas may not have died of natural causes.

01:45:11.972 --> 01:45:14.274
Their suspicions deepen an hour and a half later,

01:45:14.975 --> 01:45:17.244
when a package of lidocaine is found to be missing.

01:45:18.879 --> 01:45:19.012
Okay,

01:45:19.046 --> 01:45:19.513
lidocaine,

01:45:19.546 --> 01:45:20.013
two gram?

01:45:21.014 --> 01:45:21.148
No.

01:45:22.949 --> 01:45:23.850
You don't find any two gram?

01:45:24.451 --> 01:45:25.152
we didn't use a two gram.

01:45:25.152 --> 01:45:25.352
All right,

01:45:26.453 --> 01:45:26.553
well,

01:45:26.553 --> 01:45:27.888
we should have one two gram lidocaine in.

01:45:27.888 --> 01:45:29.156
I don't have one.

01:45:29.156 --> 01:45:31.758
Lidocaine is a drug given to stabilize heartbeat.

01:45:32.693 --> 01:45:33.960
In large concentrations,

01:45:34.061 --> 01:45:34.928
it can be fatal.

01:45:39.199 --> 01:45:41.668
Whenever a death seems unusual or suspicious,

01:45:42.002 --> 01:45:46.406
medical and law enforcement personnel are required to contact the medical examiner's office.

01:45:47.507 --> 01:45:47.641
Hi,

01:45:47.708 --> 01:45:47.841
Dr.

01:45:47.908 --> 01:45:48.175
Fierro.

01:45:48.241 --> 01:45:48.542
How are you?

01:45:48.542 --> 01:45:48.942
Hello,

01:45:48.942 --> 01:45:49.009
Jim.

01:45:49.009 --> 01:45:49.309
How are you?

01:45:49.543 --> 01:45:49.776
Dr.

01:45:49.810 --> 01:45:53.180
Marcela Fierro is chief medical examiner for the state of Virginia.

01:45:54.081 --> 01:45:56.283
In her first year as an assistant medical examiner,

01:45:56.616 --> 01:45:58.352
she worked on the Petersburg case.

01:45:59.653 --> 01:46:03.056
The problem arose when it became apparent that there was,

01:46:03.223 --> 01:46:03.390
one,

01:46:03.457 --> 01:46:07.761
an increased number of deaths in the unit as compared to previous months,

01:46:08.428 --> 01:46:09.129
and secondly,

01:46:09.730 --> 01:46:15.102
that a significant number of them were occurring in the presence of or were being discovered by

01:46:15.469 --> 01:46:16.470
Leroy Hargrave.

01:46:19.940 --> 01:46:21.174
Leroy Hargrave was a

01:46:21.675 --> 01:46:27.080
21-year-old nurse's aide whose reputation around the hospital was decidedly mixed.

01:46:37.023 --> 01:46:40.260
It was Hargrave who had called the alert on Josephine Thomas.

01:46:41.228 --> 01:46:44.331
Several other deaths had also occurred during Hargrave's shifts.

01:46:45.399 --> 01:46:49.102
Was this coincidence or was something else going on?

01:46:53.240 --> 01:46:55.642
No one had seen Hargrave do anything wrong.

01:46:56.476 --> 01:47:00.881
It would take all the resources of forensic science to establish what had happened.

01:47:04.050 --> 01:47:05.452
Throughout the United States,

01:47:05.519 --> 01:47:08.755
an autopsy is performed whenever the cause of death is unknown.

01:47:09.623 --> 01:47:12.726
This unknown male was found dead on a Michigan street.

01:47:15.095 --> 01:47:15.529
All right,

01:47:16.196 --> 01:47:16.897
go ahead and...

01:47:18.799 --> 01:47:20.300
Check the status of the hair,

01:47:20.467 --> 01:47:22.736
looking for any injuries on the head.

01:47:24.104 --> 01:47:25.505
How did the individual die?

01:47:26.706 --> 01:47:27.741
Was it natural death,

01:47:28.041 --> 01:47:28.642
an accident,

01:47:29.042 --> 01:47:30.710
a homicide or suicide?

01:47:31.645 --> 01:47:33.513
Sometimes the answer is undetermined.

01:47:33.547 --> 01:47:35.382
Did he make it to a hospital?

01:47:36.349 --> 01:47:36.483
No,

01:47:36.550 --> 01:47:37.117
not this guy.

01:47:37.117 --> 01:47:39.386
He was found dead in an older part of town.

01:47:39.386 --> 01:47:40.787
I'm wondering about lead poisoning.

01:47:40.787 --> 01:47:42.522
Do you think we ought to get a heavy metal screen on him?

01:47:42.689 --> 01:47:43.557
I think it would be a good idea.

01:47:44.624 --> 01:47:46.626
Why don't we get some head hair,

01:47:46.726 --> 01:47:46.993
too,

01:47:47.594 --> 01:47:49.229
since arsenic can accumulate in the hair.

01:47:49.563 --> 01:47:52.032
If we do find arsenic on the general heavy metal screen,

01:47:52.032 --> 01:47:53.667
then we can fractionate the hair.

01:47:54.501 --> 01:47:56.269
Since hair grows about one centimeter a month,

01:47:57.070 --> 01:47:59.339
see if there's any chronicity to the poisoning.

01:48:00.474 --> 01:48:01.274
Pathologists

01:48:01.408 --> 01:48:02.976
gather a variety of samples,

01:48:03.176 --> 01:48:04.311
including muscle tissue,

01:48:05.078 --> 01:48:05.445
brain,

01:48:06.213 --> 01:48:06.513
kidney,

01:48:07.481 --> 01:48:07.781
urine,

01:48:08.248 --> 01:48:08.748
and blood.

01:48:09.449 --> 01:48:10.784
About 30 cc's will be enough,

01:48:10.851 --> 01:48:11.117
Paul.

01:48:13.620 --> 01:48:13.954
All right,

01:48:13.954 --> 01:48:15.121
let's get a piece of kidney too,

01:48:15.188 --> 01:48:15.455
Paul.

01:48:18.058 --> 01:48:22.128
Each sample may tell a story about the victim's life and death.

01:48:26.333 --> 01:48:28.134
Poisoning is a rare occurrence,

01:48:28.201 --> 01:48:29.669
according to James Valentour,

01:48:29.936 --> 01:48:32.973
head of the toxicology laboratory for the Commonwealth of Virginia.

01:48:34.307 --> 01:48:36.843
Poisoning is not automatically suspected.

01:48:36.843 --> 01:48:40.680
It depends on the circumstances or lack of other evidence.

01:48:41.248 --> 01:48:42.415
following the investigation.

01:48:42.516 --> 01:48:48.955
If an autopsy is conducted on a young person in otherwise apparent good health and no cause of death is found,

01:48:49.789 --> 01:48:54.427
then the suspicion of drug involvement or drug contribution to that death increases.

01:48:54.828 --> 01:49:00.600
And we proportionately increase our analyses until we rule out all the possibilities that we can.

01:49:01.768 --> 01:49:02.102
Today,

01:49:02.469 --> 01:49:03.570
poison specialists,

01:49:03.670 --> 01:49:05.005
called toxicologists,

01:49:05.238 --> 01:49:08.308
employ a bewildering array of sophisticated technologies.

01:49:09.209 --> 01:49:09.809
Antibodies,

01:49:10.176 --> 01:49:13.446
protein substances the body produces to combat foreign matter,

01:49:13.813 --> 01:49:15.482
are used to detect toxins.

01:49:16.416 --> 01:49:18.919
To test for cocaine and its metabolic products,

01:49:18.952 --> 01:49:19.519
for example,

01:49:19.953 --> 01:49:24.224
blood is mixed with antibodies specific to certain parts of the cocaine molecule.

01:49:25.125 --> 01:49:27.193
A fluorescent label is added to the mixture.

01:49:27.894 --> 01:49:28.962
If cocaine is present,

01:49:29.262 --> 01:49:30.931
the pattern of fluorescence changes.

01:49:32.265 --> 01:49:34.134
To identify poison compounds,

01:49:34.267 --> 01:49:37.637
they must be broken down into smaller parts called ions.

01:49:38.438 --> 01:49:38.705
Dr.

01:49:38.738 --> 01:49:42.242
Valentour and his colleagues use the most powerful techniques available,

01:49:42.609 --> 01:49:45.679
which include the gas chromatograph and mass spectrometer,

01:49:46.012 --> 01:49:47.614
which are often used in combination.

01:49:48.848 --> 01:49:53.720
Chemicals are added to a sample to separate any organic drugs present from the blood matrix.

01:49:56.790 --> 01:50:00.961
The isolated portion is vaporized and passed along a hydrogen-filled tube.

01:50:02.562 --> 01:50:06.199
Molecules of different size migrate through the tube at different rates.

01:50:07.834 --> 01:50:09.502
As molecules emerge from the tube,

01:50:09.803 --> 01:50:11.304
their concentration is measured.

01:50:13.773 --> 01:50:14.107
Next,

01:50:14.341 --> 01:50:17.577
the mass spectrometer shatters the molecule into ions,

01:50:18.011 --> 01:50:19.412
its chemical building blocks.

01:50:21.548 --> 01:50:24.918
A complex molecule may generate hundreds of ion fragments.

01:50:26.086 --> 01:50:29.956
The pattern of ions produced is unique to a particular molecule.

01:50:30.991 --> 01:50:32.892
To identify the unknown substance,

01:50:33.159 --> 01:50:38.732
its ion signature is compared to those of tens of thousands of chemicals in the computer's database.

01:50:41.167 --> 01:50:41.768
In time,

01:50:42.068 --> 01:50:44.537
techniques like these would reveal not one,

01:50:44.871 --> 01:50:48.241
but six poisonings at Petersburg General Hospital.

01:50:53.613 --> 01:50:53.747
Roger,

01:50:53.747 --> 01:50:54.047
Poison Center,

01:50:54.080 --> 01:50:55.949
can I help you?

01:50:55.949 --> 01:50:58.318
The world is filled with deadly chemicals.

01:50:58.852 --> 01:51:02.022
An average home harbors dozens of dangerous substances,

01:51:02.155 --> 01:51:03.323
such as chlorine bleach,

01:51:03.657 --> 01:51:04.290
drain opener,

01:51:04.457 --> 01:51:05.058
and aspirin,

01:51:05.592 --> 01:51:07.093
each capable of causing death.

01:51:08.995 --> 01:51:11.731
The Blodgett Regional Poison Center in Grand Rapids,

01:51:11.765 --> 01:51:12.132
Michigan,

01:51:12.399 --> 01:51:15.301
receives thousands of anxious phone calls each year.

01:51:15.902 --> 01:51:17.337
Many concern overdoses,

01:51:17.737 --> 01:51:19.472
accidental or intentional.

01:51:21.107 --> 01:51:26.613
Many more involve children who have eaten some bright berry or sampled the household bleach.

01:51:27.480 --> 01:51:30.116
And a few involve deliberate attempts to kill.

01:51:31.084 --> 01:51:35.555
John Trestrail directs the center and is an authority on poisons and poisoners.

01:51:36.956 --> 01:51:37.090
Well,

01:51:37.123 --> 01:51:42.429
poisoning homicides differ from normal homicides in that there usually are no witnesses.

01:51:43.997 --> 01:51:45.865
There are no visible signs of trauma.

01:51:46.833 --> 01:51:47.901
You see no bullet holes,

01:51:47.901 --> 01:51:48.935
no stab wounds,

01:51:49.002 --> 01:51:49.736
no bruises.

01:51:50.670 --> 01:51:52.072
Usually it's very covert,

01:51:52.772 --> 01:51:53.440
very secret.

01:51:55.075 --> 01:51:57.343
The evidence is usually lacking.

01:51:58.411 --> 01:52:00.714
There's no cartridge cases or anything.

01:52:01.014 --> 01:52:02.048
Often it's missed,

01:52:02.916 --> 01:52:04.551
and this crime scene is cleaned up.

01:52:07.087 --> 01:52:11.291
Trestrail's office reveals a lifetime of fascination with things poisonous.

01:52:12.792 --> 01:52:13.059
Take,

01:52:13.093 --> 01:52:13.860
for example,

01:52:14.861 --> 01:52:15.395
spiders.

01:52:16.763 --> 01:52:20.934
We know that this small spider compared to one big spider here,

01:52:21.401 --> 01:52:21.735
you say,

01:52:21.801 --> 01:52:21.935
well,

01:52:21.935 --> 01:52:23.203
which one's more poisonous?

01:52:23.303 --> 01:52:23.503
Well,

01:52:23.503 --> 01:52:24.637
most little kids will say,

01:52:25.105 --> 01:52:26.139
obviously the big one.

01:52:26.172 --> 01:52:26.272
Well,

01:52:26.306 --> 01:52:30.243
the tarantula is no more worse than being stung by a bee,

01:52:30.744 --> 01:52:31.578
but the black widow.

01:52:32.445 --> 01:52:33.546
It's capable of killing.

01:52:33.580 --> 01:52:38.985
So size is not always associated with degree of deadliness.

01:52:41.755 --> 01:52:42.689
In Trestrell's view,

01:52:43.056 --> 01:52:46.025
mankind's knowledge of poisons is extremely ancient.

01:52:48.895 --> 01:52:49.763
In all likelihood,

01:52:49.996 --> 01:52:53.566
it goes back to making the connection between eating something and getting sick.

01:52:55.335 --> 01:52:55.568
Okay,

01:52:55.635 --> 01:53:01.841
here we have an example of what primitive man might have first associated cause and effect relationships with.

01:53:01.875 --> 01:53:04.043
Here is a mushroom called the fly agaric,

01:53:04.210 --> 01:53:04.778
common name,

01:53:05.879 --> 01:53:06.946
Amanita muscaria.

01:53:07.781 --> 01:53:13.253
And it contains chemicals that produce hallucinations and a lot of other bad garbage that go with it.

01:53:13.653 --> 01:53:16.222
But I'm sure at one time in prehistory,

01:53:16.890 --> 01:53:19.159
man looked at someone eating this and said,

01:53:20.460 --> 01:53:20.894
What he...

01:53:22.362 --> 01:53:26.866
What he did when he ate this was it caused these symptoms.

01:53:27.033 --> 01:53:30.236
And from that they said this mushroom contains these poisons.

01:53:30.904 --> 01:53:31.938
I have this knowledge.

01:53:32.071 --> 01:53:38.578
This knowledge led to mystery and the shadowy figures of the shamans and witch doctors and everything.

01:53:38.611 --> 01:53:43.516
So the whole history of toxicology began a couple of hundred thousand years ago.

01:53:43.683 --> 01:53:48.154
It was probably the third weapon developed after the stick and the stone,

01:53:48.454 --> 01:53:49.022
the poison.

01:53:51.191 --> 01:53:53.226
The essence of poisoning is stealth.

01:53:53.993 --> 01:53:55.528
To shoot or strangle a person,

01:53:55.795 --> 01:53:57.197
the murderer must be present.

01:53:59.165 --> 01:54:02.168
Poison offers the opportunity of long-distance homicide.

01:54:05.038 --> 01:54:08.474
There is also the chance that homicide won't be recorded at all,

01:54:08.942 --> 01:54:11.578
that the death will be deemed due to natural causes.

01:54:12.579 --> 01:54:17.483
This is what had occurred at Petersburg General Hospital until Josephine Thomas'

01:54:17.584 --> 01:54:19.752
death began to arouse suspicions.

01:54:21.421 --> 01:54:26.593
The first lady came in and was autopsied and was found to have serious heart disease,

01:54:27.060 --> 01:54:33.099
but she also had lethal concentrations of lidocaine in her blood and in her tissues,

01:54:33.566 --> 01:54:38.605
which she should not have had because she had no order for it in her chart,

01:54:39.239 --> 01:54:42.141
nor had she been given lidocaine during her code.

01:54:43.910 --> 01:54:45.745
Concerned about the rising death rate,

01:54:46.012 --> 01:54:50.750
the hospital reviewed all the fatalities since Leroy Hargrave had been assigned to the unit.

01:54:54.120 --> 01:55:00.560
And they identified ten persons that they thought might be at risk for lidocaine poisoning.

01:55:02.462 --> 01:55:03.229
James Dalton,

01:55:03.596 --> 01:55:04.330
now a judge,

01:55:04.597 --> 01:55:08.201
was assistant Commonwealth's attorney in Petersburg when the deaths took place.

01:55:10.703 --> 01:55:12.772
I received a call from an attorney.

01:55:12.805 --> 01:55:13.172
He said,

01:55:13.273 --> 01:55:17.110
we've got something I hope doesn't exist,

01:55:17.210 --> 01:55:24.584
but I'm afraid that there's a possibility that there have been a number of people actually killed at the hospital.

01:55:26.252 --> 01:55:28.588
Dalton also learned the name of the chief suspect,

01:55:28.955 --> 01:55:29.956
Leroy Hargrave.

01:55:31.991 --> 01:55:33.359
They were on his shift,

01:55:33.426 --> 01:55:34.160
and he may have,

01:55:34.294 --> 01:55:35.628
his shift may have varied,

01:55:35.662 --> 01:55:36.829
but he was,

01:55:37.430 --> 01:55:38.431
he moved around,

01:55:38.431 --> 01:55:40.066
so whenever he was on a shift,

01:55:40.533 --> 01:55:43.569
but it was a night shift normally that these occurred,

01:55:43.569 --> 01:55:51.778
and I guess it was not too suspicious initially because I think there is some history of people dying at night more frequently.

01:55:53.146 --> 01:55:53.613
So far,

01:55:53.713 --> 01:55:55.214
the evidence was circumstantial.

01:55:55.715 --> 01:55:58.718
Hargrave happened to be around when the deaths took place.

01:55:59.886 --> 01:56:00.887
To convince Dalton,

01:56:01.254 --> 01:56:02.388
and ultimately a jury,

01:56:02.822 --> 01:56:04.457
that they were premeditated murders,

01:56:04.724 --> 01:56:09.329
the medical examiner had to show that the patients had been given lidocaine in unusual amounts.

01:56:10.897 --> 01:56:15.668
The task was made more difficult because several of the victims had been dead for several months.

01:56:18.037 --> 01:56:32.618
It was a toxicological masterpiece as far as detective work because they were in one case they're working with fresh tissue in other instances they had to recover the poison from bodies which had been embalmed.

01:56:32.952 --> 01:56:48.067
buried and in the third instance they had to try and establish sufficient tissue levels in the biopsy samples from the previous autopsies when it was all over of those 10 deaths that were suspicious six

01:56:48.301 --> 01:56:51.337
were established as due to acute lidocaine poisoning

01:56:53.806 --> 01:56:57.810
Prosecutors piece together a likely scenario leading to Josephine Thomas'

01:56:57.910 --> 01:56:58.144
death.

01:56:59.012 --> 01:57:01.781
It begins with Hargrave stealing some lidocaine.

01:57:08.021 --> 01:57:10.423
Once the cardiac care unit has settled for the night,

01:57:10.757 --> 01:57:11.858
he slips into Thomas'

01:57:11.958 --> 01:57:13.126
room unobserved.

01:57:19.932 --> 01:57:23.002
After loading a hypodermic with a massive dose of lidocaine,

01:57:23.703 --> 01:57:25.538
he temporarily disables Thomas'

01:57:25.638 --> 01:57:26.973
cardiac monitor alarm.

01:57:37.116 --> 01:57:39.018
Within seconds of administering the drug,

01:57:39.385 --> 01:57:41.054
it will disrupt the rhythm of Thomas'

01:57:41.154 --> 01:57:41.354
heart,

01:57:41.821 --> 01:57:43.022
causing a fatal seizure.

01:57:47.226 --> 01:57:47.460
Then

01:57:47.860 --> 01:57:51.364
Hargrave raises the alarm to divert suspicion from himself.

01:57:52.331 --> 01:57:53.399
We need some help in here

01:57:55.568 --> 01:58:00.940
Leroy Hargrave was convicted of poisoning Josephine Thomas and sentenced to life imprisonment.

01:58:02.675 --> 01:58:03.709
He never confessed,

01:58:03.943 --> 01:58:08.081
and his motive for killing Thomas and the other patients was never established.

01:58:10.216 --> 01:58:10.516
Dr.

01:58:10.583 --> 01:58:12.418
Fierro talks about one theory.

01:58:13.319 --> 01:58:14.020
The code,

01:58:14.220 --> 01:58:15.588
the actual code procedure,

01:58:15.588 --> 01:58:20.726
the cardiac arrest procedure with the response by a resuscitation team,

01:58:21.160 --> 01:58:22.862
is a very dramatic event.

01:58:23.229 --> 01:58:24.330
It's very intense.

01:58:24.363 --> 01:58:25.031
It's very rath.

01:58:25.331 --> 01:58:26.132
There are orders,

01:58:26.165 --> 01:58:26.933
there's no pleases,

01:58:26.933 --> 01:58:27.900
there's no thank yous,

01:58:27.900 --> 01:58:29.602
there's just instructions.

01:58:30.336 --> 01:58:33.840
And this is quite exciting to persons who are on the outside,

01:58:34.440 --> 01:58:34.674
you know,

01:58:34.841 --> 01:58:37.677
if you're not related to the person who's being coded.

01:58:38.277 --> 01:58:40.580
So it was thought that he really got into the code.

01:58:42.982 --> 01:58:45.251
Without the initial suspicions of the nurses,

01:58:45.885 --> 01:58:51.090
without the technology to precisely identify lidocaine weeks or months after death,

01:58:51.657 --> 01:58:53.593
Leroy Hargrave might have gone free.

01:58:54.694 --> 01:58:55.561
John Trestrell.

01:58:57.330 --> 01:58:59.632
How many bodies do you dig up to find a bullet hole?

01:58:59.999 --> 01:59:00.199
None.

01:59:00.233 --> 01:59:01.701
They catch that before they're buried.

01:59:02.034 --> 01:59:04.103
How many bodies do you dig up to find a poison?

01:59:04.437 --> 01:59:05.037
Most of them.

01:59:05.138 --> 01:59:07.640
We're missing them at the point of death,

01:59:07.807 --> 01:59:09.208
and we're burying the evidence.

01:59:10.009 --> 01:59:12.478
That's difficult to overcome,

01:59:12.545 --> 01:59:13.713
but that's what we're going to do.

01:59:16.649 --> 01:59:20.920
Among the most difficult poisonings to detect are those involving product tampering.

01:59:20.920 --> 01:59:22.855
In 1982,

01:59:23.289 --> 01:59:26.626
seven people in Chicago died from poison-laced Tylenol.

01:59:27.426 --> 01:59:29.529
A rash of copycat crimes followed,

01:59:30.096 --> 01:59:31.864
each with its own bizarre twist.

01:59:38.337 --> 01:59:38.871
June 11,

01:59:38.871 --> 01:59:40.239
1986,

01:59:40.606 --> 01:59:41.607
the town of Auburn,

01:59:41.707 --> 01:59:42.241
Washington.

01:59:44.010 --> 01:59:44.143
Hey,

01:59:44.210 --> 01:59:44.510
Mom.

01:59:49.582 --> 01:59:53.452
Fifteen-year-old Haley Snow finds her mother collapsed in the bathroom.

01:59:56.088 --> 01:59:57.690
Susan Snow is unconscious.

01:59:58.224 --> 01:59:59.158
Her pulse is weak.

02:00:00.326 --> 02:00:01.627
Her breaths come in long,

02:00:01.794 --> 02:00:02.929
irregular gasps.

02:00:04.664 --> 02:00:06.365
Haley dials 911.

02:00:07.600 --> 02:00:08.734
Within four hours,

02:00:09.101 --> 02:00:10.236
this apparently healthy

02:00:10.770 --> 02:00:13.639
40-year-old mother of two will be declared dead.

02:00:19.412 --> 02:00:22.615
When paramedics arrive at Seattle's Harborview Medical Center,

02:00:22.915 --> 02:00:24.350
Susan Snow is comatose.

02:00:27.753 --> 02:00:30.356
Harborview is the best trauma center in the Northwest,

02:00:30.690 --> 02:00:32.225
but Snow is fading fast.

02:00:33.960 --> 02:00:36.262
Snow's condition puzzles the emergency team.

02:00:36.929 --> 02:00:38.231
Her brain appears swollen,

02:00:38.598 --> 02:00:39.165
inflamed.

02:00:40.166 --> 02:00:41.400
She is struggling for breath,

02:00:41.767 --> 02:00:43.236
and her vital signs are failing.

02:00:44.003 --> 02:00:45.705
She never recovers consciousness.

02:00:46.606 --> 02:00:47.673
An hour after admission,

02:00:48.007 --> 02:00:49.408
she is declared brain dead.

02:00:51.110 --> 02:00:55.514
What appeared at first as a random death soon will be revealed as murder.

02:00:56.549 --> 02:01:00.119
It will cost a pharmaceutical company tens of millions of dollars,

02:01:00.720 --> 02:01:01.854
incite public panic,

02:01:02.622 --> 02:01:07.360
and set off one of the largest law enforcement operations ever mounted in Washington state.

02:01:09.929 --> 02:01:15.034
Special Agents Jack Cusack and Mike Byrne were part of the FBI investigation team.

02:01:16.068 --> 02:01:17.470
The first victim in this case was

02:01:18.070 --> 02:01:18.571
Sue Snow.

02:01:18.971 --> 02:01:22.008
She was a 40-year-old bank teller that lived in Auburn,

02:01:22.074 --> 02:01:22.608
Washington.

02:01:23.175 --> 02:01:25.444
And in her daily routine,

02:01:25.711 --> 02:01:28.581
she always ingested Excedrin.

02:01:29.949 --> 02:01:33.653
The cause of Susan Snow's death emerged almost by chance.

02:01:34.587 --> 02:01:35.354
At her autopsy,

02:01:35.688 --> 02:01:38.891
the assistant medical examiner noticed the smell of bitter almonds,

02:01:39.225 --> 02:01:41.560
a telltale sign of cyanide poisoning.

02:01:42.795 --> 02:01:44.497
Many people cannot detect the odor.

02:01:45.298 --> 02:01:46.399
By a quirk of genetics,

02:01:46.766 --> 02:01:48.734
the assistant medical examiner could.

02:01:52.638 --> 02:01:54.840
Dying by cyanide isn't pleasant.

02:01:55.775 --> 02:01:56.842
Inside each cell,

02:01:57.310 --> 02:02:00.846
cyanide displaces oxygen in the energy transport mechanism.

02:02:02.748 --> 02:02:03.816
Deprived of oxygen,

02:02:04.216 --> 02:02:05.217
cells collapse.

02:02:05.818 --> 02:02:06.886
The body dies,

02:02:07.420 --> 02:02:08.654
slowly asphyxiating,

02:02:09.121 --> 02:02:10.222
bit by bit.

02:02:12.758 --> 02:02:13.759
By June 16th,

02:02:13.993 --> 02:02:16.062
the cyanide source had been identified,

02:02:16.562 --> 02:02:20.466
the bottle of extra-strength Excedrin capsules in Susan Snow's kitchen.

02:02:21.767 --> 02:02:26.472
750,000 bottles of Excedrin were immediately recalled or withdrawn.

02:02:27.206 --> 02:02:29.475
The news made headlines all over America.

02:02:31.077 --> 02:02:34.380
Fresh in everyone's minds were the Tylenol poisonings in Chicago.

02:02:35.915 --> 02:02:37.616
How many would die this time?

02:02:40.453 --> 02:02:41.320
To catch a poisoner,

02:02:41.620 --> 02:02:44.090
it helps to get inside his or her head.

02:02:45.524 --> 02:02:48.894
John Trestrell explains how poisoners target their victims.

02:02:50.563 --> 02:02:51.831
One is type

02:02:52.765 --> 02:02:52.898
S,

02:02:53.899 --> 02:02:55.568
S meaning specific victim.

02:02:55.868 --> 02:02:57.536
That victim has a name.

02:02:57.870 --> 02:02:59.271
They know exactly who it's going to be,

02:02:59.338 --> 02:03:00.439
whether it's their spouse,

02:03:00.439 --> 02:03:01.774
or whether it's a political figure,

02:03:01.774 --> 02:03:02.341
or somebody.

02:03:02.808 --> 02:03:04.777
They're going out after an individual with a name.

02:03:05.277 --> 02:03:06.812
The other type is type

02:03:07.179 --> 02:03:08.681
R, random victim.

02:03:08.948 --> 02:03:09.448
In other words,

02:03:09.482 --> 02:03:10.750
they know they're going to get somebody.

02:03:10.750 --> 02:03:12.151
They have no idea who it's going to be.

02:03:12.151 --> 02:03:15.521
And this is where you find your tamperers who are at work,

02:03:15.621 --> 02:03:18.290
either for ego or for industrial blackmail.

02:03:18.858 --> 02:03:25.998
And sometimes you can actually find individuals cloaked and camouflaged as random poisoners that are specific.

02:03:25.998 --> 02:03:28.134
So this gets to be a real mind game,

02:03:28.134 --> 02:03:30.302
the investigation of this kind of offender.

02:03:32.538 --> 02:03:33.639
Susan Snow's death,

02:03:33.672 --> 02:03:34.473
it would turn out,

02:03:34.707 --> 02:03:35.841
was a random killing.

02:03:36.809 --> 02:03:41.647
She was poisoned in a cold-blooded attempt to draw attention away from the real target.

02:03:45.017 --> 02:03:45.951
On June 5th,

02:03:46.285 --> 02:03:48.621
six days before Susan Snow died,

02:03:48.988 --> 02:03:52.358
Bruce Nickel had suddenly collapsed at his mobile home in Kent,

02:03:52.425 --> 02:03:52.925
Washington.

02:03:56.829 --> 02:03:58.063
When paramedics arrived,

02:03:58.297 --> 02:03:58.864
they found the

02:03:59.398 --> 02:04:00.766
52-year-old Nickel on his back,

02:04:01.200 --> 02:04:02.101
gasping for breath.

02:04:06.839 --> 02:04:08.374
His face was cherry red,

02:04:08.908 --> 02:04:10.709
his body deathly pale.

02:04:12.611 --> 02:04:15.314
The paramedics had never seen such symptoms before.

02:04:17.583 --> 02:04:18.284
Nickel's wife,

02:04:18.350 --> 02:04:18.651
Stella,

02:04:18.951 --> 02:04:20.786
told them he had been complaining of headaches.

02:04:21.654 --> 02:04:22.888
Shortly before collapsing,

02:04:22.955 --> 02:04:25.524
he had taken two extra-strength Excedrin capsules.

02:04:27.626 --> 02:04:31.530
Stella emphasized Excedrin over and over the paramedics would remember later.

02:04:32.097 --> 02:04:34.099
She even retrieved the bottle from her kitchen.

02:04:38.971 --> 02:04:41.574
Evacuated to Harborview Medical Center in Seattle,

02:04:41.974 --> 02:04:43.642
Nickel died a few hours later.

02:04:44.643 --> 02:04:47.446
The autopsy recorded death from natural causes.

02:04:47.980 --> 02:04:49.748
Nickel had died from emphysema.

02:04:52.551 --> 02:04:53.185
Strangely enough,

02:04:53.819 --> 02:04:56.922
Stella Nichols was also making a few additional calls on her own.

02:04:56.989 --> 02:04:57.923
She called her doctor,

02:04:57.990 --> 02:04:59.425
are you sure this is what he died of?

02:04:59.859 --> 02:05:00.926
She called the paramedics,

02:05:01.126 --> 02:05:02.328
it could have been anything else.

02:05:03.095 --> 02:05:05.698
Are you sure that he died of natural causes?

02:05:05.965 --> 02:05:06.999
She wasn't quite that obvious,

02:05:07.366 --> 02:05:08.734
but she did make these telephone calls.

02:05:10.135 --> 02:05:13.772
Stella called again after news of Susan Snow's poisoning appeared.

02:05:14.540 --> 02:05:18.511
The lot number of Snow's Excedrin matched the capsules Bruce had taken.

02:05:20.446 --> 02:05:22.481
Tests quickly proved that Nichols'

02:05:22.481 --> 02:05:24.483
capsules were laced with cyanide.

02:05:25.818 --> 02:05:26.185
Nichols'

02:05:26.218 --> 02:05:27.286
body had been buried,

02:05:27.620 --> 02:05:29.255
but a vial of his blood remained.

02:05:29.855 --> 02:05:29.955
It,

02:05:30.055 --> 02:05:30.256
too,

02:05:30.356 --> 02:05:30.923
was tested.

02:05:31.457 --> 02:05:31.824
Again,

02:05:32.358 --> 02:05:32.992
cyanide.

02:05:35.995 --> 02:05:36.829
Across the state,

02:05:37.162 --> 02:05:38.797
shelves were cleared of Excedrin.

02:05:39.665 --> 02:05:41.567
But of the thousands of bottles screened,

02:05:41.867 --> 02:05:44.570
only five were found to contain cyanide.

02:05:47.306 --> 02:05:49.074
A little quirky thing about that was,

02:05:50.175 --> 02:05:51.644
Stella had two of the bottles.

02:05:52.444 --> 02:05:54.013
And even more strange than that was,

02:05:54.647 --> 02:05:55.047
she said,

02:05:55.781 --> 02:05:55.915
Hey,

02:05:55.915 --> 02:05:58.817
I bought one of the bottles about a month before Bruce died.

02:05:59.952 --> 02:06:04.056
And I bought the second bottle a little more than a week before he died.

02:06:06.091 --> 02:06:08.727
The odds of that are unbelievable.

02:06:10.896 --> 02:06:13.532
Stella's deadly plan began to unfold.

02:06:14.333 --> 02:06:14.667
First,

02:06:14.900 --> 02:06:17.670
she poisoned Bruce with cyanide-laced capsules.

02:06:18.938 --> 02:06:19.171
Then,

02:06:19.471 --> 02:06:21.774
to make his death appear a random killing,

02:06:22.174 --> 02:06:25.444
she placed more contaminated capsules on drugstore shelves.

02:06:27.379 --> 02:06:29.348
Investigators began searching for a motive.

02:06:29.949 --> 02:06:31.083
They soon found one.

02:06:32.017 --> 02:06:33.052
Insurance fraud.

02:06:35.154 --> 02:06:36.288
When Bruce Nichols died...

02:06:37.389 --> 02:06:40.359
And he was found to have died of natural causes.

02:06:40.793 --> 02:06:43.662
Stella stood to gain approximately $31,000.

02:06:45.130 --> 02:06:46.398
If he died accidental death,

02:06:46.599 --> 02:06:49.535
then this jumped up to about $178,000.

02:06:49.735 --> 02:06:50.869
And in addition to that,

02:06:50.936 --> 02:06:52.938
she had a couple $20,000 policies.

02:06:55.040 --> 02:06:56.141
To insurance companies,

02:06:56.442 --> 02:06:59.178
dying by poison counts as accidental death.

02:06:59.778 --> 02:07:01.914
But had Bruce taken out the policies?

02:07:02.982 --> 02:07:05.284
Handwriting experts scrutinized his signatures,

02:07:06.018 --> 02:07:07.186
comparing them to Stella's.

02:07:08.654 --> 02:07:10.122
The height and width of a letter,

02:07:10.556 --> 02:07:11.690
its shape and slope,

02:07:12.091 --> 02:07:13.993
all contain tell-tale clues.

02:07:14.893 --> 02:07:15.928
So does the fluidity,

02:07:16.261 --> 02:07:17.863
the flow and rhythm of the writing,

02:07:18.864 --> 02:07:20.232
which is very hard to fake.

02:07:22.167 --> 02:07:24.169
Bruce had not signed the documents.

02:07:25.938 --> 02:07:26.639
Stella had.

02:07:28.407 --> 02:07:31.443
Could the investigators now tie Stella to the cyanide?

02:07:35.147 --> 02:07:39.251
When the capsules were shipped to the FBI's toxicology laboratory in Washington,

02:07:39.318 --> 02:07:39.551
D.C.,

02:07:40.085 --> 02:07:41.720
an unexpected clue appeared.

02:07:42.254 --> 02:07:45.658
The white powder was contaminated with tiny green specks.

02:07:47.926 --> 02:07:49.361
The specs were 99%

02:07:49.728 --> 02:07:50.696
sodium chloride,

02:07:51.063 --> 02:07:52.197
ordinary table salt.

02:07:53.232 --> 02:07:56.969
Bulking out products with salt is a common manufacturing practice.

02:07:57.670 --> 02:07:59.238
But what of the remaining 1%?

02:08:00.873 --> 02:08:03.942
Laboratory analysis revealed four separate chemicals.

02:08:04.777 --> 02:08:08.781
Two were used almost exclusively in preparations called algaecides.

02:08:09.748 --> 02:08:12.851
One commercial product contained all four of the chemicals.

02:08:13.485 --> 02:08:15.087
It came in the form of tablets,

02:08:15.387 --> 02:08:16.488
which were bright green.

02:08:19.358 --> 02:08:21.093
The laboratory called us back and said,

02:08:22.294 --> 02:08:30.536
we have positively identified those green crystals as a product known as algae destroyer.

02:08:31.036 --> 02:08:35.240
It's an algaecide used in fish tanks by fish fanciers.

02:08:36.642 --> 02:08:43.048
And that really rang a bell for us because from the first time any of our agents went in Stella's residence,

02:08:43.382 --> 02:08:44.917
it was obvious she was a fish fancier.

02:08:45.017 --> 02:08:47.886
She had fish aquariums all around the front room.

02:08:51.056 --> 02:08:53.559
The cyanide had led to algae destroyer.

02:08:54.393 --> 02:08:56.662
Would algae destroyer now lead to Stella?

02:08:59.898 --> 02:09:02.367
Agents combed pet stores all over Seattle.

02:09:02.935 --> 02:09:06.772
Someone might remember Stella and the kind of algaecide she preferred.

02:09:09.108 --> 02:09:12.411
The search ended at a store not far from where Stella lived.

02:09:13.011 --> 02:09:14.646
It stocked Algae Destroyer.

02:09:15.581 --> 02:09:19.384
And the manager recognized a photograph of the woman who frequently bought it there.

02:09:20.886 --> 02:09:21.887
It was Stella Nickel.

02:09:27.092 --> 02:09:28.761
For months after Bruce Nichols'

02:09:28.794 --> 02:09:29.094
murder,

02:09:29.428 --> 02:09:31.597
Stella avoided taking a polygraph test.

02:09:32.598 --> 02:09:33.832
Her health would not permit it,

02:09:33.899 --> 02:09:34.566
she explained.

02:09:36.135 --> 02:09:37.636
In November 1986,

02:09:37.936 --> 02:09:39.738
she finally agreed to cooperate.

02:09:42.074 --> 02:09:45.344
The test is designed to pick up tiny physiological changes.

02:09:48.680 --> 02:09:51.350
Blood pressure and pulse rate are carefully monitored.

02:09:52.184 --> 02:09:54.486
Each may change when an individual lies.

02:09:57.589 --> 02:10:02.828
Forensic detectives used the polygraph to eliminate innocent individuals from their suspect list.

02:10:03.428 --> 02:10:05.397
That was not to be Stella's fate.

02:10:07.366 --> 02:10:11.336
I remember the comment she made when we were walking back to the examination room.

02:10:11.837 --> 02:10:13.005
She kind of turned around and said,

02:10:14.239 --> 02:10:16.308
I feel as though I'm walking into a lion's den.

02:10:17.509 --> 02:10:20.045
I didn't understand the significance of that comment,

02:10:20.045 --> 02:10:20.445
but I thought,

02:10:20.445 --> 02:10:21.647
a little unusual.

02:10:23.248 --> 02:10:23.882
Stella took the.

02:10:24.783 --> 02:10:25.651
Took the examination.

02:10:26.051 --> 02:10:27.152
I formulated the questions.

02:10:27.719 --> 02:10:28.554
They were clear cut.

02:10:30.489 --> 02:10:32.224
Did you cause your husband's death?

02:10:33.959 --> 02:10:37.462
Did you put potassium cyanide in those extra strength excedrin capsules?

02:10:39.431 --> 02:10:39.998
Stella clearly,

02:10:40.666 --> 02:10:42.367
clearly showed deception on the test.

02:10:43.302 --> 02:10:47.139
When we informed her of that in straightforward fashion,

02:10:47.873 --> 02:10:49.808
she asked for her attorney who was in the waiting room,

02:10:49.908 --> 02:10:53.312
and basically everybody knows how the Miranda rights go.

02:10:53.378 --> 02:10:55.380
That pretty much terminated that interview that day.

02:10:57.649 --> 02:10:59.451
More trouble was in store for Stella.

02:11:00.118 --> 02:11:02.487
This time it came from an unexpected source,

02:11:02.921 --> 02:11:03.922
her daughter Cindy.

02:11:06.925 --> 02:11:09.194
When she heard Stella had failed the polygraph,

02:11:09.528 --> 02:11:11.196
she contacted Jack Cusack.

02:11:12.931 --> 02:11:13.165
You know,

02:11:13.165 --> 02:11:14.099
there's no doubt about it.

02:11:14.166 --> 02:11:17.336
We had a lot of good breaks come our way on this case.

02:11:18.036 --> 02:11:23.075
And one that paid off initially was the fact that Stella's daughter,

02:11:23.175 --> 02:11:23.475
Cindy,

02:11:24.710 --> 02:11:30.816
decided to share with us some of the conversations that she had had with her mother out at the airport.

02:11:32.651 --> 02:11:34.019
In 1985,

02:11:34.052 --> 02:11:35.220
after years of feuding,

02:11:35.554 --> 02:11:37.656
Stella and Cindy briefly reconciled.

02:11:38.824 --> 02:11:42.728
They even got jobs together as security guards at Seattle's Sea-Tac Airport.

02:11:44.129 --> 02:11:44.763
It was there,

02:11:45.097 --> 02:11:46.632
in the pauses between flights,

02:11:46.932 --> 02:11:49.134
that Cindy heard hints of Stella's plan.

02:11:52.838 --> 02:11:53.238
First,

02:11:53.472 --> 02:11:55.908
Stella asked her daughter where she might buy heroin.

02:11:57.209 --> 02:11:58.443
What was a lethal dose?

02:11:58.977 --> 02:11:59.912
How much cocaine?

02:12:00.846 --> 02:12:04.750
Then she talked about hiring a hitman to shoot Bruce while she was out of town,

02:12:05.384 --> 02:12:05.550
or,

02:12:05.951 --> 02:12:06.618
less plausibly,

02:12:06.952 --> 02:12:08.720
to kill him in an automobile crash.

02:12:11.023 --> 02:12:11.423
Finally,

02:12:11.890 --> 02:12:13.592
she talked about poisoning Bruce.

02:12:13.692 --> 02:12:16.061
You know,

02:12:16.128 --> 02:12:16.995
Cindy told us that...

02:12:18.030 --> 02:12:18.263
You know,

02:12:18.263 --> 02:12:20.899
my mother spent a lot of time doing research at the library.

02:12:22.100 --> 02:12:22.401
She said,

02:12:22.901 --> 02:12:27.306
my mother would go to the libraries within the Seattle area,

02:12:27.339 --> 02:12:28.273
within King County,

02:12:29.374 --> 02:12:31.710
and research various plants.

02:12:34.146 --> 02:12:34.680
At first,

02:12:35.013 --> 02:12:37.749
Stella hoped to identify poisonous plants on her property,

02:12:38.083 --> 02:12:40.218
which she could surreptitiously feed to Bruce.

02:12:41.820 --> 02:12:43.088
To check out Cindy's story,

02:12:43.455 --> 02:12:47.359
the FBI sent teams of agents to every library in South Kent County.

02:12:48.427 --> 02:12:49.962
It was a monumental task.

02:12:51.029 --> 02:12:52.264
They were searching for a book,

02:12:52.698 --> 02:12:53.131
any book,

02:12:53.432 --> 02:12:55.233
that would tie Stella to poison.

02:12:58.136 --> 02:13:01.740
Boxes of books were sent back to the FBI laboratory in Washington,

02:13:01.807 --> 02:13:02.074
D.C.

02:13:03.108 --> 02:13:03.375
There,

02:13:03.642 --> 02:13:06.812
a team of examiners scrutinized them for Stella's fingerprints.

02:13:08.580 --> 02:13:11.583
We're kind of part of the human behavior aspect of the case,

02:13:11.583 --> 02:13:11.883
and that's,

02:13:11.883 --> 02:13:12.784
in a lot of ways,

02:13:13.452 --> 02:13:14.286
that's pretty interesting.

02:13:14.953 --> 02:13:15.921
You're in on the interviews.

02:13:15.954 --> 02:13:19.224
You're kind of putting the pieces of the puzzle together.

02:13:19.725 --> 02:13:30.769
One of the unsung heroes in this is a poor guy back in the FBI laboratory that gets a half a truckload of books and a set of fingerprints and said,

02:13:31.169 --> 02:13:31.870
find him in there.

02:13:31.870 --> 02:13:33.972
And he spends months and months,

02:13:34.506 --> 02:13:36.475
and all of a sudden and calls us up one day and says,

02:13:36.975 --> 02:13:37.175
hey,

02:13:38.343 --> 02:13:39.144
In this one book,

02:13:39.945 --> 02:13:41.546
of the multitude of books you sent me,

02:13:42.514 --> 02:13:46.952
I found 104 of Stella's fingertips in there,

02:13:47.486 --> 02:13:49.154
and I found three palm prints.

02:13:50.922 --> 02:13:54.259
You can imagine when we're on the receiving end of a telephone call like that.

02:13:57.596 --> 02:13:59.731
The circle was closing on Stella.

02:14:01.199 --> 02:14:02.667
Special Agent Mike Byrne.

02:14:03.869 --> 02:14:06.271
So now we have library books that we went out,

02:14:06.505 --> 02:14:08.340
came up with her latent prints on library books,

02:14:08.407 --> 02:14:10.709
where she actually researched cyanide.

02:14:11.410 --> 02:14:12.444
We had the insurance,

02:14:13.512 --> 02:14:15.580
where she forged Bruce's signature,

02:14:15.981 --> 02:14:18.917
and now we have the algae destroyer product that she actually purchased.

02:14:20.318 --> 02:14:20.786
On May 9,

02:14:20.786 --> 02:14:22.421
1988,

02:14:22.421 --> 02:14:26.691
Stella Nickel was found guilty of murdering Bruce Nickel and Susan Snow.

02:14:27.426 --> 02:14:30.328
She was sentenced to life imprisonment without parole.

02:14:31.830 --> 02:14:32.731
She never buckled.

02:14:33.331 --> 02:14:34.900
When Jack had a chance to interview her,

02:14:35.167 --> 02:14:35.333
said,

02:14:35.333 --> 02:14:37.135
you failed the polygraph examination,

02:14:37.369 --> 02:14:38.703
it was just a stone face.

02:14:39.337 --> 02:14:40.338
And she never buckled.

02:14:40.405 --> 02:14:41.139
Even to this day,

02:14:41.206 --> 02:14:42.941
she's never said anything.

02:14:45.777 --> 02:14:47.879
No one saw Stella poison Bruce.

02:14:48.413 --> 02:14:50.148
She never even met Susan Snow.

02:14:50.882 --> 02:14:51.183
Still,

02:14:51.450 --> 02:14:53.085
she refuses to face her guilt.

02:14:54.719 --> 02:14:57.289
Denial may be a characteristic of poisoners.

02:14:58.190 --> 02:14:59.491
According to John Trestrail,

02:14:59.958 --> 02:15:01.426
imagination is another.

02:15:02.561 --> 02:15:05.997
We also find that poisoners are very intelligent,

02:15:07.599 --> 02:15:15.507
certainly more so than the typical brute-like clubbing of an individual as a sophistication of the weapon's delivery.

02:15:16.074 --> 02:15:17.476
We find they're very artistic.

02:15:18.043 --> 02:15:18.643
By artistic,

02:15:18.677 --> 02:15:20.345
I don't mean they paint pretty pictures,

02:15:20.378 --> 02:15:23.381
but I mean that they can design almost something like a play.

02:15:24.950 --> 02:15:27.986
Investigators believe Joe Mealing adopted Stella Nichols'

02:15:27.986 --> 02:15:31.256
scenario when he read about her story in Reader's Digest.

02:15:32.491 --> 02:15:34.126
The case begins on February 2,

02:15:34.159 --> 02:15:36.261
1991,

02:15:36.261 --> 02:15:36.628
in Tumwater,

02:15:36.695 --> 02:15:37.195
Washington,

02:15:37.462 --> 02:15:39.664
50 miles southwest of Stella Nichols'

02:15:39.698 --> 02:15:39.965
home.

02:15:43.802 --> 02:15:44.236
One evening,

02:15:44.569 --> 02:15:46.238
as the Mealings are preparing for bed,

02:15:46.738 --> 02:15:49.341
Joe reminds his wife Jennifer to take a Sudafed.

02:15:50.642 --> 02:15:52.177
Jennifer's snoring disturbs him,

02:15:52.177 --> 02:15:52.811
he claims.

02:15:53.178 --> 02:15:54.246
She must be congested.

02:15:56.314 --> 02:15:57.983
Jennifer does not feel congested,

02:15:58.383 --> 02:16:00.018
but under Joe's continued pressure,

02:16:00.285 --> 02:16:01.019
she agrees.

02:16:04.322 --> 02:16:05.023
A week earlier,

02:16:05.323 --> 02:16:07.325
Joe had purchased some Sudafed capsules.

02:16:08.026 --> 02:16:08.994
But why Sudafed,

02:16:09.327 --> 02:16:10.595
which they have never used?

02:16:10.929 --> 02:16:11.930
And why capsules?

02:16:12.631 --> 02:16:13.632
After Stella Nickel,

02:16:14.099 --> 02:16:15.867
Jennifer is wary of using them.

02:16:22.040 --> 02:16:22.607
Moments later,

02:16:22.941 --> 02:16:25.710
Jennifer collapses and rapidly becomes unconscious.

02:16:35.554 --> 02:16:39.591
Special Agent Tom Turgison was involved with the FBI investigation.

02:16:40.392 --> 02:16:54.272
What happens when someone is poisoned by sodium cyanide was perhaps best described by Joe Mealing himself when he called the 911 operator and described to the operator what was happening to his wife.

02:16:55.307 --> 02:16:57.475
My wife is going through some kind of convulsion.

02:16:57.842 --> 02:16:58.043
What's that?

02:16:58.043 --> 02:16:58.777
Second Avenue South.

02:16:58.777 --> 02:16:58.910
Yes,

02:16:58.977 --> 02:17:01.346
1111 Second Avenue South,

02:17:01.379 --> 02:17:01.813
number two.

02:17:02.514 --> 02:17:03.548
Has she ever had a problem,

02:17:03.548 --> 02:17:03.982
a seizure before?

02:17:03.982 --> 02:17:04.015
No,

02:17:04.015 --> 02:17:05.150
she's never had a problem before.

02:17:05.183 --> 02:17:05.984
How old is she?

02:17:06.351 --> 02:17:07.219
She's 29,

02:17:07.319 --> 02:17:07.819
28.

02:17:09.921 --> 02:17:11.089
She called me from the other room,

02:17:11.590 --> 02:17:12.123
and she's now,

02:17:12.224 --> 02:17:12.924
she's,

02:17:12.924 --> 02:17:14.025
her mouth is foaming.

02:17:14.993 --> 02:17:15.694
Take a deep breath.

02:17:15.760 --> 02:17:17.128
You need to be calm for your wife,

02:17:17.229 --> 02:17:17.329
OK?

02:17:17.562 --> 02:17:17.896
Yes,

02:17:17.896 --> 02:17:18.363
I'm calm.

02:17:18.797 --> 02:17:18.930
OK,

02:17:19.231 --> 02:17:22.067
did she use drugs or any medication of any kind?

02:17:22.067 --> 02:17:23.134
She just took a Sudafed.

02:17:24.369 --> 02:17:25.537
It was fast,

02:17:25.737 --> 02:17:26.471
it was swift,

02:17:26.972 --> 02:17:28.340
and she was in agony.

02:17:30.242 --> 02:17:31.576
Rushed to the emergency room,

02:17:31.910 --> 02:17:32.978
Jennifer is in a coma.

02:17:34.913 --> 02:17:36.815
The staff struggles to save her life,

02:17:37.182 --> 02:17:39.584
treating Jennifer for diabetic ketoacidosis,

02:17:40.385 --> 02:17:42.387
elevated acid and blood sugar levels.

02:17:43.688 --> 02:17:44.556
In the midst of it all,

02:17:44.889 --> 02:17:48.326
Joe Mealing makes a curious suggestion to the physician in charge.

02:17:48.927 --> 02:17:50.028
Have you considered poison,

02:17:50.495 --> 02:17:51.763
cyanide in particular?

02:17:54.032 --> 02:17:54.733
Miraculously,

02:17:55.100 --> 02:17:56.067
Jennifer recovers.

02:17:59.671 --> 02:18:04.309
She tells the emergency room doctor that she had taken Sudafed at her husband's insistence.

02:18:06.411 --> 02:18:09.547
The doctor alerts the police and orders a cyanide test.

02:18:10.315 --> 02:18:10.815
Next day,

02:18:11.116 --> 02:18:12.617
his suspicions are confirmed.

02:18:13.018 --> 02:18:15.287
There is cyanide in Jennifer's blood.

02:18:19.057 --> 02:18:21.626
Without Joe Mealing's comments about cyanide,

02:18:21.926 --> 02:18:24.362
Jennifer Mealing's poisoning might have been overlooked,

02:18:24.863 --> 02:18:25.964
just as Bruce Nichols'

02:18:25.964 --> 02:18:27.699
poisoning initially was missed.

02:18:29.801 --> 02:18:33.872
But why don't toxicology laboratories routinely screen for cyanide?

02:18:34.706 --> 02:18:36.808
Washington State toxicologist Dr.

02:18:36.875 --> 02:18:37.575
Barry Logan.

02:18:39.177 --> 02:18:42.981
It's relatively uncommon to uncover an unsuspected poisoning.

02:18:43.815 --> 02:18:47.886
Usually if an investigation has been done properly by the investigator's office,

02:18:47.886 --> 02:18:48.987
by the coroner's office,

02:18:49.788 --> 02:18:53.858
we have information up front about what the circumstances are surrounding the death,

02:18:53.892 --> 02:18:55.860
so we know whether it's likely to be a poisoning or not.

02:18:57.796 --> 02:19:01.333
Dozens of compounds show up on routine drug screening tests.

02:19:01.766 --> 02:19:02.901
Cyanide does not.

02:19:03.935 --> 02:19:05.537
And there is a further complication.

02:19:07.405 --> 02:19:10.642
Prior tests for cyanide have taken a long time to do,

02:19:11.376 --> 02:19:18.917
and it's been difficult to generate the results in a way in which the information can be used effectively by the investigators.

02:19:21.186 --> 02:19:22.987
Before 1991,

02:19:22.987 --> 02:19:25.657
detecting cyanide involved a multi-step test,

02:19:25.957 --> 02:19:27.625
which took at least a day to complete.

02:19:28.460 --> 02:19:32.030
Every sample of tissue or blood had to be tested separately.

02:19:33.698 --> 02:19:33.998
Dr.

02:19:34.065 --> 02:19:36.134
Logan has devised a simpler procedure,

02:19:36.468 --> 02:19:37.502
a litmus test.

02:19:38.403 --> 02:19:42.040
Chemicals are added to a sample generating hydrogen cyanide gas,

02:19:42.507 --> 02:19:43.808
if cyanide is present.

02:19:45.977 --> 02:19:46.811
At the same time,

02:19:47.078 --> 02:19:47.846
the litmus strip,

02:19:48.346 --> 02:19:50.548
paper impregnated with a complex of chemicals,

02:19:50.849 --> 02:19:51.850
is added to the tube.

02:19:52.851 --> 02:19:54.753
If hydrogen cyanide has been produced,

02:19:54.986 --> 02:19:56.154
the strip turns purple.

02:19:56.888 --> 02:20:01.760
The degree of coloration is a rough guide to how much cyanide the sample contains.

02:20:04.462 --> 02:20:09.467
The test that we currently use in this lab to screen for the presence of cyanide is very easy to do.

02:20:10.368 --> 02:20:13.004
We can do a large number of screens in a short period of time,

02:20:13.037 --> 02:20:14.072
which is a big advantage.

02:20:17.509 --> 02:20:20.111
Within days of Jennifer Mealing's near poisoning,

02:20:20.445 --> 02:20:20.745
Dr.

02:20:20.812 --> 02:20:23.515
Logan had the opportunity to try out his new tests.

02:20:25.583 --> 02:20:26.251
On February

02:20:26.651 --> 02:20:27.318
11th and February

02:20:27.819 --> 02:20:30.121
18th, there were two mysterious deaths.

02:20:30.655 --> 02:20:32.757
Both fell within Logan's jurisdiction.

02:20:35.093 --> 02:20:45.537
We conducted a full drug screen on the sample and found the presence of therapeutic levels of compound called pseudoephedrine which is the major component of Sudafed.

02:20:46.237 --> 02:20:52.744
That in itself wasn't particularly remarkable but at the pathologist request we also did a test for cyanide.

02:20:53.378 --> 02:20:55.680
The cyanide test came back strongly positive,

02:20:56.614 --> 02:21:01.686
a level that indicated almost certainly the cause of death as being cyanide poisoning.

02:21:03.888 --> 02:21:04.756
On March 1st,

02:21:04.956 --> 02:21:06.658
a pattern finally emerged.

02:21:07.091 --> 02:21:08.526
Three cyanide poisonings,

02:21:08.827 --> 02:21:09.494
two deaths.

02:21:10.161 --> 02:21:12.330
All three victims had taken Sudafed.

02:21:13.398 --> 02:21:13.865
Next day,

02:21:14.332 --> 02:21:15.633
Sudafed was withdrawn,

02:21:16.201 --> 02:21:18.036
and the FBI was called in.

02:21:18.970 --> 02:21:21.473
Before we've actually focused on Joe,

02:21:21.539 --> 02:21:24.943
because that's one of the things we look at are the victim families,

02:21:25.443 --> 02:21:34.419
but Joe made several comments that he is the primary suspect and that he's too smart to ever be caught,

02:21:34.419 --> 02:21:36.888
and he certainly wouldn't be caught by the FBI.

02:21:39.524 --> 02:21:44.729
But Joe Mealing underestimated the agent's tenacity and the FBI's resources.

02:21:47.232 --> 02:21:53.338
FBI agents carefully examined the backing of the Sudafed packages in the hopes of finding some evidence of tampering.

02:21:57.242 --> 02:21:59.143
They found that on untampered packages,

02:21:59.444 --> 02:22:02.013
the foil breaks when the pill is pushed through the back,

02:22:02.480 --> 02:22:03.581
leaving a jagged edge.

02:22:07.151 --> 02:22:08.453
But on the tampered packages,

02:22:08.753 --> 02:22:10.388
the break in the foil was smooth.

02:22:12.223 --> 02:22:14.459
Mealing had removed the capsules with a razor,

02:22:14.893 --> 02:22:18.296
carefully slicing them from the package so he could replace them later.

02:22:22.166 --> 02:22:23.401
Throughout the investigation,

02:22:24.269 --> 02:22:29.874
we tried to learn about nearly every aspect of Joe Meiling's life.

02:22:30.642 --> 02:22:35.647
We tried to reconstruct everything that he did for a fairly extensive time period,

02:22:36.314 --> 02:22:43.254
geographically place him into the areas of the crime scenes to find out what's making him tick,

02:22:43.688 --> 02:22:44.822
try to find the motive,

02:22:45.123 --> 02:22:46.157
how does he operate.

02:22:46.324 --> 02:22:49.327
We want to know everything about Joe Meiling.

02:22:52.163 --> 02:22:54.666
Joe and Jennifer married in 1986.

02:22:55.333 --> 02:22:56.067
From the beginning,

02:22:56.267 --> 02:22:57.135
they had quarreled,

02:22:57.502 --> 02:22:58.503
mostly about money.

02:23:01.372 --> 02:23:04.042
Jennifer took multiple jobs to make ends meet.

02:23:04.876 --> 02:23:06.311
Joe indulged his hobbies,

02:23:07.211 --> 02:23:07.779
photography,

02:23:08.646 --> 02:23:09.113
computer,

02:23:10.114 --> 02:23:11.215
and theatrical makeup.

02:23:13.384 --> 02:23:15.086
But in the days before her poisoning,

02:23:15.386 --> 02:23:16.421
Jennifer later admitted,

02:23:16.788 --> 02:23:18.957
Joe's normal behavior had changed.

02:23:19.791 --> 02:23:21.459
Five days before her collapse,

02:23:21.826 --> 02:23:23.394
Joe bought the Sudafed capsules,

02:23:23.761 --> 02:23:25.463
which neither of them had used before.

02:23:29.000 --> 02:23:30.301
When Jennifer returned home,

02:23:30.635 --> 02:23:33.004
she found Joe polishing his gram scale.

02:23:34.205 --> 02:23:34.772
Why now?

02:23:35.073 --> 02:23:36.941
He had not used it in 18 months.

02:23:38.009 --> 02:23:38.843
Stranger still,

02:23:39.310 --> 02:23:41.045
Joe was wearing latex gloves.

02:23:42.180 --> 02:23:43.481
He never cleaned the kitchen.

02:23:43.715 --> 02:23:44.616
It wasn't his job.

02:23:47.285 --> 02:23:47.652
Finally,

02:23:47.952 --> 02:23:49.821
there was the matter of the insurance policy.

02:23:51.623 --> 02:23:59.063
Joe caught Jennifer on the way to work one day and asked her to sign what she thought was a small life insurance policy for only $30,000.

02:24:00.965 --> 02:24:02.433
He knew exactly what he was doing.

02:24:02.467 --> 02:24:03.568
He asked her to sign it.

02:24:03.968 --> 02:24:05.870
When she was in a hurry on the way out the door,

02:24:06.204 --> 02:24:06.871
she signs,

02:24:07.905 --> 02:24:11.943
and now he has a total of $700,000 on her life.

02:24:14.545 --> 02:24:17.281
Catching poisoners is rarely quick or easy.

02:24:18.016 --> 02:24:21.919
Despite the forensic detective's arsenal of sophisticated scientific techniques,

02:24:22.387 --> 02:24:27.025
often the most important element of a successful investigation is sheer doggedness.

02:24:29.360 --> 02:24:34.298
The FBI Evidence Response Team begins by meticulously examining a crime scene.

02:24:35.133 --> 02:24:36.734
Agents don special clothing,

02:24:37.301 --> 02:24:38.636
not to protect themselves,

02:24:38.903 --> 02:24:40.405
but to safeguard the evidence.

02:24:42.407 --> 02:24:44.442
With today's ultra-sensitive techniques,

02:24:44.709 --> 02:24:46.711
a single drop of blood or saliva,

02:24:47.145 --> 02:24:51.883
a strand of hair or scraping of skin may seal the conviction of a criminal.

02:24:54.986 --> 02:24:56.654
Careful record keeping is vital.

02:24:57.455 --> 02:24:58.823
Where each item was found,

02:24:59.257 --> 02:24:59.524
when,

02:24:59.691 --> 02:25:03.761
and by whom must be documented if the evidence is to stand up in court.

02:25:06.664 --> 02:25:11.035
FBI headquarters in Washington receives so many items of evidence for testing each day,

02:25:11.369 --> 02:25:13.071
it has been given its own zip code.

02:25:16.574 --> 02:25:19.510
Fingerprints still remain among the most important clues.

02:25:19.911 --> 02:25:22.780
The FBI has more than 200 million on file.

02:25:24.849 --> 02:25:28.186
There are dozens of ways to develop otherwise invisible prints,

02:25:28.586 --> 02:25:29.387
latent prints.

02:25:30.221 --> 02:25:33.024
One of the most effective is with the vapor of superglue,

02:25:33.424 --> 02:25:36.194
which adheres to the print and raises it to the surface.

02:25:39.063 --> 02:25:40.631
In the Sudafed poisoning case,

02:25:40.998 --> 02:25:46.370
Agent Turgison and his colleagues fingerprinted every piece of evidence that might implicate Joe Meeling.

02:25:47.572 --> 02:25:50.374
Six contaminated bottles were eventually retrieved,

02:25:50.675 --> 02:25:52.944
but Joe's fingerprints could not be found.

02:25:54.979 --> 02:25:56.981
Dozens of library books were examined,

02:25:57.248 --> 02:25:58.616
again without success.

02:25:59.450 --> 02:26:02.854
Perhaps Joe remembered that fingerprints helped catch Stella Nickel,

02:26:03.254 --> 02:26:04.989
and so he wore rubber gloves.

02:26:07.258 --> 02:26:11.062
FBI agents mapped the locations where the tampered packs were found.

02:26:12.063 --> 02:26:15.733
Joe told the FBI he bought Sudafed from a store near his house.

02:26:17.502 --> 02:26:21.372
Other tampered packages turned up in a store in the Lacey area near Joe's work.

02:26:22.273 --> 02:26:25.343
But four stores in Tacoma also had tampered product.

02:26:26.444 --> 02:26:28.045
Special Agent Tom Turgison.

02:26:29.113 --> 02:26:33.951
The red dots indicate places where we could place Joe Meiling through physical evidence,

02:26:33.951 --> 02:26:34.652
through receipts,

02:26:34.719 --> 02:26:35.486
through witnesses,

02:26:35.920 --> 02:26:36.587
and other methods,

02:26:37.054 --> 02:26:39.991
place him in one store,

02:26:40.024 --> 02:26:40.591
for instance,

02:26:40.725 --> 02:26:47.665
and in other areas around the time frame of the tamperings and in the immediate surroundings of those stores.

02:26:49.333 --> 02:26:52.970
Joe's link to the two stores near his home and work were evident enough.

02:26:53.504 --> 02:26:55.139
But what of the four stores in Tacoma?

02:26:58.075 --> 02:26:58.810
We found out that

02:26:59.177 --> 02:27:16.260
Joel would frequent the stripper bars and talk to the strippers and seemingly just relate his problems to

02:27:16.360 --> 02:27:16.494
them.

02:27:18.462 --> 02:27:20.631
several of the dancers from

02:27:21.332 --> 02:27:23.935
Topless Dance Clubs who recognized

02:27:24.402 --> 02:27:24.969
Joe Mealing,

02:27:25.069 --> 02:27:27.371
who testified against him at the trial,

02:27:27.705 --> 02:27:31.475
who placed him up here at certain dates and times.

02:27:32.476 --> 02:27:40.751
Some of them said he was a regular frequenter of the establishments and they easily recognized him.

02:27:44.455 --> 02:27:48.259
The investigators had tied Joe to the tampered Sudafed packages.

02:27:48.860 --> 02:27:50.928
Could they now tie him to the cyanide?

02:27:53.564 --> 02:27:54.632
As the major suspect,

02:27:54.932 --> 02:27:58.603
the FBI tapped Joe's telephone early on in the investigation.

02:27:59.570 --> 02:27:59.937
One day,

02:28:00.238 --> 02:28:03.674
they heard him describe in vivid detail a building he had once visited.

02:28:04.876 --> 02:28:05.743
From the description,

02:28:06.177 --> 02:28:10.181
FBI agents identified it as the Emerald City Chemical Company in Kent,

02:28:10.314 --> 02:28:10.815
Washington.

02:28:11.682 --> 02:28:12.516
Among other things,

02:28:12.884 --> 02:28:15.319
Emerald City Chemicals sells cyanide.

02:28:16.921 --> 02:28:21.058
When investigators examined the poison register for the day Joe had visited the area,

02:28:21.392 --> 02:28:22.860
they could not find his signature.

02:28:23.995 --> 02:28:24.662
A Richard P.

02:28:24.896 --> 02:28:27.265
Johnson had bought cyanide that day.

02:28:28.065 --> 02:28:30.101
It was all the FBI required.

02:28:31.435 --> 02:28:32.803
As in the Stella Nichol case,

02:28:33.204 --> 02:28:35.273
handwriting analysis proved decisive.

02:28:36.474 --> 02:28:39.277
Despite Joe Mealing's attempt to disguise his handwriting,

02:28:39.677 --> 02:28:42.046
there are clear similarities between Richard P.

02:28:42.179 --> 02:28:44.115
Johnson's signature and Joe's own.

02:28:46.117 --> 02:28:47.418
Consider the formation of the J,

02:28:47.718 --> 02:28:48.319
for example,

02:28:48.753 --> 02:28:49.553
the shape of the O,

02:28:51.455 --> 02:28:53.391
and the way the letters sit upon the line.

02:28:54.392 --> 02:28:57.561
The person who signed the poison register was Joe.

02:28:59.363 --> 02:29:05.670
It was determined that Joe Mealing purchased one pound of sodium cyanide on January 11.

02:29:05.670 --> 02:29:09.240
A pound similar to this container,

02:29:09.273 --> 02:29:12.276
which is a representative container of sodium cyanide,

02:29:12.476 --> 02:29:13.577
and he might have one pound.

02:29:13.978 --> 02:29:14.946
That's a lot of poison,

02:29:15.179 --> 02:29:21.185
considering a pill the size of a Sudafed capsule killed two people and injured one other.

02:29:23.554 --> 02:29:25.823
One last point puzzled the investigators.

02:29:26.390 --> 02:29:29.560
When the clerk at the chemical store was shown a photograph of Joe Mealing,

02:29:29.860 --> 02:29:31.162
he failed to recognize him.

02:29:33.698 --> 02:29:35.099
In the residence of Joe Mealing,

02:29:35.299 --> 02:29:38.169
we found this array of theatrical makeup equipment.

02:29:39.136 --> 02:29:48.412
Experts in theatrical makeup testified at his trial and determined on the witness stand that there is the equipment here available to make someone up,

02:29:48.746 --> 02:29:51.849
to be unrecognizable as to their true identity.

02:29:52.950 --> 02:29:56.354
We found a photograph of a person who looks elderly,

02:29:56.420 --> 02:29:56.921
graying hair,

02:29:56.988 --> 02:29:57.655
wrinkled face,

02:29:58.122 --> 02:30:00.825
and it's a friend of Joe Mealing in his late 20s,

02:30:00.825 --> 02:30:01.525
early 30s.

02:30:04.829 --> 02:30:07.898
It had taken 18 months of exhaustive investigation,

02:30:08.332 --> 02:30:10.134
but by August 1992,

02:30:10.501 --> 02:30:13.437
the FBI case against Joe Mealing was complete.

02:30:14.538 --> 02:30:16.240
Shortly before Jennifer's poisoning,

02:30:16.407 --> 02:30:18.476
he prepared six packages of Sudafed.

02:30:20.111 --> 02:30:20.478
In each,

02:30:20.644 --> 02:30:24.882
he replaced the Sudafed with capsules filled with lethal doses of cyanide.

02:30:26.217 --> 02:30:28.686
Five packets he returned to drugstore shelves.

02:30:29.420 --> 02:30:32.156
One remained at home to kill Jennifer.

02:30:33.524 --> 02:30:38.195
Joe Mealing was indicted on two counts of murder and the attempted murder of Jennifer Mealing.

02:30:39.630 --> 02:30:41.599
He is now serving life imprisonment.

02:30:44.802 --> 02:30:46.804
Among evidence discovered at Joe's home,

02:30:47.171 --> 02:30:49.273
agents found one last item of note,

02:30:49.874 --> 02:30:53.077
an outline for a screenplay about the poisoning of Jennifer.

02:30:55.880 --> 02:30:56.714
The protagonist,

02:30:57.048 --> 02:30:57.681
James Martin,

02:30:57.982 --> 02:30:59.650
is described as devoted...

02:31:00.851 --> 02:31:01.185
Honest,

02:31:01.652 --> 02:31:02.253
reliable,

02:31:03.087 --> 02:31:03.687
sincere,

02:31:04.388 --> 02:31:04.889
truthful.

02:31:09.160 --> 02:31:10.928
Joe Mealing was greedy.

02:31:11.729 --> 02:31:13.030
He was very selfish.

02:31:13.998 --> 02:31:17.902
I don't think he was concerned about the health and welfare of his wife,

02:31:17.935 --> 02:31:18.402
Jennifer.

02:31:20.171 --> 02:31:21.305
He's concerned about Joe.

02:31:22.840 --> 02:31:29.313
Just as Stella Nichol learned from the Tylenol poisonings and Joe Meeling learned from Stella Nichol's mistakes,

02:31:29.880 --> 02:31:34.385
so too investigators learn to prevent and detect such murders.

02:31:35.486 --> 02:31:35.820
Dr.

02:31:35.886 --> 02:31:36.587
Barry Logan.

02:31:37.421 --> 02:31:40.991
There's no way to prevent a product from being tampered with.

02:31:41.158 --> 02:31:45.696
What we can do is package it in such a way that if anything is done to interfere with the material,

02:31:46.230 --> 02:31:51.368
that it's evident to the person when they buy it from the supermarket or before they use the product.

02:31:53.571 --> 02:31:58.642
What's important for the conviction of the poisoner are four things I call the conviction pyramid.

02:31:58.709 --> 02:31:59.176
Number one,

02:31:59.176 --> 02:32:00.344
we have to have the victim.

02:32:01.278 --> 02:32:01.745
Number two,

02:32:01.779 --> 02:32:03.147
we have to have the poisoner.

02:32:03.981 --> 02:32:06.217
And then we have to have the poison and the intent.

02:32:06.283 --> 02:32:08.452
And all these things have to be linked together.

02:32:09.787 --> 02:32:11.222
Poison to victim.

02:32:11.889 --> 02:32:13.090
Poison to offender.

02:32:14.191 --> 02:32:15.493
Offender to victim.

02:32:16.827 --> 02:32:18.662
And then finally the intent.

02:32:19.230 --> 02:32:21.499
Was the intent really to kill that victim.

02:32:22.967 --> 02:32:25.503
Once you get those four things locked on,

02:32:25.736 --> 02:32:27.571
then you have the potential for a conviction.

02:32:29.540 --> 02:32:30.040
Each year,

02:32:30.341 --> 02:32:34.044
forensic science provides investigators with powerful new tools.

02:32:34.812 --> 02:32:37.248
How many poisoners still elude detection?

02:32:38.115 --> 02:32:38.782
We don't know.

02:32:39.416 --> 02:32:41.285
Perhaps more than we would like to admit.

02:32:44.588 --> 02:32:45.923
Who is winning the battle?

02:32:46.891 --> 02:32:47.424
In the end,

02:32:48.025 --> 02:32:48.425
no one.

02:32:50.828 --> 02:32:52.062
The fight goes on.

